window.SF_CHAPTERS=[{"number":1,"title":"Chapter One — The Back Court","body":"Florida heat did not care what time it was.\n\nAt six-thirty in the evening, the sun still hung over Sunridge Gardens like a bright orange warning. Heat shimmered above the cracked pavement. Window units rattled from the apartment buildings. Somewhere near Sunridge Plaza, bass from a passing car shook loose glass in an abandoned bus shelter.\n\nBehind Building 17, Kareem Dawson stood at the center of the Back Court with the basketball tucked beneath one arm and the whole neighborhood watching him argue.\n\nNobody called him Kareem out there.\n\nOn the court, he was Reck.\n\nHis red designer shirt burned against the faded orange concrete. Gold chains rested over his chest, catching sunlight every time he moved. A thin scar split his right eyebrow. His twisted fade was fresh, his black jeans were stacked perfectly over spotless red-and-white sneakers, and his voice carried from one side of the apartments to the other.\n\n“Ain’t no next point,” he said. “That was game.”\n\nThe older boy across from him laughed. His name was Curtis, but everybody called him C-Mack. He was nineteen, nearly six feet tall, and surrounded by three friends who spent most afternoons leaning against the fence as if the Back Court belonged to them.\n\n“Ball hit the fence,” C-Mack said. “Out of bounds.”\n\n“After it went through the rim.”\n\n“Still out.”\n\nReck bounced the ball once. “You losing got you rewriting basketball now?”\n\nLaughter rolled across the bleachers.\n\nThat was the part C-Mack could not forgive.\n\nReck never disagreed quietly. He turned every argument into a performance and every audience into his audience.\n\nJamari Collins jumped down from the lowest bleacher. Mari was shorter than everyone else, but the white tank top stretched across his muscular shoulders made him look built for collisions. Sweat darkened the orange jersey hanging open over his chest. When he smiled, dimples appeared beside a gold bottom grill.\n\n“He changing rules like he the commissioner,” Mari said. “Next he gon’ tell us the hoop too high.”\n\nMore laughter.\n\n“Mari,” Isaiah Brooks warned.\n\nZay stood near the free-throw line, taller than everybody on the court. His royal-blue basketball shorts reached his knees, and long two-strand twists brushed his shoulders. He had the serious face of somebody who was always calculating how much trouble his friends could create in the next thirty seconds.\n\nUsually, the answer was too much.\n\nTerrence Jackson held a phone from the bleachers, recording everything. TJ’s full curly Afro moved in the hot breeze while his purple graphic shirt hung loosely from his narrow shoulders.\n\n“I got the whole game,” TJ announced. “Reck scored. Clear as day.”\n\nC-Mack pointed at him. “Put that phone down, little boy.”\n\nTJ’s smile disappeared.\n\nReck stepped forward before TJ could respond.\n\n“Talk to me,” he said. “Not him.”\n\nNasir Grant remained beside the fence. Naz wore a charcoal hoodie despite the heat, sleeves pushed to his elbows. Neat cornrows ran toward the back of his neck, and dark-framed glasses rested over deep-set eyes. He had been silent throughout the argument, studying C-Mack’s friends and the people gathering on the apartment walkways.\n\nThen he noticed the black Chevrolet idling near the entrance to the court.\n\n“Reck,” Naz said.\n\nIt was only one word, but Reck heard the warning inside it.\n\nHe glanced toward the car.\n\nRamon Ellis sat behind the wheel.\n\nMon was King K’s most feared man, and he did not visit the Back Court to watch basketball. He was tall even while seated, with reddish-brown skin and a scar running along his chin. His black shirt matched the dark interior of the car. One hand rested on the steering wheel while his eyes remained fixed on Reck.\n\nThe laughter faded.\n\nC-Mack saw Mon too. Confidence returned to his face.\n\n“You do a lot of talking when your daddy can’t teach you better,” he said.\n\nThe court went still.\n\nReck’s fingers tightened around the basketball.\n\nEverybody in Sunridge knew about Kareem Dawson Sr. Reem had once carried weight throughout The Ridge. Now he existed in old stories, prison photographs and collect calls Shanice Dawson sometimes refused to answer.\n\nZay moved closer. “Leave it alone, Reck.”\n\nMari’s grin disappeared.\n\nTJ lowered the phone.\n\nNaz pushed himself away from the fence.\n\nReck looked at C-Mack, then toward the apartment windows. Mothers stood behind blinds. Children leaned over the upstairs railings. Men outside the corner store had turned their heads toward the court.\n\nWalking away would have been smart.\n\nReck hated looking smart when it felt anything like looking scared.\n\nHe tossed the basketball behind him.\n\n“My father ain’t got nothing to do with you losing,” he said.\n\nC-Mack stepped close enough for their shoes to nearly touch. “Your father lost too.”\n\nReck shoved him.\n\nC-Mack stumbled backward, caught himself and charged.\n\nThe Back Court erupted.\n\nThey crashed against the chain-link fence as people scattered from the sideline. C-Mack swung hard. Reck ducked the first punch but caught the second across his cheek. Mari rushed toward them, only for Zay to grab him around the chest.\n\n“Let Reck handle it!” Zay shouted.\n\n“Get off me!”\n\nTJ climbed onto the bleacher seat for a better view, caught between recording and fear. Naz did neither. He watched C-Mack’s three friends, waiting to see if the fight would remain between two people.\n\nIt did not.\n\nOne of them stepped forward.\n\nNaz blocked his path. “Don’t.”\n\nThe boy looked at Naz’s glasses and quiet expression, mistaking calmness for weakness.\n\n“Move.”\n\nNaz did not.\n\nAt the fence, Reck drove his shoulder into C-Mack and forced him backward. His gold chains jumped against his chest. C-Mack was older and heavier, but Reck fought like the entire neighborhood would remember whichever one of them took a step backward first.\n\nReck refused to let it be him.\n\nA car door slammed.\n\n“Enough.”\n\nMon’s voice cut through the noise.\n\nNobody listened immediately—not even C-Mack.\n\nMon crossed the court, grabbed him by the back of his shirt and pulled him away from Reck with one arm. Reck stepped forward again, breathing hard, a small streak of blood at the corner of his mouth.\n\nMon placed himself between them.\n\n“I said enough.”\n\nC-Mack looked down. “He put his hands on me.”\n\n“And you let him.”\n\nThat answer wounded C-Mack worse than the fight.\n\nMon turned toward Reck. The court became silent enough to hear the broken streetlight buzzing above the fence.\n\n“You think being loud makes you dangerous?” Mon asked.\n\nReck wiped his mouth with the back of his hand. He looked at the blood, then smiled.\n\n“You heard me, didn’t you?”\n\nMari whispered, “Man, Reck crazy.”\n\nZay closed his eyes for half a second.\n\nTJ lifted the phone again.\n\nNaz watched Mon.\n\nMon stepped closer, studying the sixteen-year-old who had just challenged him in front of half the complex. Reck’s chest rose and fell, but he did not move. Fear lived somewhere inside him—Naz knew that—but pride stood in front of it like a locked door.\n\n“King K wants to see you,” Mon said.\n\nThat changed the air.\n\nEven Reck’s smile weakened.\n\nKing K did not call teenagers to his car wash for conversation. He called people when he had business, questions or problems.\n\n“About what?” Reck asked.\n\n“If he wanted you to know out here, I would’ve told you out here.”\n\nMon turned and walked back toward the Chevrolet.\n\nC-Mack and his friends followed, leaving the court without another word. He looked over his shoulder once, humiliation burning across his face.\n\nReck had won the crowd.\n\nHe might have made an enemy too.\n\nThe apartment walkways came alive with whispers. Somebody replayed TJ’s video. Younger boys repeated Reck’s answer to Mon, each version louder than the last.\n\nYou heard me, didn’t you?\n\nWithin an hour, the words would cross Sunridge Plaza, pass through Garden Courts and reach The Palms. By midnight, people who had never attended the game would swear they had stood beside Reck when he said them.\n\nThat was how reputations grew in The Ridge.\n\nNot from truth.\n\nFrom witnesses who wanted better stories.\n\nMari slapped Reck’s shoulder. “You just talked crazy to Mon in front of everybody.”\n\n“I talked regular.”\n\n“That ain’t regular for nobody with sense.”\n\nZay picked up the abandoned basketball. “King K calling you isn’t something to celebrate.”\n\n“Who celebrating?” Reck asked, still smiling.\n\n“Your face.”\n\nTJ hurried down from the bleachers. “The video already got three hundred views.”\n\nReck’s smile vanished. “Take it down.”\n\nTJ stared at him. “What? Why?”\n\n“Because I said take it down.”\n\n“But everybody posting it.”\n\nNaz finally walked over. “Then everybody is showing King K exactly who was here, what happened and what Mon said.”\n\nTJ looked at his screen, understanding too late. He deleted the post.\n\n“You still treating me like I’m little,” he muttered.\n\nReck reached over and squeezed the back of TJ’s neck. “Then stop making little mistakes.”\n\nTJ pulled away, embarrassed.\n\nZay tossed the ball toward Reck. “Don’t go see King K by yourself.”\n\nReck caught it against his chest.\n\n“He didn’t call all of us.”\n\n“That’s the point.”\n\nFor the first time all evening, Reck looked uncertain. Across the parking lot, The Sunshine’s yellow paint glowed beneath the sunset. His cousin D-Low stood on its porch in a vintage Miami jersey and gold-framed glasses, watching from a distance.\n\nD-Low raised his chin.\n\nCome here.\n\nReck handed the ball to Zay.\n\n“I’m going to see what my cousin want.”\n\nMari stepped beside him. “We coming.”\n\n“Nah.”\n\n“What happened to together?”\n\nReck looked at all four of them: Mari’s restless energy, Zay’s concern, TJ’s wounded pride and Naz’s unreadable stare.\n\nThey had spent their childhood on that court. Back then, the biggest danger had been broken glass beneath the bleachers or Shanice calling Reck upstairs before the streetlights came on.\n\nThey were not children anymore.\n\nThe Ridge had started noticing them.\n\nAnd somebody powerful had started noticing Reck.\n\n“Stay here,” he said. “If I need y’all, I’ll call.”\n\nHe crossed the parking lot alone, red shirt bright beneath the dying sun. Residents watched from porches and windows. Reck walked slowly, shoulders loose, as if King K’s invitation had not placed a knot inside his stomach.\n\nD-Low opened the door to The Sunshine.\n\nCold air spilled from the darkness behind him.\n\n“You been busy, little cousin,” he said.\n\nReck stopped at the bottom of the steps. “Everybody keep saying that.”\n\nD-Low studied the blood at the corner of his mouth.\n\n“King K heard about you.”\n\n“Good.”\n\n“You sure about that?”\n\nReck looked back toward the Back Court. His friends stood beneath the flickering light, each one watching him.\n\nThen he faced the open doorway.\n\nMost people in Sunridge spent their lives trying not to attract the attention of men like King K.\n\nReck wanted the entire neighborhood to know his name.\n\nHe climbed the steps.\n\n“Yeah,” he said. “I’m sure.”\n\nD-Low moved aside.\n\nReck entered The Sunshine, and the door closed behind him.\n\n---"},{"number":2,"title":"Chapter Two — Inside The Sunshine","body":"The first thing Reck noticed was the cold.\n\nOutside, Sunridge Gardens felt like an oven. Inside The Sunshine, two stolen window units pushed freezing air through rooms with boarded windows. The abandoned yellow duplex had looked dead from the street for years, but behind its reinforced door, it never slept.\n\nMusic played from a speaker on the kitchen counter. Men moved through the narrow hallway. Somebody counted money at a folding table beneath a bare bulb. A security monitor showed four grainy camera angles—the front steps, the parking lot, the canal path and the entrance to Garden Courts.\n\nThe place smelled like cigar smoke, sweat and old carpet.\n\nReck had been inside once as a child, when the duplex still belonged to Mrs. Green and plastic covered her furniture. She used to hand out frozen cups from the porch.\n\nNow the family photographs were gone. Metal covered the back door. The living room had become a waiting area for people who never stayed long.\n\nD-Low locked the entrance behind them.\n\n“Keep your eyes forward,” he said.\n\nReck glanced at the money table again. “I know how to act.”\n\n“Knowing and doing ain’t never been the same thing with you.”\n\nD-Low led him down the hallway. His freeform locs brushed the shoulders of a faded Miami jersey. Gold-framed glasses made him look relaxed, but he checked every room they passed.\n\nAt the end of the hall, Mon stood beside a closed bedroom door.\n\nHe looked at Reck’s bleeding lip. “You proud of that?”\n\n“You keep asking me questions like you care.”\n\nMon moved away from the wall.\n\nD-Low stepped between them. “Not tonight.”\n\nMon stared over D-Low’s shoulder. “That mouth gon’ put him somewhere his legs can’t carry him out of.”\n\nReck smiled. “But not tonight, right?”\n\nD-Low pushed open the bedroom door before Mon could answer.\n\nKendrick Wallace sat behind a glass desk wearing a cream polo shirt, dark tailored pants and brown loafers. King K was heavyset, bald and perfectly groomed. A gold watch rested against his wrist. He looked more like the owner of an expensive restaurant than the man whose name controlled half of western Palm Haven.\n\nBree Lawson stood beside the desk with a tablet in one hand. Her platinum-blonde pixie cut, fitted black blazer and sharp cheekbones made her look completely out of place inside the ruined duplex.\n\nThat was probably intentional.\n\nKing K looked at Reck for several seconds.\n\n“Kareem Dawson.”\n\n“People call me Reck.”\n\n“I know what people call you.” King K leaned back. “I knew your father before people called him Reem.”\n\nReck’s jaw tightened.\n\n“Everybody know my father.”\n\n“Everybody knows a story about him. That’s different.”\n\nBree watched Reck without expression. Mon remained in the doorway. D-Low took a position near the wall.\n\nReck suddenly understood that this was not a conversation. It was an examination.\n\nKing K folded his hands. “Why did you fight Curtis?”\n\n“He lost and started talking crazy.”\n\n“People talk crazy every day.”\n\n“Not to me.”\n\nKing K smiled slightly. “That what makes you special?”\n\n“I ain’t say I was special.”\n\n“You didn’t have to. You wear it.”\n\nReck looked down at his red shirt and chains. “I like looking good.”\n\n“You like being seen.” Bree spoke for the first time. Her voice was cool and precise. “There’s a difference.”\n\nReck looked at her. “Who are you?”\n\nD-Low exhaled softly.\n\nMon shook his head.\n\nBree did not react. “Someone you should know before you enter rooms asking questions.”\n\nKing K laughed once. “That fearlessness might be real. Or he might not understand danger yet.”\n\n“Same result if nobody corrects it,” Mon said.\n\nReck looked toward the doorway. “I’m standing right here.”\n\n“That’s what we’re discussing,” King K replied. “Where you stand.”\n\nThe room became quiet.\n\nKing K reached into a desk drawer and removed a thick white envelope. He set it on the glass between them.\n\n“Your mother behind on rent?”\n\nReck’s face changed before he could stop it.\n\n“Who told you that?”\n\n“The Ridge talks.”\n\n“My family ain’t neighborhood business.”\n\n“Everything becomes neighborhood business when eviction papers touch the door.”\n\nReck stepped toward the desk. D-Low straightened. Mon unfolded his arms.\n\nKing K did not move.\n\n“Be careful,” he said quietly. “Your pride is about to disrespect somebody trying to help your mother.”\n\nReck stared at the envelope.\n\nIt was thick enough to solve a problem Shanice had been hiding behind closed bedroom doors and late-night phone calls. The lights had already been disconnected once that summer. Kobe believed it was a neighborhood outage. Reck had let him believe it.\n\n“What you want?” Reck asked.\n\n“Smartest thing you’ve said since you walked in.”\n\nKing K tapped the envelope.\n\n“D-Low needs somebody to make a delivery across town.”\n\n“I can do that.”\n\n“You don’t know what it is.”\n\n“Don’t matter.”\n\nBree’s eyes narrowed. “It should.”\n\nKing K pushed the envelope across the desk. “Half now. Half when you return.”\n\nReck picked it up. It felt heavier than money should have felt.\n\n“Where am I going?”\n\nD-Low answered from behind him. “Paradise Beach.”\n\nReck turned.\n\nParadise Beach was not part of their world. Tourists filled its hotels, valet drivers parked cars worth more than Building 17, and police arrived before arguments had time to become fights.\n\nIt was also territory claimed by men who did not recognize King K’s control.\n\n“Why me?” Reck asked.\n\nKing K smiled again. “Because nobody looking for you yet.”\n\nYet.\n\nThe word stayed with Reck.\n\n“You want respect?” King K continued. “Learn what it costs before you start spending it.”\n\nReck slipped the envelope into his pocket.\n\n“When?”\n\n“Now.”\n\n---\n\nOutside, night had settled over Sunridge.\n\nThe broken streetlight behind Building 17 flickered above the Back Court. Mari was dribbling between his legs. Zay sat on the bleachers, long arms resting across his knees. TJ stared at his phone. Naz stood where Reck had left him, charcoal hoodie almost disappearing into the darkness.\n\nAll four looked toward The Sunshine when the door opened.\n\nReck walked across the parking lot with D-Low beside him.\n\nA silver sedan waited near the curb. It belonged to one of D-Low’s girlfriends, which meant police would not immediately connect it to The Sunshine.\n\n“What King K want?” Mari asked.\n\n“Business,” Reck said.\n\nZay stood. “What kind?”\n\n“The kind that don’t concern you.”\n\nThat answer irritated all of them.\n\nMari stopped dribbling. “We been out here waiting for you.”\n\n“I ain’t ask you to.”\n\nTJ looked up from his phone. “You said if you needed us, you’d call.”\n\n“I don’t need you.”\n\nThe words landed harder than Reck intended.\n\nNaz studied the silver car, then D-Low. “Where are you taking him?”\n\nD-Low smiled. “He always let you question grown men?”\n\n“Only when grown men avoid simple answers.”\n\nReck stepped between them. “Chill, Naz.”\n\nZay came down from the bleachers. At six-foot-four, he stood eye to eye with D-Low.\n\n“If something happens to him—”\n\n“What?” D-Low interrupted. “You gon’ throw a basketball at me?”\n\nMari moved beside Zay.\n\nTJ rose too.\n\nFor one brief second, the Ridge Boyz stood together beneath the dying streetlight. They had no rank, no money and no real power. But D-Low saw something in the way they closed around Reck without being called.\n\nSo did Reck.\n\n“I’m good,” he told them. “Go home.”\n\nNaz looked directly at him. “That envelope worth lying to your brothers?”\n\nReck touched his pocket instinctively.\n\nNaz noticed.\n\nOf course he did.\n\n“I said I’m good.” Reck opened the passenger door. “Stop acting scared for me.”\n\nZay’s serious expression hardened. “We ain’t scared for you. We scared of what you become when people start paying you to be reckless.”\n\nReck climbed into the car without answering.\n\nD-Low drove away from the Back Court, leaving the four boys beneath the flickering light.\n\n---\n\nParadise Boulevard separated two versions of Florida.\n\nOn the west side stood Sunridge Gardens: stained apartment walls, dark windows, corner stores protected by scratched security glass and patrol cars moving slowly enough to study everybody outside.\n\nOn the east side, palm trees were wrapped in white lights. Hotel towers reflected moonlight from the ocean. Tourists walked between restaurants carrying shopping bags, unaware that ten minutes away, families were deciding which bill could remain unpaid.\n\nD-Low drove with one hand on the wheel.\n\nA black duffel bag rested at Reck’s feet.\n\n“You don’t open it,” D-Low said.\n\n“Wasn’t planning to.”\n\n“You don’t ask the man receiving it what’s inside.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“And if anything feel wrong, you call me before you make one of your Reck decisions.”\n\nReck looked through the passenger window. “Why King K pick me?”\n\n“You wanted him to notice you.”\n\n“That ain’t an answer.”\n\nD-Low glanced at him. “People like King K don’t give opportunities. They purchase pieces of you. Tonight he bought the part worried about your mama’s rent.”\n\nReck touched the envelope again.\n\n“Then I got something I needed too.”\n\n“That’s how everybody explains the first time.”\n\nBlue lights appeared in the rearview mirror.\n\nD-Low’s face changed.\n\nThe police cruiser was two cars behind them, moving closer through traffic.\n\nReck looked down at the duffel bag.\n\n“Is that for us?”\n\n“Don’t turn around.”\n\nThe cruiser changed lanes.\n\nReck’s heartbeat climbed. Every instinct told him to look back, open the bag or demand answers. Instead, he stared through the windshield as Paradise Beach glowed ahead of them.\n\nThe blue lights grew brighter across the mirrors.\n\nThen the siren sounded.\n\nD-Low tightened both hands around the steering wheel.\n\n“Listen carefully,” he said. “Whatever happens next, don’t panic.”\n\nReck looked at his cousin.\n\nThen he looked at the black bag between his feet.\n\nFor once, Kareem “Reck” Dawson had nothing loud to say.\n\n---"},{"number":3,"title":"Chapter Three — Blue Lights","body":"The siren screamed behind them.\n\nBlue-and-red light rolled across the silver sedan, bounced off hotel windows and flashed over the black duffel bag between Reck’s shoes.\n\nD-Low slowed the car.\n\n“What you doing?” Reck asked.\n\n“What it look like?”\n\n“You pulling over?”\n\n“This ain’t the movies.”\n\nD-Low signaled and guided the sedan toward the curb outside a brightly lit seafood restaurant. Tourists waited beneath a blue awning while valets rushed between luxury cars. Several people turned to watch the police stop unfolding beside them.\n\nReck pushed the duffel farther beneath his seat.\n\n“Leave it,” D-Low said.\n\n“It’s sticking out.”\n\n“Moving around makes it worse.”\n\nD-Low lowered both front windows and placed his hands on the steering wheel. His gold-framed glasses reflected the patrol lights. Reck tried to copy his calm posture, but his right knee would not stop moving.\n\nIn the side mirror, two officers stepped from the cruiser.\n\nOne approached D-Low. The other moved toward Reck’s side with a flashlight resting above his shoulder.\n\n“License and registration,” the first officer said.\n\nHe was white, middle-aged and thick through the waist. His nameplate read **MILLER**.\n\nD-Low kept his hands visible. “My license is in my back pocket.”\n\n“Get it slowly.”\n\nD-Low reached behind him.\n\nThe officer on Reck’s side shined his flashlight directly into his face. She was younger, Latina, with dark hair pulled into a tight bun. Her nameplate read **SANTIAGO**.\n\n“How old are you?” she asked.\n\n“Sixteen.”\n\n“You have identification?”\n\n“Not on me.”\n\n“What’s your name?”\n\nReck hesitated.\n\n“Kareem Dawson.”\n\nOfficer Santiago studied him. “Where are you two headed?”\n\n“To see somebody.”\n\nD-Low glanced across the car.\n\n“Where?” she asked.\n\n“Paradise Beach.”\n\n“You’re already in Paradise Beach.”\n\n“Then I guess we almost there.”\n\nSantiago lowered the flashlight slightly. “Is something funny?”\n\n“No, ma’am,” D-Low answered for him.\n\nOfficer Miller took the license back to the cruiser. Santiago remained beside the passenger window, watching Reck breathe.\n\nThe duffel bag pressed against his ankle.\n\nAcross the street, music drifted from a rooftop lounge. Couples laughed beneath strings of white lights. A horse-drawn tourist carriage rolled through the intersection as if two different cities were occupying the same block.\n\nSantiago aimed the flashlight toward Reck’s lap.\n\n“What happened to your mouth?”\n\n“Basketball.”\n\n“Basketball hit back now?”\n\nReck looked away.\n\nThe officer moved the light across the floorboard.\n\nIt stopped.\n\n“What’s in the bag?”\n\nD-Low answered. “Gym clothes.”\n\n“I asked him.”\n\nReck could feel D-Low’s warning without looking at him.\n\n“Gym clothes,” he repeated.\n\n“Whose bag is it?”\n\n“Mine,” D-Low said.\n\n“His,” Reck said at the same time.\n\nSilence filled the sedan.\n\nSantiago took one step back from the window.\n\n“Driver, turn the vehicle off.”\n\nD-Low’s jaw tightened. “For what?”\n\n“Turn it off.”\n\nOfficer Miller left the cruiser and walked back toward them. One hand rested near his holster.\n\nReck looked at D-Low.\n\nD-Low looked straight ahead.\n\n“Don’t,” he whispered.\n\nReck did not know whether his cousin was speaking to him, himself or both of them.\n\nMiller reached the driver’s door. “Step out of the vehicle.”\n\n“Officer, you stopped me over a taillight.”\n\n“I’m not going to ask again.”\n\nD-Low turned the key.\n\nFor half a second, the engine went silent.\n\nThen his hand snapped back to the ignition.\n\nThe sedan roared.\n\n“Hold on!”\n\nD-Low slammed the accelerator.\n\nThe rear tires jumped from the curb. Officer Miller struck the side of the car with his palm as it shot into traffic. Santiago leaped backward, shouting into her radio.\n\nReck crashed against the passenger seat.\n\nThe duffel slid from beneath him and hit the dashboard.\n\n“You said this ain’t the movies!” Reck shouted.\n\n“Shut up and watch the bag!”\n\nThey tore through a red light.\n\nHorns exploded around them. A convertible swerved toward the sidewalk. D-Low cut between two SUVs and pushed the sedan down Paradise Boulevard as the police cruiser surged after them.\n\nReck grabbed the duffel.\n\nThe zipper had split open.\n\nInside were several tightly wrapped packages and a handgun sealed in a plastic bag.\n\nHis stomach dropped.\n\n“D-Low!”\n\n“Zip it!”\n\n“King K sent me with this?”\n\n“Zip the bag!”\n\nThe cruiser struck the rear bumper.\n\nReck lurched forward. The handgun slid across the floorboard and disappeared beneath D-Low’s seat.\n\n“It came out!”\n\nD-Low looked down for a fraction of a second.\n\nThe sedan clipped a parked delivery van.\n\nMetal screamed. The passenger mirror shattered, spraying glass through Reck’s open window. He covered his face as D-Low fought the steering wheel and forced the car back into the lane.\n\nMore sirens joined the first.\n\nReck looked behind them. Two cruisers were approaching from the next intersection. Farther back, the beach traffic had become a wall of brake lights and frightened drivers.\n\n“Call Mon,” D-Low said.\n\n“You call him!”\n\n“I’m driving!”\n\nReck pulled out his phone. His hand shook badly enough to miss the contact twice.\n\nMon answered on the first ring.\n\n“What?”\n\n“Police behind us.”\n\n“Where’s D-Low?”\n\n“Driving!”\n\n“Put him on.”\n\n“He driving!”\n\nMon’s voice sharpened. “Where are you?”\n\nReck looked through the windshield. “Paradise and Ocean.”\n\n“Why did he run?”\n\nD-Low shouted, “Tell him the bag got seen!”\n\n“They saw the bag!”\n\nThere was a brief silence on the phone.\n\n“Do not bring that car back to Sunridge,” Mon said.\n\nThe call ended.\n\n“He hung up,” Reck said.\n\n“Of course he did.”\n\nD-Low jerked the wheel and turned west.\n\nThe glowing hotels fell behind them. Tourist shops gave way to tire stores, pawnshops and shuttered businesses. The streets narrowed. Rainwater from an afternoon storm remained in deep potholes, exploding beneath the sedan’s tires.\n\nThe police stayed close.\n\nA helicopter light appeared above the rooftops.\n\nReck stared through the rear window. “They got a chopper.”\n\n“I see it.”\n\n“Then where we going?”\n\nD-Low did not answer.\n\nThey crossed beneath the expressway. The sedan bottomed out on the railroad tracks, throwing Reck against the door. His phone flew from his hand and landed near the loose gun.\n\nThe engine made a grinding noise.\n\n“Car breaking down,” Reck said.\n\n“I know.”\n\n“So what now?”\n\nD-Low looked toward a row of dark warehouses beside the canal.\n\n“Now we run.”\n\nHe turned sharply into an industrial lot, struck a locked chain across the entrance and broke through it. The sedan bounced over gravel. The helicopter light swept across the roof.\n\nD-Low braked behind an abandoned warehouse.\n\n“Get out!”\n\nReck grabbed the duffel.\n\nD-Low slapped it from his hands.\n\n“Leave it!”\n\n“King K gon’—”\n\n“We get caught with that, King K ain’t gon’ matter!”\n\nD-Low reached beneath his seat, found the handgun and threw it back into the open bag. Then he pushed through the driver’s door.\n\nReck followed.\n\nThey sprinted toward the canal as cruisers entered the lot behind them. Officers shouted. Gravel scattered beneath Reck’s sneakers. The helicopter light found them, turning night into violent white daylight.\n\n“Stop running!” a voice boomed from above.\n\nD-Low reached the canal fence first and climbed.\n\nReck jumped, caught the top and pulled himself upward. Metal cut into his palms. His chains tangled against the links.\n\nA police dog barked somewhere behind them.\n\n“Move!” D-Low shouted from the other side.\n\nReck ripped one chain from his neck and dropped into the weeds. Together they ran along the black canal water toward a concrete drainage tunnel.\n\nThe helicopter circled.\n\nOfficers climbed the fence.\n\nD-Low reached the tunnel entrance and disappeared inside. Reck followed him into ankle-deep water and darkness.\n\nThe tunnel swallowed the sirens but multiplied every breath and footstep. Reck could not see more than a few feet ahead. Dirty water filled his expensive sneakers. Spiderwebs brushed his face. He kept one hand on the concrete wall and chased the sound of D-Low moving ahead.\n\n“Where this go?” he asked.\n\n“Just keep moving.”\n\nBehind them, light entered the tunnel.\n\nPolice were coming.\n\nThey reached a split in the drainage system. D-Low went left.\n\nReck grabbed his jersey. “That way leads back toward the road.”\n\n“How you know?”\n\n“Me and my boys ride bikes through these canals.”\n\nReck pointed right. “That comes out behind Cypress Bottom.”\n\nD-Low hesitated.\n\nFor the first time that night, the older man followed Reck.\n\nThey turned right.\n\nThe tunnel narrowed until both had to crouch. Reck’s legs burned. His lungs pulled at the humid air. He thought about Shanice cleaning hotel rooms on the other side of the city. He thought about Kobe’s drawings and Zay warning him at the Back Court.\n\nHe thought about the envelope still folded inside his pocket.\n\nHalf the money now.\n\nHalf when you return.\n\nThey reached a rusted grate overlooking a ditch behind Cypress Bottom. D-Low kicked it twice before it broke loose.\n\nBoth crawled through.\n\nNo helicopter light waited outside.\n\nFor the moment, they had disappeared.\n\nThey ran through the rear of the neighborhood, passing dark homes and barking dogs. D-Low slowed behind an abandoned daycare center and leaned against the wall, fighting for breath.\n\nReck bent forward with his hands on his knees.\n\nThen he started laughing.\n\nD-Low stared at him. “What’s funny?”\n\nReck tried to answer but laughed harder.\n\nThe fear had nowhere else to go.\n\n“We got away,” he finally said.\n\nD-Low shoved him against the wall.\n\n“The car is gone. The work is gone. The gun is gone. Your phone is inside it, and cameras caught our faces from Paradise Beach to the warehouse.”\n\nReck’s laughter stopped.\n\n“King K trusted you with one thing,” D-Low continued. “Now he lost everything.”\n\n“You was driving.”\n\n“And you think he gon’ care?”\n\nReck pushed D-Low’s hand from his chest. “You brought me.”\n\n“Because I thought you wanted to grow up.”\n\n“I handled myself.”\n\n“You survived ten minutes. That ain’t the same thing.”\n\nD-Low stepped away and looked toward the street.\n\n“We need a phone.”\n\nReck reached into his pocket and removed King K’s envelope. The edges were wet, but the money remained inside.\n\nD-Low stared at it.\n\n“At least we got this,” Reck said.\n\n“That ain’t yours anymore.”\n\nReck closed his fist around the envelope.\n\nIn the distance, sirens spread across Palm Haven. Police would trace the sedan. They would identify D-Low. They would find Reck’s phone, his fingerprints and the blood from his split lip.\n\nBack in Sunridge, the Ridge Boyz were probably watching the chase on social media without knowing their friend had been inside the car.\n\nReck had wanted the city to know his name.\n\nBy sunrise, it might.\n\nBut not for the reason he imagined.\n\n---"},{"number":4,"title":"Chapter Four — Evidence","body":"By two in the morning, the police had turned the warehouse lot into a field of flashing lights.\n\nThe silver sedan sat crooked behind yellow tape with its passenger mirror missing and its front bumper hanging toward the gravel. Officers photographed the doors, the floorboards and the blood smeared along the inside of the passenger window.\n\nDetective Renee Holloway stood beneath the helicopter wind with blue gloves covering her hands.\n\nShe was forty-one, tall, dark-skinned and composed, with her hair pulled into a low bun. Unlike the patrol officers moving excitedly around the car, Holloway looked at every piece of evidence as if it were part of a conversation only she could hear.\n\nOfficer Santiago approached with a clear evidence bag.\n\nInside was Reck’s phone.\n\n“Found beneath the driver’s seat,” Santiago said. “Screen is cracked, but it still powers on.”\n\nHolloway studied it through the plastic. A photograph filled the lock screen: five boys standing beneath a mural at the Back Court.\n\nReck stood in the center wearing red and gold. Mari grinned beside him. Zay towered over the others. TJ held up one hand toward the camera. Naz remained at the edge, expression unreadable behind his glasses.\n\n“Kids,” Holloway said.\n\n“One of those kids almost got me run over.”\n\n“That doesn’t make him grown.”\n\nAn evidence technician called from the sedan.\n\n“Detective, you need to see this.”\n\nThe black duffel bag rested open across the back seat. Its wrapped packages had been arranged beside the sealed handgun for photographs.\n\nHolloway’s face hardened.\n\n“Run the weapon,” she said. “Print everything. Find the registered owner of the car and pull every camera between the traffic stop and this lot.”\n\nSantiago held up Reck’s phone. “And him?”\n\nHolloway looked again at the five boys on the screen.\n\n“Find out who he is before whoever owns that bag decides he knows too much.”\n\n---\n\nReck and D-Low reached Sunridge shortly before sunrise.\n\nThey traveled separately for the final mile. D-Low disappeared toward The Palms. Reck crossed the canal on foot, wet jeans scraping his legs and ruined sneakers making soft sucking noises against the pavement.\n\nHis red shirt was torn. One chain was gone. Blood had dried beside his mouth, and dirt covered the bright clothes that had looked perfect only hours earlier.\n\nSunridge Gardens appeared quiet.\n\nIt wasn’t.\n\nFaces watched from dark windows. People stood outside The Sunshine speaking in low voices. Clips of the Paradise Beach chase had already spread across social media: the silver sedan running a red light, police cruisers striking its bumper, two figures sprinting toward the canal beneath a helicopter light.\n\nNobody could clearly identify the passenger.\n\nYet.\n\nReck headed toward Building 17.\n\nThe Ridge Boyz were waiting at the Back Court.\n\nMari paced beneath the broken streetlight wearing the same open orange jersey. Zay sat forward on the bleachers, elbows on his knees. TJ’s colorful clothes looked dull beneath the weak light. Naz stood beside the fence with his hood raised.\n\nAll four saw Reck at once.\n\nMari reached him first. “That was you?”\n\nReck kept walking. “Keep your voice down.”\n\n“That was you in that car?”\n\nZay rose. “Look at him. Of course it was.”\n\nTJ stared at Reck’s torn clothes. “People saying they found a bag.”\n\n“People say anything.”\n\nNaz looked at Reck’s empty hands. “Where’s your phone?”\n\nReck stopped.\n\nNobody spoke.\n\nNaz removed his glasses and cleaned them with the bottom of his hoodie. “Police have it.”\n\n“Maybe.”\n\n“Your location history, messages and pictures too.”\n\n“I know what’s in my phone.”\n\n“Do you know what police can pull out of it?”\n\nMari stepped between them. “Let him breathe.”\n\nZay looked toward The Sunshine. “King K know you came back?”\n\n“Not yet.”\n\n“Then go home,” Zay said. “Wake your mother and tell her the truth before police tell it for you.”\n\nReck laughed bitterly. “You got all the answers now?”\n\n“I had one answer before you left. Don’t get in that car.”\n\nReck walked toward him. “Say what you really want to say.”\n\nZay did not back away. “You wanted King K to notice you so bad you let him put you in a car with something that could bury you.”\n\n“I didn’t know what was in the bag.”\n\n“That make it better?”\n\n“I did it for my family.”\n\n“Everybody says that after.”\n\nReck shoved Zay’s chest.\n\nZay barely moved.\n\nMari grabbed Reck’s arm. “Chill.”\n\n“Get off me.”\n\n“We waited all night thinking you was dead or locked up!” TJ shouted.\n\nThe youngest boy’s voice cracked. Anger and fear fought across his freckled face.\n\n“You told us you didn’t need us,” TJ continued. “Then the whole city chasing you and we had to find out online.”\n\nReck looked at each of them.\n\nThese were not King K’s people. They did not need his money, fear his reputation or owe him favors. They had stayed because they loved him.\n\nThat made their disappointment harder to face than Mon’s threats.\n\n“I messed up,” Reck said.\n\nMari blinked. “What?”\n\n“You heard me.”\n\n“Say it again. I want to record it.”\n\nDespite everything, Zay almost smiled.\n\nNaz put his glasses back on. “What happens now?”\n\nA car turned into the parking lot.\n\nBlack headlights swept across the court.\n\nMon’s Chevrolet stopped beside the fence.\n\nThe driver’s door opened.\n\nMon stepped out with C-Mack and another man behind him. He wore the same black shirt from earlier, but his calm had disappeared.\n\n“Now,” Reck said, “we find out.”\n\nMon entered through the court gate.\n\n“King K wants you at the car wash.”\n\n“I just got back.”\n\n“He knows.”\n\n“Then he know D-Low was driving.”\n\nMon stopped several feet away. “He knows D-Low lost his property. He knows you left your phone in the car. And he knows both of you ran without calling until police were already chasing you.”\n\n“What was I supposed to do?”\n\n“You can explain that to him.”\n\nMon looked toward the other boys.\n\n“Alone.”\n\nReck wiped dried blood from his lip.\n\n“They coming.”\n\n“They weren’t invited.”\n\nMari moved beside Reck. “We ain’t asking.”\n\nC-Mack laughed from behind Mon. His cheek had swollen from the Back Court fight.\n\n“These little boys think they an army now.”\n\nZay stepped to Reck’s other side. His height erased C-Mack’s smile.\n\nTJ joined them, frightened but determined not to show it.\n\nNaz moved last. He did not stand shoulder to shoulder with the others. He remained half a step behind, watching Mon, C-Mack, the second man and the Chevrolet.\n\nCounting.\n\nMon’s eyes traveled across all five.\n\n“This ain’t a basketball game,” he said.\n\nReck stepped forward.\n\n“I ain’t ask for that chase. I ain’t tell D-Low to run, and I ain’t put that bag in the car. But I’m not letting nobody treat me like I’m weak either.”\n\nThe apartment windows seemed to lean closer.\n\n“We out here living fast,” Reck continued, “so we know what comes with it. If King K wants his money, we can talk business. If he wants to disrespect me, threaten my family or go to war over it, then it’s whatever. I’m not a pushover, and I’m not standing by myself.”\n\nMari nodded immediately. “Not ever.”\n\nZay’s expression remained serious. “We handle our problems together.”\n\nTJ lifted his chin. “All of us.”\n\nNaz looked directly at Mon. “So tell King K five are coming.”\n\nFor several seconds, Mon said nothing.\n\nThen he smiled.\n\nIt was the first time Reck had seen him do it, and there was no warmth inside it.\n\n“Get in the car.”\n\n---\n\nKing’s Premium Car Wash stood closed beneath the purple light before sunrise.\n\nDuring business hours, employees detailed luxury cars beneath white awnings while music played from outdoor speakers. Tonight, the gates were locked. Only the office lights were on.\n\nMon led the Ridge Boyz through the wash tunnel. Their footsteps echoed between silent machinery. Water dripped from hanging brushes like rain inside a cave.\n\nKing K waited in the upstairs office with Bree and D-Low.\n\nD-Low had changed clothes. A cut marked his forehead. He would not meet Reck’s eyes.\n\nKing K sat behind a wide wooden desk. He looked at the five boys entering his office and then at Mon.\n\n“I asked for one.”\n\n“One brought four,” Mon said.\n\nKing K leaned back.\n\n“The Ridge Boyz,” he said. “That what people calling y’all?”\n\nNobody answered.\n\nBree placed several photographs on the desk. Images from traffic cameras showed the sedan racing through Paradise Beach. Another captured Reck’s face through the shattered passenger window.\n\n“Police will identify him,” Bree said. “If they haven’t already.”\n\nKing K looked at Reck. “You lost my product, my weapon and my connection at the beach. D-Low says the officer saw the bag because you pushed it beneath the seat.”\n\nReck turned toward his cousin.\n\nD-Low finally looked at him. “That’s what happened.”\n\nThe betrayal was small enough to sound reasonable.\n\nThat made it worse.\n\n“You told me to watch the bag,” Reck said.\n\n“I told you not to move.”\n\n“And who started the car?”\n\nD-Low’s jaw tightened.\n\nKing K raised one hand. The room became silent.\n\n“Blame does not repay loss,” he said. “Results do.”\n\nHe named a number.\n\nMari stopped smiling. TJ looked at Reck. Even Zay’s controlled expression broke.\n\nReck had never seen that much money outside music videos.\n\n“I don’t owe all that,” he said.\n\n“You and D-Low owe it together.”\n\n“Then why am I the only one being tested?”\n\nKing K’s gaze moved toward the four boys behind Reck.\n\n“Maybe you aren’t.”\n\nNaz understood first. “You want us working off his debt.”\n\nBree answered. “Nobody said that.”\n\n“You didn’t need to.”\n\nKing K studied Naz with new interest.\n\n“The quiet one listens,” he said.\n\nReck placed both hands on the desk.\n\n“My people don’t owe you.”\n\n“Your people followed you into my office.”\n\n“To stand beside me. Not work for you.”\n\nKing K’s polished calm disappeared for a moment.\n\n“Be careful, son.”\n\n“Don’t call me that.”\n\nMon closed the office door.\n\nMari moved closer to Reck. Zay shifted his feet. TJ’s hands trembled at his sides, but he stayed in place. Naz watched Bree, recognizing that she—not Mon—was studying every possible outcome.\n\nKing K rose slowly from his chair.\n\n“Your father once stood in this same neighborhood believing respect made him untouchable,” he said. “Then the streets taught him otherwise.”\n\n“I’m not my father.”\n\n“No. Your father knew when he was surrounded.”\n\nReck looked around the office.\n\nThen he looked back at King K.\n\n“So do I.”\n\nOutside, tires squealed near the front gate.\n\nBlue lights flashed across the car-wash windows.\n\nEveryone froze.\n\nA loudspeaker broke the morning silence.\n\n“Palm Haven Police Department. Nobody leave the building.”\n\nBree swept the photographs from the desk. Mon reached beneath his shirt. D-Low stepped away from the window.\n\nKing K’s eyes locked onto Reck.\n\nThe same question crossed every face inside the office.\n\nHow had police found them so quickly?\n\nThen TJ’s phone began to ring.\n\nThe sound filled the silent room.\n\nKing K looked at the youngest Ridge Boy.\n\nMon looked at the phone.\n\nAnd for the first time since entering the car wash, TJ looked like the fifteen-year-old he was.\n\n---"},{"number":5,"title":"Chapter Five — Nobody Talks","body":"TJ’s phone kept ringing.\n\nNobody inside King K’s office moved.\n\nBlue light swept through the blinds, crossed King K’s face and disappeared. Outside, police radios crackled beneath the mechanical hum of the car wash.\n\nMon extended one hand.\n\n“Give it to me.”\n\nTJ looked at Reck.\n\n“Who calling?” Reck asked.\n\nTJ pulled the phone from his pocket. The screen showed **MAMA** above a photograph of a woman in hospital scrubs.\n\n“My mom.”\n\nMon snatched it and rejected the call.\n\n“Hey!” TJ reached for it.\n\nMon held the phone above him. “You brought this in here with the location on?”\n\n“I don’t know!”\n\n“You don’t know?”\n\nReck stepped between them. “Give him his phone.”\n\n“Your phone already put police on one car,” Mon said. “Now his got them at the gate.”\n\n“Police could’ve followed anybody here.” Naz looked toward D-Low. “Especially someone who escaped a chase a few hours ago.”\n\nD-Low came away from the window. “Watch how you talk to me.”\n\n“Watch how many stories you tell.”\n\nKing K struck the desk with his palm.\n\nEverybody stopped.\n\n“Phones on the desk,” he said.\n\nMari hesitated. Zay removed his first. Naz followed. Mari placed his beside it. TJ glared at Mon until the older man dropped his phone with the others.\n\nReck had nothing to surrender.\n\nHis phone was already in police evidence.\n\nThe loudspeaker sounded again.\n\n“Exit through the front entrance with your hands visible.”\n\nKing K looked at Bree.\n\n“What do they have?”\n\n“Enough for the building,” she replied. “Maybe not enough for everybody inside.”\n\nMon moved toward a cabinet.\n\n“Leave it closed,” Bree said.\n\nHis hand stopped.\n\n“If they find you reaching when they enter, none of this matters.”\n\nFootsteps pounded below them.\n\nThe front door shattered.\n\n“Police! Search warrant!”\n\nMen shouted throughout the wash tunnel. Equipment crashed. Mari moved closer to Reck. Zay grabbed the back of TJ’s shirt before the youngest boy could retreat toward the corner.\n\n“Hands where they can see them,” Zay said.\n\nThe office door burst inward.\n\nOfficers flooded the room wearing tactical vests and helmets. Red laser dots traveled across shirts, walls and faces.\n\n“On the floor!”\n\nReck lowered himself beside his friends. Plastic restraints tightened around his wrists. A boot pressed between his shoulder blades while officers searched his pockets and removed King K’s envelope.\n\n“That’s mine,” Reck said.\n\n“Nobody asked,” an officer replied.\n\nAcross the room, King K lay facedown in his cream polo, silent and composed. Mon looked furious enough to bite through the floor. D-Low stared at Reck as if they were strangers.\n\nTJ’s breathing became fast and shallow.\n\n“Look at me,” Zay said from beside him.\n\n“Face down!” an officer shouted.\n\nZay kept his head turned anyway. “Breathe, TJ. You good.”\n\n“I can’t—”\n\n“Yes, you can.”\n\nReck heard the panic in TJ’s voice and pulled against the restraints.\n\n“He fifteen!”\n\nAn officer pushed him down harder.\n\n“Then he should’ve been home.”\n\nThat sentence stayed with Reck as officers raised him from the floor and marched him downstairs.\n\nOutside, dawn had arrived.\n\nSo had the cameras.\n\nLocal reporters stood beyond the police line. Neighbors held phones above the gate. Employees from nearby businesses watched as King K, Bree, Mon, D-Low and the Ridge Boyz were led from the car wash one by one.\n\nReck lifted his head.\n\nZay looked straight ahead. Mari tried to smile at the cameras. Naz studied every vehicle in the lot. TJ searched the crowd for his mother.\n\nThe Ridge Boyz entered police custody together.\n\n---\n\nDetective Holloway placed five photographs across the interview-room table.\n\nTJ sat opposite her with his wrists free and his hands hidden beneath his thighs. Without the other boys around him, he looked smaller. His curly Afro had flattened on one side from the ride downtown. The purple shirt that seemed bright at the Back Court now made him appear even younger beneath the fluorescent lights.\n\nHis mother had not arrived yet.\n\nHolloway sat without opening a folder or raising her voice.\n\n“Terrence, do you know why you’re here?”\n\n“Everybody calls me TJ.”\n\n“All right, TJ. Do you know why you’re here?”\n\n“Because y’all brought me.”\n\nHolloway almost smiled. “You sound like your friend Kareem.”\n\nTJ stared at the photographs.\n\nThey showed the Ridge Boyz at the Back Court, Reck entering The Sunshine and Mon’s Chevrolet leaving Sunridge with all five boys inside.\n\n“Where is Kareem’s phone?” Holloway asked.\n\nTJ shrugged.\n\n“We found it inside a car carrying narcotics and an illegal firearm.”\n\nTJ swallowed but said nothing.\n\n“Did Kareem tell you about the chase?”\n\nNothing.\n\n“Did D-Low take him to Paradise Beach?”\n\nNothing.\n\n“You’re fifteen years old,” Holloway said. “King K is not going to protect you. Mon is not going to protect you. When grown men get scared, they place everything on the youngest person in the room.”\n\nTJ looked toward the mirrored glass.\n\n“You think Reck will protect you?”\n\nTJ finally spoke. “I want my mama.”\n\n“She’s on her way.”\n\n“Then I’ll wait.”\n\nHolloway leaned back. “You understand you can help yourself?”\n\n“I ain’t got nothing to say.”\n\n“That line belong to you or Kareem?”\n\nTJ’s freckles stood out against his frightened face. His fingers trembled beneath the table.\n\nBut his voice held.\n\n“Nobody talks.”\n\n---\n\nReck sat in another room with dried canal water stiffening his jeans.\n\nHolloway entered carrying his cracked phone in an evidence bag.\n\nShe placed it between them.\n\n“You lose something?”\n\nReck looked at the lock-screen photograph of his crew.\n\n“Never seen it.”\n\n“That’s unfortunate. There are photographs of your mother, your brother and about six hundred pictures of you on it.”\n\n“A lot of people take pictures of me.”\n\n“Your confidence is impressive.”\n\n“Thank you.”\n\n“It wasn’t a compliment.”\n\nHolloway sat across from him.\n\n“Officer Santiago identified you as the passenger. Traffic cameras identified you. Your blood is in the car. Your fingerprints will probably be on the bag.”\n\nReck’s expression did not change.\n\n“You were used,” she continued. “King K put a sixteen-year-old beside contraband because he believed you were disposable.”\n\n“I don’t know no King K.”\n\n“You were arrested in his office.”\n\n“I went to get my car washed.”\n\nHolloway looked through the mirrored glass as if inviting whoever stood behind it to appreciate the answer.\n\n“At five in the morning?”\n\n“I like beating the crowd.”\n\nThis time she did smile.\n\n“You’re funny. That won’t make the evidence disappear.”\n\nShe placed a photograph of D-Low beside the phone.\n\n“DeAndre says you brought the duffel into his girlfriend’s car.”\n\nThe lie hit Reck harder than he expected.\n\nHe looked at D-Low’s photograph but did not touch it.\n\n“He says you asked him for a ride to Paradise Beach,” Holloway continued. “He panicked when he discovered what you were carrying.”\n\nReck heard D-Low inside the car.\n\nYou don’t open it.\n\nYou don’t ask the man receiving it what’s inside.\n\nFamily had brought him into The Sunshine. Family was now trying to bury him beneath it.\n\n“You want to tell me your version?” Holloway asked.\n\nReck leaned back.\n\n“I want a lawyer.”\n\nHolloway gathered the photographs.\n\n“That is the smartest decision you’ve made all night.”\n\nAt the door, she stopped.\n\n“One more thing. That envelope police took from you? The bills were recorded. King K’s people used money from a controlled operation. So whatever story he tells about helping your mother, understand this: he handed you evidence.”\n\nShe left Reck alone with that information.\n\n---\n\nBy early afternoon, four of the Ridge Boyz were released to their families while investigators continued processing evidence.\n\nZay’s mother drove away without looking at any of them. Mari’s uncle cursed from the parking lot. TJ’s mother held him so tightly that his face disappeared against her hospital scrubs.\n\nNaz waited alone near the courthouse steps.\n\nReck emerged last.\n\nShanice stood beside Amaya’s car wearing her hotel uniform. Her shoulder-length locs were tied behind her head. Exhaustion and anger had hollowed her round face.\n\nShe looked at the torn shirt, the dried blood and the plastic bag containing his wet shoes.\n\n“Kareem,” she said.\n\nHe could face Mon, King K and a police helicopter without lowering his eyes.\n\nHe could not look at his mother.\n\n“Get in the car.”\n\nNobody spoke during the drive home.\n\nWhen they entered Sunridge, every corner seemed to know Reck had returned. People pointed from Sunridge Plaza. Children rode bicycles beside the car. Someone had already printed a blurry image from the chase and placed the words **RECK REALLY LIKE THAT** across it.\n\nShanice saw it too.\n\n“They celebrating,” she said quietly. “Because they don’t have to live with what happens to you.”\n\nReck stared through the window.\n\nAt Building 17, the Ridge Boyz were waiting.\n\nAgain.\n\nShanice parked but kept the doors locked.\n\n“Those boys love you,” she said. “That means you can save every one of them or destroy every one of them. You decide which kind of leader you want to be.”\n\nShe unlocked the doors.\n\nReck stepped out.\n\nMari met him with a grin. “Nobody said nothing.”\n\nTJ lifted his chin proudly. “Nobody.”\n\nZay looked at Reck. “What did they show you?”\n\nReck’s eyes moved toward The Sunshine.\n\nD-Low stood on the porch speaking with Mon.\n\n“My cousin told police the bag was mine.”\n\nMari’s grin vanished.\n\nTJ stared across the parking lot.\n\nZay exhaled slowly.\n\nNaz nodded once, as if a suspicion had just become fact.\n\n“What we doing?” Mari asked.\n\nReck watched D-Low laugh at something Mon said.\n\nHours earlier, Reck had been prepared to accept his share of the loss. He had been prepared to work, repay the debt and stand in King K’s office without bending.\n\nThat was before family placed his freedom on the table to protect itself.\n\n“Nothing yet,” Reck said.\n\nMari looked disappointed. “Nothing?”\n\nReck turned toward his crew.\n\n“Being loud got King K watching me. Being reckless got police watching me. Now we move smart.”\n\nNaz’s expression changed first. Approval.\n\n“We find out what D-Low told King K,” Reck continued. “We find out what he told police. We find out who really put that bag together and why the money King K gave me was already marked.”\n\nZay studied him. “Then what?”\n\nReck looked back at The Sunshine.\n\nD-Low noticed him watching.\n\nThe cousins held each other’s gaze across the parking lot.\n\n“Then,” Reck said, “we decide who started the war.”\n\n---"},{"number":6,"title":"Chapter Six — Family Debt","body":"King K gave D-Low until sunset.\n\nThe message arrived before breakfast through Mon, who found him sitting alone inside The Sunshine with the security monitors glowing against his face.\n\nNobody else was working.\n\nThe folding tables had been cleared. The waiting room was empty. King K had shut down the duplex before police could raid it, and every hour it remained closed cost money.\n\nMon entered without knocking.\n\n“Get up.”\n\nD-Low remained in the chair. His gold-framed glasses rested on the table beside him. Without them, the exhaustion around his eyes made him look older than twenty-five.\n\n“I’m working on it.”\n\n“You got twelve hours.”\n\n“King K know I can’t replace all that in twelve hours.”\n\nMon stepped close. “He knows.”\n\nD-Low looked toward the dead security screens.\n\n“And after twelve hours?”\n\n“You know.”\n\nMon picked up the glasses and crushed them beneath his boot.\n\n“You blamed a child for your bag,” he said. “Now you hiding inside a house King K owns, asking what happens when time runs out.”\n\nD-Low stared at the broken gold frames.\n\n“Reck ain’t no child.”\n\n“Then you created another problem.”\n\nMon walked out.\n\nD-Low waited until the reinforced door closed before removing a small prepaid phone from beneath the seat.\n\nHe dialed a number he had promised never to use.\n\nA man answered after one ring.\n\n“You got my property?”\n\nD-Low looked toward the front windows.\n\n“Police got it.”\n\nSilence.\n\n“Then why are you calling me?”\n\n“Because King K set us both up.”\n\n---\n\nAt noon, Reck stood outside Sunridge Plaza wearing a fresh black tracksuit, gold chains and brand-new sneakers purchased with part of King K’s envelope.\n\nShanice had refused the money.\n\nShe found it on the kitchen table, counted enough to understand where it came from and pushed it back against Reck’s chest.\n\n“I would rather sleep in my car than let the streets pay my rent with you,” she told him.\n\nReck kept the envelope anyway.\n\nIn The Ridge, looking defeated invited people to participate in your defeat. So he dressed as if the chase, arrest and interrogation had improved his life.\n\nMari leaned against the plaza wall beside him, short and broad in an orange football jersey. Zay stood near the curb wearing blue basketball warmups. TJ sat on the hood of an abandoned car in a purple shirt, while Naz remained beneath the laundromat awning in forest green and charcoal.\n\nFive different faces.\n\nFive different styles.\n\nOne gathering that had drawn the attention of the entire plaza.\n\n“There he go,” TJ said.\n\nD-Low crossed the parking lot alone.\n\nHis usual glasses were missing. A bruise darkened one side of his face. People stepped away as he approached, sensing that whatever happened next belonged to the whole neighborhood.\n\nReck met him in front of Lena’s Kitchen.\n\n“You told police that bag was mine.”\n\nD-Low looked at the crowd gathering behind them. “We need to talk somewhere private.”\n\n“You blamed me in private. Explain it in public.”\n\n“Reck—”\n\n“My name was Kareem when you asked me to get in that car. Then police came, and suddenly I was Reck again.”\n\nMari moved closer. Zay remained still, but his presence closed one side of the parking lot. TJ recorded with his phone held low. Naz watched the black SUV idling across Paradise Boulevard.\n\nD-Low lowered his voice. “King K told me what to say.”\n\nThe words quieted the plaza.\n\nReck’s expression hardened. “Say it louder.”\n\n“King K told me to put the bag on you.”\n\nAcross the street, the black SUV’s headlights came on.\n\nNaz saw them first.\n\n“We need to move.”\n\nReck ignored him. “Why?”\n\nD-Low looked over his shoulder. “Because that delivery was never supposed to arrive.”\n\nThe SUV jumped from the curb.\n\nIts rear window lowered.\n\nNaz shouted, “Down!”\n\nGunfire ripped across Sunridge Plaza.\n\nStorefront glass exploded. People dropped shopping bags and ran. Reck hit the pavement as bullets chewed through the wall above him. Mari tackled TJ behind the abandoned car. Zay pulled an elderly woman into Lena’s Kitchen. Naz disappeared behind a concrete planter as the black SUV rolled through the lot.\n\nD-Low drew a handgun and fired toward it.\n\nThe SUV swerved but did not stop shooting.\n\nReck crawled behind a parked sedan. Glass rained over his back. A bullet struck the hood inches above his head, leaving a smoking hole in the metal.\n\nThe SUV accelerated toward Paradise Boulevard.\n\nD-Low kept firing until it disappeared between traffic.\n\nThen silence arrived all at once.\n\nA car alarm wailed. Someone screamed inside the beauty shop. Water poured from a shattered pipe above the laundromat. Lena Price stood in her doorway with one hand pressed against her shoulder, blood spreading through her yellow blouse.\n\nZay caught her before she fell.\n\n“Call an ambulance!” he shouted.\n\nTJ stared at the blood, frozen.\n\nMari grabbed his face. “Look at me. You hit?”\n\nTJ shook his head.\n\nNaz came from behind the planter. A thin cut crossed his forehead, but his voice remained calm.\n\n“Police will be here in minutes.”\n\nReck rose from behind the sedan.\n\nHis black tracksuit was gray with dust. The flashy smile he wore before D-Low arrived had vanished.\n\n“That was Mon?” he asked.\n\nD-Low tucked the gun beneath his shirt. “One of his trucks.”\n\n“King K shot up his own plaza?” Mari asked.\n\n“He don’t own this one,” D-Low replied. “And he wasn’t shooting at the plaza.”\n\nHe looked at Reck.\n\n“He was shooting at us.”\n\nSirens rose in the distance.\n\nD-Low grabbed Reck’s arm. “You want the truth? Come with me now.”\n\nReck pulled free. “I’m not getting in another car with you.”\n\n“Then stay and let Mon circle back.”\n\nMari stepped beside Reck. “Where we going?”\n\nD-Low looked at all four Ridge Boyz.\n\n“This ain’t for them.”\n\nReck answered without hesitation. “Then it ain’t for me.”\n\nD-Low looked toward the approaching lights.\n\n“Fine. All of you.”\n\n---\n\nThey crossed Sunridge through back streets and canal paths, avoiding the patrol cars flooding Paradise Boulevard.\n\nD-Low led them beyond Cypress Bottom to an abandoned boatyard near the western marshes. Rusted fishing vessels leaned against broken stands. Tall grass moved around empty trailers. Beyond the property, dark water stretched toward the Gulf beneath a sky heavy with afternoon storms.\n\n“Who we meeting?” Naz asked.\n\nD-Low kept walking. “A man King K don’t want Reck to know exists.”\n\nThey entered a corrugated-metal warehouse.\n\nThree black luxury SUVs waited inside.\n\nMen stood around them wearing clean linen shirts, dark pants and no visible jewelry. They did not look like Sunridge hustlers. They looked organized, disciplined and completely comfortable inside a place nobody else knew was operating.\n\nA tall man stepped from behind the center vehicle.\n\nHe was in his mid-thirties, dark-skinned and lean, with a close-cropped beard and a pale burn scar climbing the left side of his neck. A cream linen shirt hung open at the collar. On one wrist sat a plain black watch worth more than every car in the Back Court parking lot.\n\nHis name was Marcellus Baptiste.\n\nPalm Haven knew him as **Cell**.\n\nCell looked past D-Low and studied Reck.\n\n“Kareem Dawson Jr.”\n\nReck disliked how easily powerful strangers knew his government name.\n\n“People call me Reck.”\n\n“Your father called you Little Reem.”\n\nThe warehouse became still.\n\nReck stepped forward. “You knew my father?”\n\nCell smiled faintly. “Before prison. Before King K owned a car wash. Before D-Low learned how expensive fear could become.”\n\nD-Low lowered his eyes.\n\nCell motioned toward the SUVs. One of his men opened the rear of the center vehicle.\n\nInside were sealed cases.\n\nEnough product to change the balance of Sunridge Gardens.\n\nMari stared openly. TJ tried not to. Zay looked toward the exits. Naz watched Cell instead of the cargo.\n\nReck kept his attention on the man’s face.\n\n“What this got to do with the bag?”\n\nCell walked closer.\n\n“The Paradise Beach shipment belonged to me. King K claimed Harbor View robbed one of his trap houses and told me he needed enough weight to answer. I gave him a smaller shipment as a test.”\n\n“A test he put in my lap.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Why was the money marked?”\n\nCell looked toward D-Low.\n\nD-Low removed the broken remains of his glasses from his pocket and placed them on a workbench.\n\n“Because King K has been feeding police controlled money,” he said. “He points them toward whoever he wants removed.”\n\nReck stared at him. “You knew?”\n\n“Not until after the chase.”\n\n“That what you called somebody to say?” Naz asked.\n\nD-Low looked at him. “How you know I called anybody?”\n\n“Because scared men don’t wait for deadlines. They look for another boss.”\n\nCell’s faint smile returned.\n\n“Keep the quiet one close,” he told Reck.\n\nZay crossed his arms. “If King K wanted Reck arrested, why send D-Low in the same car?”\n\n“Because D-Low had become a liability,” Cell replied. “King K removed two problems with one bag.”\n\nD-Low’s face tightened.\n\nReck thought about King K sitting calmly behind the desk.\n\nBecause nobody looking for you yet.\n\nHe had mistaken those words for opportunity.\n\nThey had been a sentence.\n\n“King K planned the traffic stop?” Reck asked.\n\n“A corrupt officer was supposed to recover the bag quietly,” Cell said. “D-Low running turned a controlled seizure into a citywide spectacle.”\n\nThe plot twist rearranged everything Reck believed about that night. The invitation, the envelope, the route, the police waiting near Paradise Beach—none of it had been chance.\n\nHe had never been recruited.\n\nHe had been selected to fall.\n\nGunfire struck the warehouse doors.\n\nMetal sparked inward.\n\nCell’s men moved immediately. One pulled him behind an SUV. Another killed the warehouse lights. Darkness swallowed the room as more shots tore through the walls.\n\n“Mon followed us!” D-Low shouted.\n\n“Everybody down!” Zay pulled TJ behind a rusted engine block.\n\nMari crouched beside Reck. “This what you call moving smart?”\n\n“Not right now, Mari.”\n\nThe main door rattled beneath an impact. Headlights appeared through gaps in the metal.\n\nCell’s people returned fire from cover. The sound became deafening inside the warehouse. Glass burst from one SUV. A tire collapsed. Bullets punched white holes through the corrugated walls.\n\nD-Low moved toward a side exit.\n\nReck grabbed his jersey. “Where you going?”\n\n“To draw them away.”\n\n“Or leave us again?”\n\nD-Low looked at his cousin.\n\n“You can hate me if we live.”\n\nHe pushed through the side door.\n\nSeconds later, a car engine roared outside. Gunfire shifted toward it.\n\nCell appeared beside Reck, calm even as bullets struck the SUV behind them.\n\n“Your cousin just purchased us time,” he said.\n\n“He still owe me.”\n\n“Survival keeps accounts open.”\n\nOne of Cell’s men shouted that the marsh exit was clear.\n\nThe Ridge Boyz followed him through a rear passage and into tall grass behind the warehouse. Zay kept TJ in front of him. Mari stayed shoulder to shoulder with Reck. Naz looked back once, memorizing the vehicles entering the boatyard.\n\nMon stepped from the lead truck with a rifle in his hands.\n\nNaz saw him.\n\nMon saw Naz.\n\nThat single glance confirmed the war more clearly than any declaration could.\n\nThe Ridge Boyz reached an airboat hidden beside the marsh. Cell climbed aboard with them while his remaining men covered the retreat.\n\nThe engine thundered.\n\nThe boat shot across the shallow water as black smoke rose from the warehouse behind them.\n\nReck watched flames appear along the roof. Somewhere beyond the fire, D-Low was either escaping, captured or dead.\n\nNobody knew.\n\nCell stood beside Reck as the Florida marsh opened ahead.\n\n“King K tried to erase you,” he said. “Now you have a decision.”\n\nReck looked toward his friends.\n\nMari’s orange jersey was torn. Blood from Naz’s forehead marked his charcoal hoodie. TJ’s freckles had disappeared beneath dirt. Zay remained alert, protecting the youngest even while watching Cell. Every one of them had followed Reck into a war they had not started.\n\nBut they had chosen to stay.\n\n“What decision?” Reck asked.\n\nCell pointed back toward the burning warehouse.\n\n“You can spend your life running from men who already decided you are disposable.”\n\nThen he pointed toward the distant Palm Haven skyline.\n\n“Or I can boss you up.”\n\nReck looked at him.\n\n“Why would you do that?”\n\nCell’s burn scar caught the orange light from the fire.\n\n“Because King K stole from me. Because your father once saved my life. And because Sunridge is about to choose a new leader whether King K accepts it or not.”\n\nReck felt the entire future of The Ridge shifting beneath him.\n\n“What you want in return?”\n\nCell smiled.\n\n“Everything King K thinks belongs to him.”\n\n---"},{"number":7,"title":"Chapter Seven — Bossed Up","body":"The airboat carried them deep into the marsh.\n\nPalm Haven disappeared behind walls of sawgrass and black water. The fire at the boatyard became an orange stain beneath the clouds, then vanished completely.\n\nCell stood near the front with one hand resting against the rail. He had not raised his voice during the attack. He had not asked whether his warehouse, vehicles or product could be saved.\n\nHe watched Reck instead.\n\n“Do you want enough money to survive,” Cell asked, “or enough power to take The Ridge?”\n\nThe airboat engine thundered beneath them.\n\nReck thought about the eviction notice hidden in Shanice’s bedroom. He thought about King K’s marked money, Mon’s gunfire crossing Sunridge Plaza and D-Low disappearing behind the burning warehouse.\n\nSurvival had kept his family in Building 17.\n\nPower could change who owned the building.\n\n“I want The Ridge,” Reck said.\n\nCell nodded once. “Then understand something. A package makes you a hustler. Fear might make you dangerous. Neither makes you a boss.”\n\n“What does?”\n\n“People choosing your order when another man is threatening them.”\n\nReck looked at his crew.\n\nMari grinned first. “They gon’ choose us.”\n\nZay did not share the excitement. “People get to choose, or we force them?”\n\nCell studied the tall athlete. “Good question.”\n\nReck answered before he could. “King K uses fear. We give them a better reason.”\n\nNaz wiped blood from the cut on his forehead. “And if they don’t want either side?”\n\n“Then they stay out of it,” Reck said.\n\nCell’s expression revealed nothing, but something in his eyes approved.\n\nTJ looked back toward the lost shoreline. “What about D-Low?”\n\nNobody answered.\n\n---\n\nCell’s safe house sat on a private piece of land beyond the marsh, hidden behind cypress trees and a hurricane-damaged citrus farm.\n\nFrom the outside, it looked abandoned. Inside the main barn were polished concrete floors, surveillance screens and vehicles covered beneath black fabric.\n\nCell gave the Ridge Boyz dry clothes, food and a room to sleep in.\n\nReck did not sleep.\n\nHe stood before a wall-sized map of Palm Haven marked with neighborhoods, businesses and colored lines. Sunridge Gardens occupied a small section on the west side. Paradise Beach glowed along the coast. Harbor View Courts sat across the canal like an open challenge.\n\nNaz entered wearing a forest-green shirt and dark jeans. A bandage crossed his forehead beneath the front of his cornrows.\n\n“You believe him?” Naz asked.\n\n“About King K?”\n\n“About any of it.”\n\nReck stared at the map. “I believe King K tried to bury me.”\n\n“That doesn’t make Cell honest.”\n\n“He don’t have to be honest.”\n\n“Then what does he have to be?”\n\nReck pointed toward Sunridge.\n\n“Useful.”\n\nNaz stepped closer. “And what are we to him?”\n\nReck did not answer.\n\nThat was the question he had been avoiding.\n\nCell entered behind them. His cream linen shirt had been replaced with a fitted black one that exposed more of the burn scar along his neck.\n\n“Your friend sees the entire board,” he said.\n\nNaz turned. “And you see pieces.”\n\n“Everybody with power does.”\n\n“Where’s Reck’s father fit on your board?”\n\nFor the first time, Cell’s calm expression changed.\n\nOnly slightly.\n\n“Ask Reem when he calls from prison.”\n\nCell placed a key ring on the table.\n\n“Come outside.”\n\n---\n\nMorning sunlight filled the barnyard.\n\nThe black covers had been removed from two vehicles: a midnight-black Chevrolet Tahoe and a pearl-white Dodge Durango. Both had tinted windows and clean interiors.\n\nMari circled the Durango with his mouth open.\n\n“This ours?”\n\n“They belong to me,” Cell said. “You have permission to use them.”\n\n“Same thing for today.”\n\nZay leaned against the Tahoe, unimpressed. His royal-blue warmup jacket fit his long frame. “Cars don’t make us ready.”\n\n“Correct,” Cell replied.\n\nTJ emerged wearing a new purple-and-gray outfit, colorful sneakers and a crossbody bag. The clothes fit him properly instead of hanging from his slim body, but his freckles and full Afro still made him look like the youngest.\n\n“What make us ready?” he asked.\n\nCell looked toward Reck.\n\n“Leadership.”\n\nTwo men carried black cases from the barn and placed them on a folding table.\n\nInside the first were five custom pendants.\n\nEach featured the cracked apartment crown above an interlocking **RB**, backed by a Florida sunset and small green palm trees. The emblem was the same, but the chains were not.\n\nReck’s was heavy yellow gold.\n\nMari’s had orange stones around the border.\n\nZay’s was white gold with royal-blue details.\n\nTJ’s smaller pendant carried purple stones.\n\nNaz’s used darkened metal with a single forest-green palm.\n\nFive members.\n\nFive identities.\n\nOne symbol.\n\nCell handed Reck the gold pendant last.\n\n“Jewelry makes people look,” he said. “What you do after they look becomes your reputation.”\n\nReck fastened it around his neck. The cracked crown rested above his chains.\n\nFor the first time, the Ridge Boyz looked official.\n\nThe second case contained money and sealed packages.\n\nThe excitement disappeared from Zay’s face.\n\nCell closed the case before TJ could stare too long.\n\n“This is not a gift,” he said. “It is an investment. If you lose it, you owe me. If police take it, you owe me. If somebody close to you steals it, you still owe me.”\n\nReck met his eyes. “We heard this speech before.”\n\n“Then learn from the last ending.”\n\nCell pushed the case toward him.\n\n“King K’s people abandoned three locations after the car-wash raid. One is The Sunshine.”\n\nReck looked toward the others.\n\n“That’s our first move.”\n\nCell shook his head. “No. Your first move is deciding who you are before you return.”\n\nReck faced his friends.\n\n“Mari, you with me when it gets loud.”\n\nMari smiled. “Always.”\n\n“Zay, you keep everybody thinking before we move.”\n\nZay crossed his arms. “Including you.”\n\n“Especially me.”\n\n“TJ, you see everything moving through the neighborhood. But no posting and no chasing attention.”\n\nTJ nodded seriously. “I got it.”\n\n“Naz, you watch the money, information and everybody claiming they loyal.”\n\nNaz adjusted his glasses. “Who watches you?”\n\nReck looked across all four faces.\n\n“All of y’all.”\n\nThat answer mattered.\n\nCell watched from beside the table as the Ridge Boyz placed their hands together over the open case.\n\n“Built by the Ridge,” Reck said.\n\n“Built by the Ridge,” the others answered.\n\n---\n\nThe two SUVs entered Sunridge Gardens shortly after noon.\n\nMusic shook the pearl-white Durango as Mari drove through Sunridge Plaza with the windows lowered. His gold grill flashed while he laughed at the people turning to look. TJ sat beside him filming the neighborhood—but not posting—while the purple stones in his pendant caught sunlight.\n\nThe black Tahoe followed.\n\nZay drove. His long twists rested against his blue jacket. Naz sat in the passenger seat wearing charcoal and green. Reck occupied the back alone, dressed in black and gold with the cracked crown resting against his chest.\n\nThey passed Lena’s Kitchen.\n\nPlywood covered the shattered windows. Flowers, candles and handwritten cards surrounded the doorway. Ms. Lena had survived surgery, but she remained in the hospital.\n\nReck’s excitement cooled.\n\nThe war already had an innocent victim.\n\nThen they reached The Sunshine.\n\nC-Mack stood on the porch with six men.\n\nKing K had not abandoned the duplex. He had placed C-Mack there to hold it.\n\nResidents emerged from apartments as the SUVs stopped in the parking lot. Children gathered near the Back Court. Phones appeared along second-floor railings.\n\nReck stepped from the Tahoe.\n\nThe neighborhood reacted to the pendant, the clothes and the vehicles before he said a word.\n\nMari parked the Durango sideways and joined him. Zay came around the Tahoe. TJ remained close to Mari. Naz stood slightly behind Reck, studying the porch and every window around it.\n\nC-Mack smiled through his swollen cheek.\n\n“Cell bought y’all costumes?”\n\nReck walked toward the steps.\n\n“The Sunshine closed.”\n\n“Look open to me.”\n\n“Then you ain’t hear me.”\n\nC-Mack came down one step. “Mon said nobody wearing that little pendant stands on King K’s property.”\n\n“Mrs. Green raised her family in this house before King K ever touched it,” Reck said loudly. “He don’t own The Ridge because he put a lock on somebody else’s door.”\n\nPeople murmured across the parking lot.\n\nC-Mack looked at the audience and realized Reck was doing it again—turning confrontation into performance.\n\n“You got until I count to three,” C-Mack said.\n\nMari laughed. “You ain’t even finish counting points yesterday.”\n\nC-Mack rushed from the porch.\n\nHe swung at Reck.\n\nReck slipped the punch and drove him against the railing. The six men behind C-Mack moved at once. Mari crashed into the first with his broad shoulder. Zay intercepted another, using his long frame to throw him away from TJ. Naz pulled the youngest boy behind a parked car as the fight spread across the yard.\n\nResidents shouted from the balconies.\n\nReck and C-Mack rolled down the porch steps. C-Mack struck Reck across the jaw, but Reck came back harder, fueled by the traffic stop, D-Low’s betrayal and Mon’s bullets crossing the plaza.\n\nHe hit C-Mack until the older boy stopped fighting back.\n\n“Reck!” Zay shouted.\n\nReck raised his fist again.\n\nZay grabbed his wrist.\n\n“You won.”\n\nFor one dangerous second, Reck considered striking his own friend.\n\nThen he saw Kobe near the Back Court.\n\nHis eleven-year-old brother stood beside a bicycle, round glasses reflecting the crowd. He was watching Reck the way he watched superheroes in his own drawings.\n\nReck lowered his hand.\n\nC-Mack coughed on the pavement.\n\nThe remaining men stepped away. Mari’s orange jersey had torn at the shoulder. Zay’s lip was split. TJ was unharmed. Naz had never entered the center of the fight, but he had recorded every person who came to support C-Mack and every person who quietly walked away.\n\nReck climbed onto The Sunshine’s porch.\n\nHe removed King K’s old lock from the broken door and threw it into the yard.\n\n“The Sunshine closed,” he announced. “Nobody sells out this house. Nobody from King K’s crew works on this side of Garden Courts. This building belongs to The Ridge now.”\n\nAn older woman on the balcony shouted, “What you turning it into?”\n\nReck looked through the doorway at the ruined rooms.\n\nHe could reopen the same trap house beneath a new name and become a younger copy of King K.\n\nOr he could make the building mean something larger.\n\n“Our headquarters,” he said. “And the front stays clean.”\n\nNaz looked at him sharply.\n\nThat had not been discussed.\n\nNeither had the next declaration.\n\n“Anybody in Building 17 who need groceries, school clothes or rent help comes here,” Reck continued. “King K made money off The Ridge. We putting money back into it.”\n\nThe crowd reacted.\n\nSome cheered. Others exchanged skeptical looks. Older residents had heard promises from young men wearing new jewelry before.\n\nCell’s lesson returned to Reck.\n\nPeople choosing your order when another man is threatening them.\n\nReck turned toward the Ridge Boyz.\n\n“Take his sign down.”\n\nMari and TJ pulled the small King’s Car Wash sticker from the front window. Zay helped C-Mack stand and pushed him toward the parking lot.\n\nNaz joined Reck on the porch.\n\n“That community promise costs money,” he said quietly.\n\n“Then we make money.”\n\n“Cell expects his first.”\n\n“He’ll get it.”\n\n“And if King K comes tonight?”\n\nReck looked across Garden Courts.\n\nMore residents were standing beside the Ridge Boyz than C-Mack now.\n\n“Then he finds out who The Ridge chose.”\n\n---\n\nAcross town, King K watched the takeover on three different phones.\n\nHe sat inside a private room above the Emerald Lounge with Bree beside him and Mon pacing near the bar.\n\nOn one screen, Reck stood on The Sunshine porch wearing Cell’s cracked crown.\n\nOn another, C-Mack lay beaten in the parking lot.\n\nOn the third, comments and reposts multiplied beneath the words:\n\n**RECK TOOK THE SUNSHINE.**\n\nMon slammed his fist against the bar.\n\n“Let me finish this tonight.”\n\nKing K watched Reck promise groceries and rent help to Sunridge residents.\n\n“No,” he said.\n\n“He just took your house.”\n\n“He took a building.”\n\nBree looked at King K. “He took the crowd.”\n\nThat was the part King K understood.\n\nHe set the phones facedown.\n\n“Where does his mother work?”\n\nMon stopped pacing.\n\nBree’s expression hardened. “Family wasn’t part of this.”\n\n“Family became part of it when Cell put Reem’s son against me.”\n\nKing K looked toward Mon.\n\n“Don’t touch her. Make sure Kareem understands we can.”\n\nMon smiled.\n\n---\n\nNight fell over The Sunshine.\n\nThe Ridge Boyz cleaned the house while music played from the porch. Mari dragged ruined furniture outside. Zay repaired the basketball hoop behind the building. TJ painted the first orange line of a new **RB** emblem across a plywood wall. Naz sat at a folding table with a notebook, counting every dollar from Cell’s case twice.\n\nReck stood in the upstairs bedroom overlooking Garden Courts.\n\nHis phone was gone, so Cell had given him a new one.\n\nIt rang from a blocked number.\n\nReck answered. “Who this?”\n\nFor several seconds, he heard only breathing.\n\nThen D-Low spoke.\n\n“Mon got your mama followed.”\n\nReck gripped the phone.\n\n“Where are you?”\n\n“That don’t matter.”\n\n“You alive because I ain’t decided what you owe me yet.”\n\nD-Low coughed painfully.\n\n“Listen. King K ain’t the biggest problem.”\n\n“You said that before the boatyard got shot up.”\n\n“Cell knew Mon was coming.”\n\nReck became still.\n\nDownstairs, the Ridge Boyz laughed while painting their new emblem.\n\n“What did you say?”\n\n“Cell knew we were followed,” D-Low whispered. “He let Mon attack that warehouse because he needed you to see King K as the enemy—and him as the man who saved you.”\n\nThe call ended.\n\nReck stood alone in the dark bedroom, looking over the neighborhood he had just claimed.\n\nKing K wanted to control him.\n\nCell wanted to build him.\n\nAnd D-Low wanted him to trust nobody.\n\nBelow the window, headlights entered Garden Courts.\n\nNot one car.\n\nTen.\n\n---"},{"number":8,"title":"Chapter Eight — Ten Cars","body":"The headlights filled Garden Courts.\n\nTen vehicles rolled through Sunridge Gardens in two slow lines—black sedans, dark SUVs and a gray pickup with its lights switched off. They stopped around The Sunshine until every street, parking-lot exit and path toward the Back Court appeared blocked.\n\nMusic died on the porch.\n\nTJ lowered his paintbrush.\n\nMari stepped toward the front window. “That King K?”\n\nNaz pulled him away from the glass. “It’s everybody.”\n\nReck came downstairs holding the new phone against his ear. D-Low’s warning still burned through him.\n\nMon got your mama followed.\n\nCell knew we were followed.\n\nOutside, doors opened.\n\nC-Mack emerged from the lead SUV with a bandage crossing his nose. Mon stepped from the vehicle behind him. More men spread across the parking lot while residents retreated from balconies and pulled children inside.\n\nMon looked toward the upstairs window.\n\n“Kareem!”\n\nReck opened the front door.\n\nZay caught his shoulder. “Don’t walk out there.”\n\n“This our house.”\n\n“A house ain’t worth dying over.”\n\nReck looked at him. “It ain’t about the house anymore.”\n\nHe stepped onto the porch with the four Ridge Boyz behind him.\n\nTheir new pendants caught the headlights: Reck’s gold, Mari’s orange border, Zay’s royal blue, TJ’s purple and Naz’s dark metal with the single green palm.\n\nMon smiled at the display.\n\n“Cell dressed his children.”\n\nReck searched the vehicles. One carried a small Harbor View decal beneath its rear window.\n\nNaz leaned close. “The gray pickup. That ain’t King K.”\n\nHarbor View had come to watch Sunridge fall—or help push it.\n\n“Where my mother at?” Reck asked.\n\n“Hotel,” Mon replied. “For now.”\n\nEvery muscle in Reck’s body tightened.\n\n“You follow my family again, I’m killing everybody you love.”\n\n“Big promise for somebody surrounded.”\n\nMon raised one hand.\n\nC-Mack and six men approached the porch.\n\n“Walk away from the house,” Mon said. “Leave Cell’s package, vehicles and jewelry. King K might let your friends go home.”\n\nMari laughed without humor. “Might?”\n\nReck descended one step.\n\n“Tell King K if he wants The Sunshine, come get it himself.”\n\nC-Mack drew a pistol.\n\nThe first shot struck the porch railing.\n\nThe Ridge Boyz dropped.\n\nThen Garden Courts exploded.\n\nGunfire crossed the parking lot from multiple directions. Windows burst inward. Bullets punched through The Sunshine’s yellow walls. Reck crawled behind the brick foundation while Mari dragged TJ through the doorway. Zay pulled Naz behind an overturned table.\n\nPeople screamed throughout the apartments.\n\n“Back exit!” Zay shouted.\n\n“They got it blocked,” Naz replied.\n\nReck looked through a hole in the railing. C-Mack was advancing between parked cars while Mon directed men from behind the lead SUV.\n\nThe gray Harbor View pickup remained near the court.\n\nIts occupants were not shooting at The Sunshine.\n\nThey were firing into the air and across empty vehicles, making the attack appear larger while waiting to see who survived.\n\n“Harbor View playing both sides,” Naz said.\n\nA bottle trailing fire struck the porch.\n\nFlames rolled across the steps.\n\nSmoke filled the front room. TJ froze beside the painted plywood emblem, staring as orange fire consumed the green palm trees.\n\n“Move!” Mari shoved him toward the kitchen.\n\nAnother round tore through the wall.\n\nMari jerked backward.\n\nTJ screamed.\n\nBlood spread across Mari’s orange jersey near the shoulder.\n\n“I’m good,” Mari said through clenched teeth. “It went through.”\n\nZay grabbed him beneath the uninjured arm and pulled him toward the hallway. Reck remained near the porch, watching C-Mack reach the burning steps.\n\nThe front door kicked inward.\n\nC-Mack entered through smoke with his pistol raised.\n\nHe saw Reck.\n\nMari saw him first.\n\nThe shot came from Mari’s hand.\n\nC-Mack stopped in the doorway. His weapon fell. He collapsed across the cracked apartment crown painted on the floor.\n\nMari stared at him.\n\nThe wild smile was gone.\n\nTJ looked from the body to Mari’s face.\n\nNeither boy spoke.\n\nOutside, a vehicle crashed through the rear fence.\n\nA faded green Buick struck one of Mon’s men and stopped beside the kitchen door. D-Low climbed out with blood soaking the side of his shirt.\n\n“Get in!”\n\nReck stared at him. “Where you been?”\n\n“Dying. We can talk later.”\n\nD-Low fired toward the parking lot. Zay pushed TJ into the back seat. Naz climbed beside him. Mari stumbled toward the car while pressing one hand against his wounded shoulder.\n\nReck remained in the doorway.\n\n“My family.”\n\n“Cell got people heading to the hotel,” D-Low said. “Move!”\n\nThat answer made Reck trust Cell less, not more.\n\nHe climbed into the Buick anyway.\n\nD-Low accelerated through the broken fence as Mon’s men turned toward them. Bullets shattered the rear window. Zay covered TJ with his body. Naz pulled Mari down against the floor.\n\nThe Buick reached the Back Court.\n\nThe gray Harbor View pickup moved to block them.\n\nD-Low did not slow.\n\nHe struck its front end hard enough to spin both vehicles. Metal folded. The Buick’s airbags exploded. Reck’s head hit the door.\n\nFor several seconds, the world became white powder, ringing ears and distant shouting.\n\nThen D-Low opened his door.\n\n“Run to the canal!”\n\nThe Ridge Boyz spilled from the wreck.\n\nMen climbed from the Harbor View pickup. One fired toward Zay and TJ. D-Low returned fire, forcing them behind the truck.\n\nReck grabbed Mari’s good arm and helped him toward the fence.\n\n“Go!” D-Low shouted.\n\n“You coming!”\n\n“I’m behind you.”\n\nReck knew the sentence was a lie.\n\nMon appeared beside the basketball court.\n\nD-Low saw him.\n\nThe cousins exchanged one final look.\n\n“I did you wrong,” D-Low said. “But I ain’t letting them bury you for it.”\n\nGunfire swallowed the rest.\n\nD-Low’s body turned beneath the impact. He remained standing long enough to fire back, then collapsed beside the faded free-throw line.\n\nReck stopped running.\n\nZay wrapped both arms around him and dragged him toward the canal.\n\n“He gave us time!” Zay shouted. “Don’t waste it!”\n\nReck fought him until he saw Mon approaching D-Low’s body.\n\nMon looked up across the court.\n\nReck pointed at him.\n\nNo words crossed the distance, but the promise did.\n\nThen the Ridge Boyz disappeared into the canal path.\n\n---\n\nPolice arrived eleven minutes after the first emergency call.\n\nBy then, The Sunshine was burning.\n\nC-Mack lay dead inside the doorway. Two of Mon’s men were found in the parking lot. D-Low died beneath the broken streetlight at the Back Court.\n\nA seventeen-year-old Sunridge resident named Andre “Dreko” Mills was found near Building 16. He had been walking home from work when the shooting began. He had no weapon, no crew affiliation and no connection to King K or the Ridge Boyz.\n\nFour bodies were covered before sunrise.\n\nDetective Holloway stood beside D-Low and looked toward the canal.\n\nReck’s gold chain lay in the dirt near the fence.\n\nShe placed it into an evidence bag herself.\n\n---\n\nCell hid the Ridge Boyz inside an empty vacation house north of Palm Haven.\n\nMari sat at the kitchen table while one of Cell’s people wrapped his shoulder. The bullet had passed through cleanly, but the blood covering his orange jersey belonged partly to him and partly to C-Mack.\n\nHe had not spoken since the Back Court.\n\nTJ sat on the floor with his purple pendant clutched in one hand. His phone played the same three seconds repeatedly: fire spreading across The Sunshine, a gunshot and the camera falling sideways.\n\nZay took it from him and shut it off.\n\n“You don’t need to keep watching.”\n\n“I see it when I close my eyes.”\n\nNaz stood at the window, bandage still crossing his forehead. “Harbor View was there.”\n\nCell entered the room. “I know.”\n\nReck came from the hallway wearing a plain black shirt. Smoke stained his face. D-Low’s blood covered one side of his jeans.\n\n“Did you know Mon was coming to the boatyard?” he asked Cell.\n\nCell did not answer immediately.\n\nThat was enough.\n\nReck walked toward him. Two of Cell’s men moved, but Cell raised his hand.\n\n“I knew King K was watching D-Low,” Cell said. “I did not know when Mon would move.”\n\n“You let us walk into it.”\n\n“And I got you out.”\n\nReck shoved him.\n\nThe room froze.\n\nCell looked down at Reck’s hands, then back at his face.\n\n“Do that again when you are ready to finish what it starts.”\n\nReck did not apologize.\n\n“D-Low dead. C-Mack dead. A neighborhood kid dead. Mari shot. My family being hunted. I’m finished waiting on grown men to decide when I can move.”\n\nHe turned toward the Ridge Boyz.\n\nMari finally raised his head.\n\nZay stood beside TJ.\n\nNaz left the window.\n\nReck’s voice filled the room.\n\n“They got me messed up. I gave everybody a chance to leave me and my family alone. They shot up our neighborhood, came at my mama’s house, killed my blood and thought I was supposed to hide.”\n\nHis fists tightened.\n\n“I’m done talking.”\n\nCell watched without interrupting.\n\n“I want everybody connected to this found. King K, Mon, C-Mack’s people, Harbor View—everybody. We taking their money, their cars, their jewelry and every block they think belongs to them. I don’t care what their name is or who backing them.”\n\nZay stepped forward. “People responsible. Not families. Not random people.”\n\nNaz nodded. “And no moving angry without knowing who we hitting.”\n\nReck looked at them both.\n\nFor a moment, rage argued with reason.\n\nThen Reck agreed.\n\n“Only the ones in it.”\n\nMari stood despite his wounded shoulder.\n\n“C-Mack was my first,” he said. His dimples did not appear. “He ain’t gon’ be the reason I quit.”\n\nTJ rose from the floor. He was shaking, but he put the purple pendant back around his neck.\n\n“Nobody talks,” he said.\n\n“Nobody runs,” Mari added.\n\nZay looked at Reck. “Nobody moves alone.”\n\nNaz finished it. “And nobody controls us.”\n\nThat last line was for Cell.\n\nReck reached into his pocket and placed D-Low’s broken gold glasses on the table.\n\n“They wanted to know who Reck was,” he said.\n\nOutside, thunder rolled over Florida.\n\n“Now I’m about to show them.”\n\n---"},{"number":9,"title":"Chapter Nine — La Madrina","body":"The black vans arrived before sunrise.\n\nFour masked drivers entered Cell’s vacation property without triggering an alarm. They disabled the outside cameras, surrounded the house and knocked once on the front door.\n\nCell’s men reached for weapons.\n\nNaz looked through an upstairs window and saw a silver wave painted beside a green crown on the lead van.\n\n“Mar Azul,” he said.\n\nCell’s face changed.\n\nReck noticed.\n\n“You know them?”\n\n“They have no business here.”\n\nThe front door opened before Cell gave permission.\n\nA tall man in a gray suit entered with empty hands visible. His shaved head carried a thin scar from his left temple to his ear. Behind him stood six disciplined figures dressed in black.\n\n“Kareem Dawson,” the suited man said. “La Madrina requests a meeting.”\n\nCell stepped between him and Reck. “The boy is under my protection.”\n\n“That protection burned down twice in forty-eight hours.”\n\nMari rose from the kitchen table with his wounded arm in a sling. Zay moved beside TJ. Naz remained near Reck.\n\nThe suited man looked across all five pendants.\n\n“The invitation includes the Ridge Boyz.”\n\nReck turned toward Cell. “You said Mar Azul was finished in Palm Haven.”\n\n“They are.”\n\nThe man smiled. “Then declining should be easy.”\n\nReck walked toward the door.\n\nCell caught his arm. “If you leave with them, our agreement changes.”\n\nReck looked down at Cell’s hand.\n\n“We never had an agreement. You had a plan.”\n\nCell released him.\n\nThe Ridge Boyz entered the vans together.\n\n---\n\nThey were blindfolded after leaving the property.\n\nThe ride lasted nearly an hour. Reck counted turns until Naz quietly told him to stop wasting his time. The vans traveled over a bridge, through a security gate and finally across a long gravel drive.\n\nWhen the blindfolds came off, they stood inside a coastal mansion overlooking the Gulf.\n\nWhite stone terraces descended toward a private marina. Tropical gardens surrounded an infinity pool. Original paintings covered the walls. Nothing about the home felt temporary or hidden.\n\nThe cartel did not merely operate in Florida.\n\nIt lived comfortably inside it.\n\nCamila Vélez waited on the rear terrace wearing a perfectly tailored white suit. An emerald necklace rested against her bronze-brown skin. Waist-length black hair fell across one shoulder, divided by a single silver streak. Her amber eyes moved across the boys without hurry.\n\n“The Ridge Boyz,” she said. “You look younger in person.”\n\nReck approached the table. “You look richer.”\n\nMari tried not to smile.\n\nLa Madrina motioned toward five chairs. “Sit.”\n\nReck remained standing. “We don’t know you.”\n\n“But you entered my vehicles.”\n\n“We wanted to see where they went.”\n\nShe smiled slightly. “Your father had that same talent for turning limited options into personal decisions.”\n\nReck’s expression hardened. “Everybody knew my father except me.”\n\n“Then ask better questions when he calls.”\n\nLa Madrina opened a blue folder.\n\nInside were photographs of King K meeting Officer Miller, the same officer who stopped D-Low’s sedan. Other images showed Mon exchanging bags with members of Harbor View. A final photograph captured Cell entering a private restaurant with Detective Holloway’s supervisor.\n\nReck picked it up.\n\n“Cell police too?”\n\n“No,” La Madrina said. “Cell purchases information. King K trades people for protection. Different arrangements. Same disease.”\n\nNaz studied the dates. “Why show us?”\n\n“Because all three profit from keeping Sunridge unstable.”\n\nShe pointed toward a map of western Palm Haven.\n\n“King K controls the visible street business. Harbor View controls the canal routes. Cell supplies both sides when the price is right, then pretends their war threatens him.”\n\nReck remembered Cell’s burned warehouse, the conveniently prepared airboat and the waiting safe house.\n\n“He knew Mon was coming,” he said.\n\n“He selected the location because he knew.”\n\nZay leaned forward. “How do we know you ain’t doing the same thing?”\n\nLa Madrina looked at him. “You don’t.”\n\nThe answer silenced the terrace.\n\n“Trust is an expensive word used by people trying to avoid contracts,” she continued. “I prefer shared enemies and clearly divided profits.”\n\nMari looked at the marina. “What kind of profits?”\n\nNaz cut his eyes toward him.\n\n“What?” Mari asked. “She brought it up.”\n\nLa Madrina closed the folder.\n\n“Cell stole a coastal route from my organization. King K compromised two of my operations to strengthen his arrangement with police. Harbor View occupies access I intend to reclaim.”\n\nShe looked directly at Reck.\n\n“I need a local organization capable of taking western Palm Haven.”\n\n“You need soldiers,” Reck said.\n\n“I have soldiers.”\n\n“Then what you need us for?”\n\n“Soldiers can enter a neighborhood. They cannot make that neighborhood choose them.”\n\nReck thought about the crowd outside The Sunshine.\n\nLa Madrina had been watching before he ever knew her name.\n\n“What you offering?” he asked.\n\n“Your family disappears into secure housing today. Attorneys begin challenging every piece of evidence against your crew. The Sunshine is rebuilt. Your organization receives vehicles, legitimate businesses and access independent of Cell.”\n\nTJ looked toward Reck, excitement briefly defeating fear.\n\nZay remained guarded. “And the price?”\n\n“Cell, King K and Harbor View lose Palm Haven.”\n\n“Lose how?”\n\nLa Madrina’s amber eyes remained calm. “Completely.”\n\nReck understood.\n\nShe was not offering to help him survive a war.\n\nShe was hiring him to finish one.\n\n“Everybody keeps offering to make me a boss like I need permission,” Reck said. “Help us win, and we make money together. But The Ridge belongs to the Ridge Boyz.”\n\nThe cartel soldiers around the terrace watched La Madrina.\n\nNobody spoke to her that way.\n\nShe lifted a coffee cup and took one measured sip.\n\n“Cell wanted to build you. King K wanted to bury you. I want to see whether you can rule.”\n\nShe placed the cup down.\n\n“Kings who disappoint me do not receive second chances.”\n\nReck leaned over the table.\n\n“Neither do connects who lie to me.”\n\nFor several seconds, the Gulf wind moved between them.\n\nThen La Madrina extended her hand.\n\nReck looked toward the Ridge Boyz.\n\nMari nodded immediately. TJ followed. Zay looked unhappy but loyal. Naz studied the photographs one final time, then gave the smallest nod.\n\nReck shook her hand.\n\nThe war changed ownership.\n\n---\n\nLa Madrina’s people moved Shanice, Amaya and Kobe before noon.\n\nShanice fought the decision until Reck showed her photographs of Mon’s men outside the Paradise Crown Resort. She packed one suitcase, held Kobe close and refused to look at the cracked-crown pendant around her son’s neck.\n\n“Every man helping you wants something,” she said.\n\n“I know.”\n\n“Do you?”\n\n“I’m getting y’all somewhere safe.”\n\n“Safe from the danger following you.”\n\nHer words stayed with him after the armored SUV carried his family away.\n\nReck did not have time to feel them.\n\nLa Madrina’s first piece of intelligence arrived that afternoon.\n\nKing K was moving cash from three closed locations into a private club downtown. Harbor View men would protect the transfer. Cell expected a percentage.\n\nOne movement.\n\nAll three enemies.\n\nThe Ridge Boyz waited inside a cartel garage as two covered vehicles were revealed. The first was a black Cadillac Escalade. The second was a dark-green Dodge Durango with a sunset-orange stripe.\n\nFresh RB emblems had been placed discreetly near the rear windows.\n\nNew clothes waited beside them.\n\nReck wore black and gold.\n\nMari kept orange across his uninjured shoulder.\n\nZay wore royal blue beneath a dark jacket.\n\nTJ chose purple and gray.\n\nNaz remained in forest green and charcoal.\n\nThey looked united without looking alike.\n\nLa Madrina entered with the tall suited man.\n\n“This is Rafael ‘Stone’ Guerra,” she said. “He coordinates security for Mar Azul in Florida.”\n\nStone’s scar curved toward his ear as he looked at Reck. “Tonight, you observe. My people handle the rest.”\n\nReck opened the Escalade’s driver door.\n\n“Then your people can follow us.”\n\nStone moved into his path.\n\n“You agreed to an alliance, not command.”\n\n“The money came from The Ridge. King K made it off our families. We taking it back.”\n\nZay sighed. “We been cartel-connected six hours and already arguing.”\n\nMari grinned despite the pain in his shoulder. “That might be a record.”\n\nLa Madrina looked at Stone.\n\n“Let them lead.”\n\nStone stepped aside.\n\nNaz waited until La Madrina walked away before speaking quietly.\n\n“She wants to see which one of us breaks first.”\n\nReck started the Escalade.\n\n“Then don’t break.”\n\n---\n\nNight covered downtown Palm Haven in neon and rain.\n\nKing K’s convoy left the Emerald Lounge shortly after midnight. The money traveled inside an armored SUV with two Harbor View vehicles ahead and Cell’s men behind.\n\nThey never reached the bridge.\n\nThe street erupted beneath flashing headlights and screaming tires. Vehicles collided. Men abandoned doors and scattered through the rain. Store alarms joined distant sirens as the Ridge Boyz struck the alliance holding their neighborhood hostage.\n\nReck saw King K’s armored SUV trapped between wrecked cars.\n\nHe stepped into the rain wearing the cracked crown.\n\nAcross the intersection, Mon emerged behind a vehicle.\n\nTheir eyes met through the storm.\n\nMon raised his weapon.\n\nReck did the same.\n\nBefore either fired, a Harbor View vehicle crashed between them.\n\nMari shouted from the Durango. Zay pulled TJ away from the open street. Naz pointed toward the armored SUV as Stone’s people surrounded it.\n\nThe money was taken.\n\nKing K’s alliance broke apart in every direction.\n\nReck climbed onto the running board of the Escalade as it accelerated away. Behind them, Mon stood in the intersection beneath police lights, watching the Ridge Boyz escape with everything he had been ordered to protect.\n\nInside the SUV, black cases covered the floor.\n\nTJ opened one.\n\nStacks of money filled it.\n\nMari laughed for the first time since killing C-Mack.\n\nZay looked through the shattered rear window at the approaching cruisers.\n\n“This ain’t over.”\n\nReck removed King K’s diamond watch from the top of the case and fastened it around his wrist.\n\n“It just started.”"},{"number":10,"title":"Chapter Ten — Everybody Eats","body":"By sunrise, King K’s watch was still on Reck’s wrist.\n\nThe money was no longer in the Escalade.\n\nNaz had counted every stack twice, separated the marked bills and divided the clean cash into locked cases. La Madrina’s people expected Reck to deliver everything to the mansion.\n\nHe delivered her percentage.\n\nThe rest went home.\n\nSunridge woke to the sound of construction at The Sunshine.\n\nThe burned windows were boarded first. Then came new glass, steel doors, security lights and crews clearing smoke-blackened furniture from the ruined car wash. Reck stood in the parking lot wearing black from his shoulders to his shoes, D-Low’s memorial photograph resting against the cracked wall behind him.\n\nResidents gathered slowly.\n\nSome came because they were curious.\n\nSome came because their apartments had been damaged during the siege.\n\nOthers came because everybody in Palm Haven had heard what happened downtown.\n\nReck raised King K’s watch where they could see it.\n\n“He made this off The Ridge,” he said. “So The Ridge getting paid first.”\n\nNaz opened a folding table covered with envelopes.\n\nRent assistance for the families forced from Garden Courts.\n\nMoney for Ms. Lena’s medical bills.\n\nFuneral expenses for D-Low’s mother.\n\nA retainer for TJ’s lawyer.\n\nAnother for Mari.\n\nReck placed the final envelope in Shanice’s name even though his mother was hidden somewhere he had not been allowed to know.\n\nZay watched the crowd change.\n\nFear did not disappear.\n\nIt became loyalty.\n\n“King K always took,” an older man said from beside the curb.\n\nReck looked toward him. “We building something different.”\n\n“Different how?”\n\nReck pointed toward the workers rebuilding The Sunshine.\n\n“Everybody connected to The Ridge eats. But nobody makes money here while our people starve. Nobody uses our blocks and acts like we don’t exist. And nobody gets to call themselves a Ridge Boy because they bought the right color shirt.”\n\nBehind the crowd stood nearly forty young men.\n\nSome Reck knew from childhood.\n\nSome had appeared only after the money did.\n\nAll of them wanted the cracked crown.\n\nMari studied them with his wounded arm resting in a sling.\n\n“Half of them wasn’t around when ten cars pulled up.”\n\n“That’s why half of them leaving without a pendant,” Naz said.\n\nReck stepped toward the recruits.\n\n“Anybody can stand beside us while we winning. I need to know who stays when the money gone, when the police come and when standing here costs something.”\n\nHe looked from face to face.\n\n“The Ridge comes first. You don’t switch. You don’t steal from the crew. You don’t abandon another member. You don’t bring families or innocent people into our war. If you get caught, you stand ten toes. If you betray this crown after taking our oath, there ain’t no coming back from it.”\n\nThe parking lot went silent.\n\n“Anybody got a problem with that,” Reck said, “leave now.”\n\nThirteen people walked away.\n\nReck respected them more than the ones who remained for the wrong reason.\n\nThe original five stood behind him—Mari in orange, Zay in blue, Naz in green, TJ in purple and gray.\n\nThey had separate faces, separate styles and separate minds.\n\nBut the crown belonged to all of them.\n\nReck turned toward Naz.\n\n“Nobody joins today.”\n\nSeveral recruits looked disappointed.\n\n“They earn it,” Reck continued. “Backgrounds checked. Loyalty tested. Every founder agrees. One no means no.”\n\nTJ smiled for the first time since the siege.\n\n“Now everybody want to be us.”\n\nZay looked at the police cruiser parked across the street.\n\n“Everybody watching us too.”\n\n---\n\nHarbor View Courts sat beside dark canal water on the western edge of Palm Haven.\n\nThat afternoon, Cell stood inside an abandoned boat-repair warehouse with Tide Mercer and the first Canal Boyz assembled around him.\n\nDeep teal banners covered the rusted walls. A silver CB emblem rose from painted water beneath a drawbridge.\n\nTide wore a navy jacket and a silver chain. His long twists were tied behind his head, exposing the scar across his lower lip.\n\nHe watched Cell place a brick-sized package on the table.\n\n“Reck got one neighborhood and started believing he owns the city,” Cell said. “La Madrina gave him supply because she wants my routes.”\n\n“Then we cut him off from the water,” Tide replied.\n\nCell smiled.\n\nMon did not.\n\nHe stood near the warehouse door, restless and angry, with two of King K’s remaining men beside him.\n\n“All this talking while Reck wearing King’s watch.”\n\nTide glanced at him. “You had him in the street and let him leave with it.”\n\nMon stepped forward.\n\nLock moved between them.\n\nCell raised one hand.\n\n“This is bigger than a watch. The Ridge Boyz are growing because people believe Reck protects them. So we make his protection look like a lie.”\n\nHe laid a photograph beside the package.\n\nAmaya Dawson sat blindfolded in an unfamiliar room.\n\nMon’s anger turned into a grin.\n\nCell tapped the photograph.\n\n“Tonight, we take his money. Tomorrow, we take his people. Then Harbor View takes Sunridge one block at a time.”\n\nTide looked toward the canal doors.\n\n“And when Reck comes?”\n\nCell’s voice hardened.\n\n“We remind him who made him.”\n\n---\n\nDetective Holloway entered the evidence room carrying a sealed envelope.\n\nCaptain Vance was already waiting.\n\nThe dead jump-out officer’s financial records lay open on the steel table. Deposits appeared beside dates of raids that had never been entered into the department system.\n\nKing K’s name appeared nowhere.\n\nHis confidential-informant number appeared seventeen times.\n\n“You weren’t supposed to request these,” Vance said.\n\n“A police officer died beside stolen cartel money.”\n\n“A gang member killed him.”\n\n“A corrupt officer stole from that gang first.”\n\nVance closed the folder.\n\n“Careful, Detective.”\n\nHolloway stared at him.\n\n“Is that professional advice or a threat?”\n\nHis phone rang before he answered.\n\nVance walked into the hallway.\n\nHolloway waited, then opened the sealed envelope.\n\nInside was a copy of King K’s cooperation agreement.\n\nAt the bottom, beneath an old prosecutor’s signature, was the name of the operation that sent Kareem Dawson Sr. to prison.\n\nHolloway read it twice.\n\nKing K had not merely survived Reem’s downfall.\n\nHe had helped create it.\n\n---\n\nThe call reached Reck shortly before dark.\n\nLa Madrina’s mansion appeared on the screen behind his father.\n\nReem was supposed to be calling from prison.\n\nInstead, his face filled a private encrypted video feed while La Madrina stood beside the monitor in her white suit.\n\nReck’s expression changed.\n\n“How she get on this call?”\n\nReem leaned closer to the camera.\n\n“Because she was on my calls before you knew her name.”\n\nThe original five stood inside The Sunshine’s unfinished office. Plastic covered the windows. Drills and hammers echoed from the front garage.\n\nReck looked from his father to La Madrina.\n\n“What is he talking about?”\n\nLa Madrina said nothing.\n\nReem answered for her.\n\n“King K stole my streets. Cell stole my connection. They put me behind these walls and thought the Dawson name was finished.”\n\nReck’s jaw tightened.\n\n“La Madrina didn’t choose you, son.”\n\nReem paused.\n\n“I sent her.”\n\nNobody in the office moved.\n\nMari looked toward Naz.\n\nTJ stared at the screen.\n\nZay lowered his head as if the truth confirmed something he had feared from the beginning.\n\nReck stepped closer.\n\n“D-Low dead. Mari got blood on him. TJ facing charges. My mama and little brother can’t sleep at home. You knew all this was coming?”\n\n“I knew they would come for you eventually.”\n\n“So you built me into your replacement.”\n\nReem’s eyes remained cold.\n\n“No. I built you into my revenge.”\n\nThe words struck harder than anything King K had done.\n\nReck removed King K’s watch and placed it on the desk.\n\n“You don’t get to claim this.”\n\n“Everything you have came through my name.”\n\n“Everything I got came with one of my people bleeding for it.”\n\nReem’s voice rose. “Watch how you speak to me.”\n\n“You watch how you speak about the Ridge Boyz.” Reck pointed at the four beside him. “This ours. Not yours. Not Cell’s. Not hers.”\n\nLa Madrina finally spoke.\n\n“Reck—”\n\n“If we partners, say that. If I’m just your weapon for him, take your supply back.”\n\nFor the first time, La Madrina looked caught between two decisions.\n\nReem answered first.\n\n“You are not ready to stand without us.”\n\nReck ended the call.\n\nSilence filled the office.\n\nMari smiled slowly. “You just hung up on a prison boss and a cartel chief at the same time.”\n\n“They keep thinking somebody made us,” Reck said.\n\nNaz looked toward the window. “They did help.”\n\n“Help ain’t ownership.”\n\nZay stepped forward. “Then prove we can stand without becoming everybody we hate.”\n\nReck looked at him.\n\n“What that mean?”\n\n“It means neutral ain’t enemy. Everybody who won’t wear our crown ain’t against us. Protect the neighborhood. Handle the people who actually crossed us. Don’t turn The Ridge into a prison.”\n\nMari shook his head. “This ain’t the time to sound soft.”\n\n“It ain’t soft to know who you fighting,” Naz said. “It’s strategy.”\n\nReck looked at each founder.\n\nThen he nodded once.\n\n“Nobody innocent. Nobody neutral. But anybody helping Cell, King K, Mon or them dirty police chose a side.”\n\nTJ’s phone vibrated.\n\nHe checked the screen.\n\nHis face emptied.\n\nIt was a photograph of Amaya.\n\nBelow it were six words.\n\n**MIDNIGHT. BRING THE MONEY. COME ALONE.**\n\n---\n\nReck did not go alone.\n\nHe also did not take the money.\n\nNaz traced the photograph’s background to an old motel near Paradise Boulevard. Zay recognized the faded mural through a broken mirror. TJ found a second clue in the reflection of a delivery truck outside.\n\nThe original five prepared to move.\n\nThen every light inside The Sunshine died.\n\nThe drills stopped.\n\nThe music outside disappeared.\n\nThree unmarked police vehicles blocked the front entrance while a fourth rolled behind the building. Officers in tactical vests stepped into the parking lot.\n\nThe same jump-out unit that had stolen from the Ridge had returned.\n\nAt the opposite end of Sunridge Plaza, teal headlights appeared.\n\nCanal Boyz.\n\nNaz looked through the plastic-covered window.\n\n“This coordinated.”\n\nReck’s phone rang.\n\nCell.\n\nHe answered.\n\n“You wanted the street,” Cell said. “Come outside and see what it costs.”\n\nReck watched officers move toward the building while Canal Boy vehicles sealed the intersection beyond them.\n\n“Where my sister?”\n\n“Ask Mon.”\n\n“I’m asking you.”\n\nCell laughed quietly. “Bring me La Madrina’s shipment, return King’s money and surrender The Sunshine. Maybe your family survives your ambition.”\n\nReck looked toward the founders.\n\nMari removed his sling.\n\nZay pulled two young prospects away from the windows.\n\nTJ opened the security feeds on a tablet.\n\nNaz spread a neighborhood map across the desk.\n\nThey did not look afraid.\n\nThey looked ready.\n\n“You made one mistake,” Reck told Cell.\n\n“Only one?”\n\n“You thought La Madrina was the reason we got bigger.”\n\nOutside, apartment doors opened.\n\nResidents stepped onto balconies holding phones. Store owners turned on exterior cameras. Cars blocked side streets before the Canal Boyz could enter them. Men who had failed Reck’s membership test stood at the edges of the plaza anyway—not wearing pendants, not claiming the gang, but refusing to let outsiders invade their neighborhood.\n\nThe Ridge itself was closing around Cell’s plan.\n\nReck watched the live cameras multiply across TJ’s screen.\n\n“You brought Harbor View to our home,” he said. “Now the whole Ridge sees you.”\n\nA corrupt officer struck the front door with a metal ram.\n\nThe first blow cracked the new frame.\n\nThe second shook the building.\n\nBefore the third, red and blue lights flooded the opposite street.\n\nDetective Holloway stepped from an official cruiser with Internal Affairs behind her.\n\n“Palm Haven Police!” she shouted toward the jump-out unit. “Stand down!”\n\nConfusion broke the formation.\n\nOne officer ran.\n\nAnother reached toward his vest.\n\nThe Canal Boyz accelerated through the intersection, leaving the dirty unit exposed beneath dozens of cameras.\n\nBut one teal SUV did not leave.\n\nIt crashed through The Sunshine’s side gate.\n\nLock and three masked men rushed from it.\n\nThe building erupted into chaos.\n\nWorkers dropped behind cars. Glass shattered. Mari pulled TJ away from the garage as Zay shielded two residents trapped beside the office. Naz directed people through the rear exit while Reck moved toward the intruders.\n\nLock saw the cracked crown and charged straight for him.\n\nThey collided against the hood of King K’s recovered car.\n\nLock was heavier.\n\nReck was angrier.\n\nThe fight carried them across broken glass and wet concrete. Lock slammed Reck into a steel post, then reached for the chain around his neck.\n\nReck trapped his wrist.\n\n“You don’t earn this by touching it.”\n\nMari struck Lock from the side.\n\nZay forced the remaining attackers back toward their SUV as sirens surrounded the plaza. One Canal Boy fell wounded. Two escaped over the fence. Lock broke free and climbed into the damaged vehicle.\n\nBefore it reversed, Reck ripped the silver CB emblem from its grille.\n\nThe SUV disappeared through the rear alley.\n\nHolloway entered the garage with her weapon lowered but ready.\n\nShe saw Reck standing amid broken glass, blood running from his eyebrow, the Canal Boy emblem in one hand.\n\n“You need to come with me,” she said.\n\nReck laughed without humor. “For them attacking us?”\n\n“For staying alive long enough to hear what I found.”\n\nShe held up a copy of King K’s agreement.\n\n“King K helped put your father in prison. The officers outside have been protecting him for years. And somebody inside my department gave Cell the timing for this raid.”\n\nNaz stepped beside Reck. “Can you prove it?”\n\n“I can prove enough to make everybody involved desperate.”\n\nReck’s phone vibrated again.\n\nA live video appeared.\n\nAmaya sat inside the motel room. Mon stood behind her.\n\nKing K entered the frame wearing a county-jail uniform.\n\nReck stared.\n\nKing K was supposed to be locked up.\n\nKing smiled into the camera.\n\n“Your father got reach inside jail,” he said. “Mine reaches outside.”\n\nThe video ended.\n\nHolloway’s expression changed.\n\n“That’s impossible.”\n\nReck looked down at King K’s cooperation agreement.\n\nThen at the watch on the desk.\n\nThen toward the road leading to Paradise Boulevard.\n\nHe placed the torn Canal Boy emblem beneath his shoe and crushed it against the floor.\n\n“Naz, find out how King got out.”\n\n“Already working.”\n\n“TJ, keep eyes on that motel.”\n\nTJ lifted the tablet. “Got it.”\n\n“Zay, clear every resident away from here.”\n\nZay nodded.\n\n“Mari—”\n\nMari picked up his orange jacket.\n\n“You ain’t even got to say it.”\n\nReck fastened King K’s watch back onto his wrist.\n\nHe looked at Holloway.\n\n“You handle your police.”\n\nThen he looked at the Ridge Boyz.\n\n“We getting my sister.”\n\nOutside, the rebuilding crews returned to their feet.\n\nAcross Sunridge, phones carried the footage of corrupt officers and Canal Boyz attacking The Sunshine. By midnight, more people wanted the cracked crown than ever before.\n\nBut Reck accepted none of them.\n\nTonight belonged to the five who had started together.\n\nCell had declared war.\n\nReem had claimed ownership.\n\nKing K had escaped custody.\n\nMon had Reck’s sister.\n\nAnd the Ridge Boyz were coming for all of them."},{"number":11,"title":"Chapter Eleven — The Long Reach","body":"Paradise Boulevard looked abandoned after midnight.\n\nRainwater collected beneath the broken sign of the Royal Palms Motel. Half the rooms were dark. The others glowed behind curtains stained yellow by years of smoke.\n\nAmaya was inside one of them.\n\nReck sat in the passenger seat of a stolen sedan two blocks away, watching TJ’s tablet. The motel’s camera system had gone offline eleven minutes earlier.\n\nThat was not an accident.\n\n“They know we coming,” Mari said.\n\n“They invited us,” Naz replied.\n\nZay looked toward Reck. “Then we don’t enter the way they expect.”\n\nThe five founders had left The Sunshine together, but Sunridge was not unprotected. Three trusted associates remained behind with Stone’s cartel security posted beyond the neighborhood.\n\nOne of the associates was Malik “Mook” Ford.\n\nMook had grown up two buildings from Mari. He had attended D-Low’s funeral, helped carry injured residents during the siege and refused to abandon The Sunshine when the corrupt officers arrived.\n\nHe had not earned a pendant.\n\nBut he had earned enough trust to watch the building.\n\nReck’s phone vibrated.\n\nMook’s message was short.\n\n**RIDGE GOOD. GO GET YOUR SISTER.**\n\nReck showed Mari.\n\n“Told you he solid,” Mari said.\n\nNaz said nothing.\n\nHe was studying the motel map.\n\n“King wants us focused on room 214,” he said. “That means Amaya probably isn’t in 214.”\n\nTJ enlarged a frozen frame captured from the video. Behind Amaya, a brass number hung backward in the mirror.\n\n“It says twelve.”\n\nZay leaned closer. “Not 214?”\n\n“Building twelve,” TJ said. “Old laundry wing behind the pool.”\n\nReck opened the car door.\n\n“Then that’s where we going.”\n\n---\n\nAt Palm Haven County Jail, Reem Dawson stood alone inside the prison chapel.\n\nThe call from King K’s unit had reached him twenty minutes earlier.\n\nKing K had vanished during a medical transfer that did not exist on any official schedule. Two officers signed him out. Neither officer reported for work afterward.\n\nReem held the phone to his ear.\n\n“Close every door,” he said.\n\nThe voice on the other end hesitated. “Your son told people he don’t work for you.”\n\n“This ain’t about who he works for.”\n\n“Then what is it about?”\n\nReem looked toward the wooden cross at the front of the chapel.\n\n“My daughter.”\n\nWithin minutes, orders moved from the county jail to Blackwater State Prison, then through halfway houses and street contacts across western Florida.\n\nReem had spent years building a network nobody could see from outside the walls.\n\nHe activated all of it.\n\nCell’s incarcerated brother was pulled from his unit.\n\nTwo Canal Boy captains awaiting trial lost access to their phones.\n\nA former correctional officer supplied the license plate used in King K’s escape.\n\nIt belonged to the Palm Haven Renewal Foundation.\n\nReem stared at the name.\n\nFor the first time that night, he looked afraid.\n\n“Saint,” he whispered.\n\n---\n\nDetective Holloway watched Captain Vance leave police headquarters through a rear entrance.\n\nInternal Affairs had detained three jump-out officers. A fourth had disappeared. Vance claimed he knew nothing about their unauthorized operation at The Sunshine.\n\nHolloway did not believe him.\n\nShe followed his unmarked sedan across downtown, past Government Square and into the private underground garage of the Palm Haven Renewal Center.\n\nThe center occupied twelve floors of mirrored glass beside City Hall. Its founder appeared on charity billboards throughout the city.\n\nCouncilman Gabriel Saint.\n\nSaint had built youth centers, funded violence-prevention programs and publicly demanded tougher prosecution of Palm Haven gangs. News stations called him the conscience of the city.\n\nHolloway parked across the street.\n\nVance entered through a restricted elevator.\n\nThree minutes later, Cell’s black Bentley entered the same garage.\n\nThen La Madrina’s security chief, Stone.\n\nHolloway photographed every vehicle.\n\nThe alliances made no sense.\n\nUnless the war itself was the business.\n\n---\n\nReck and Mari moved along the motel’s rear fence while Zay, Naz and TJ crossed behind the empty swimming pool.\n\nRoom 214 exploded before they reached the laundry wing.\n\nFlames tore through the second-floor railing. Glass fell across the parking lot. Mon’s people opened fire from two dark rooms as police sirens began approaching from both ends of Paradise Boulevard.\n\n“They called police before we got here,” Zay shouted.\n\n“King’s old trick,” Naz said.\n\nThe motel became a trap closing from every direction.\n\nReck kept moving.\n\nHe found Building Twelve behind a chained maintenance gate. Mari broke the lock with a tire iron. Inside, industrial washers sat beneath layers of dust.\n\nAmaya’s scream came from the room beyond them.\n\nReck reached the door first.\n\nMon stepped from behind it and struck him across the face.\n\nReck crashed into a dryer.\n\n“Been waiting on this,” Mon said.\n\nMari rushed him, but two men emerged from the shadows. One grabbed Mari’s injured shoulder and drove him against the wall. The other blocked the hallway.\n\nReck saw Amaya tied to a chair inside the utility room.\n\nKing K stood beside her in his county uniform.\n\nHe was holding a pistol and smiling.\n\n“Look at the new king,” King said. “Still running wherever family tells him.”\n\nReck wiped blood from his mouth.\n\n“Let her go.”\n\n“Take off my watch.”\n\nReck stared at him.\n\n“You heard me.”\n\nReck slowly removed the diamond watch.\n\nKing pointed toward the floor.\n\n“Put it down.”\n\nThe watch struck the concrete between them.\n\nKing’s smile widened.\n\n“Now the chain.”\n\nReck reached for the cracked crown.\n\nAmaya shook her head.\n\n“Don’t.”\n\nKing pressed the gun against her temple.\n\n“She got more pride than sense. Must run in the family.”\n\nReck unclasped the pendant.\n\nBefore he lowered it, every light in Building Twelve went out.\n\nTJ had found the electrical panel.\n\nDarkness swallowed the room.\n\nZay crashed through a side door.\n\nNaz pulled Amaya’s chair backward as Reck drove into King K. The gun fired into the ceiling. Mari broke free and fought Mon across the laundry room.\n\nThe room erupted with shouting, metal and gunfire.\n\nThen Amaya screamed Reck’s name.\n\nEmergency lights flashed on.\n\nZay stood between her and King K.\n\nBlood spread across the royal-blue shirt beneath his jacket.\n\nHe collapsed.\n\n“Zay!”\n\nReck caught him before his head struck the floor.\n\nKing K escaped through the side door. Mon followed, wounded but moving. Their last man disappeared into the rain as police vehicles flooded the motel entrance.\n\nMari lifted Amaya while Naz pressed both hands against Zay’s chest.\n\n“Stay with us,” Naz ordered.\n\nZay’s eyes struggled to focus.\n\n“She good?”\n\nAmaya dropped beside him, her wrists still tied.\n\n“I’m here. You saved me.”\n\nZay looked toward Reck.\n\n“Get them home.”\n\nReck grabbed his hand.\n\n“We all going home.”\n\n---\n\nThe Sunshine came under attack four minutes after Zay was shot.\n\nMook opened the side gate for what he believed was a Mar Azul vehicle.\n\nTide stepped out first.\n\nThe Canal Boyz flooded the garage before Stone’s people realized the security call had been falsified. They took cash, files and the remaining product. Then they set fire to the new office Reck had finished less than twelve hours earlier.\n\nMook stood in the parking lot as flames climbed the walls.\n\nTide handed him an envelope.\n\n“You did right.”\n\nMook looked sick.\n\n“You said nobody was getting hurt.”\n\n“Nobody important did.”\n\nOne of the young construction workers crawled from the garage with burns across his arms.\n\nMook dropped the envelope.\n\nTide grabbed him by the collar.\n\n“Too late to grow a conscience.”\n\nA black SUV approached from Garden Courts.\n\nResidents filled the street behind it.\n\nTide released Mook and ordered the Canal Boyz back into their vehicles. They escaped through the same side gate Mook had opened.\n\nBy the time firefighters arrived, The Sunshine was burning again.\n\nThis time, the neighborhood knew who let the enemy inside.\n\n---\n\nLa Madrina entered Gabriel Saint’s private office just after one in the morning.\n\nCaptain Vance stood near the window. Cell sat on a leather sofa with one ankle resting over his knee.\n\nGabriel Saint faced the city from behind his desk.\n\nHe was sixty-one, silver-haired and dressed in a charcoal suit. A gold shepherd’s staff rested on the bookshelf behind him—the emblem of his foundation.\n\n“Your young friend embarrassed everyone tonight,” Saint said.\n\nLa Madrina remained standing. “Reck rescued his sister.”\n\n“And lost his headquarters.”\n\nCell smiled. “He’ll come back angry. Angry people make easy cases.”\n\nSaint turned.\n\n“Reem was easier.”\n\nLa Madrina’s eyes sharpened.\n\n“Reem understood sacrifice.”\n\n“Reem believed you would leave his family alone,” she said.\n\nSaint looked toward Stone, who stood silently beside the door.\n\n“And I did. Until his son decided to inherit a kingdom that was never his.”\n\nLa Madrina stepped closer to the desk.\n\n“You ordered Amaya taken?”\n\n“I allowed King K to solve his problem.”\n\n“That wasn’t my question.”\n\nVance shifted his hand toward his jacket.\n\nStone moved between him and La Madrina.\n\nThe room became still.\n\nSaint smiled.\n\n“Choose carefully, Camila. Reem’s son is temporary. The system that protects your ports is not.”\n\nLa Madrina looked at Cell.\n\n“You brought Saint into our business.”\n\nCell laughed. “Saint was the business before either of us had one.”\n\nThat was when she understood.\n\nCell had not created the Canal Boyz on his own.\n\nKing K had not escaped because of a few corrupt officers.\n\nThe war was clearing territory for the Renewal Foundation’s billion-dollar waterfront development. Every burned building lowered property values. Every arrest removed resistance. Every seized stash became money for Vance’s unit. Every frightened family became easier to relocate.\n\nSaint profited no matter who won.\n\nHe only needed them to keep fighting.\n\nLa Madrina turned toward the door.\n\n“Where are you going?” Saint asked.\n\n“To choose.”\n\n---\n\nDetective Holloway intercepted the Ridge Boyz behind the motel.\n\nZay lay across the back seat, barely conscious. Amaya held pressure against his wound while TJ shook beside her. Police cruisers blocked Paradise Boulevard.\n\nHolloway pointed toward an unmarked vehicle parked beside the service road.\n\n“Put him in mine.”\n\nReck hesitated.\n\n“If I wanted you arrested, tactical would already be back here.”\n\nThey transferred Zay.\n\nHolloway drove them through a maintenance exit before the road closed. She called ahead to a private trauma center and ordered the surgeon not to register Zay under his real name.\n\n“Why you helping us?” Reck asked.\n\n“I’m helping the boy who got shot protecting your sister.”\n\n“That ain’t all.”\n\nHolloway looked at him through the mirror.\n\n“Captain Vance met Cell tonight at Gabriel Saint’s building.”\n\nNaz leaned forward. “Councilman Saint?”\n\n“You know him?”\n\nAmaya stopped pressing for half a second.\n\n“Our father used to call him Uncle Gabe.”\n\nReck looked at her.\n\n“What?”\n\n“When we were little. Before Daddy went to prison.”\n\nHolloway’s phone rang.\n\nReem’s name appeared on the private line.\n\nShe answered on speaker.\n\n“Is my daughter alive?” he asked.\n\n“She’s here,” Holloway said.\n\nAmaya leaned toward the phone. “Daddy?”\n\nReem exhaled.\n\nReck took the phone.\n\n“Zay got shot. King got away. The Sunshine got hit. All while you sitting there talking about your reach.”\n\n“Listen to me. King’s escape car belonged to Gabriel Saint.”\n\n“Amaya says we called him uncle.”\n\nSilence answered.\n\n“Tell me who he is.”\n\nReem’s voice lowered.\n\n“Gabriel Saint built the first Palm Haven organization. Me, King K and Cell—we weren’t partners. We worked beneath him.”\n\nReck looked toward Naz.\n\n“You said they betrayed you.”\n\n“They did.”\n\n“You said they put you in prison.”\n\n“That part wasn’t true.”\n\nReck’s grip tightened around the phone.\n\nReem continued.\n\n“The federal case reached Saint. He controlled city contracts, police promotions, judges and the port. If his name surfaced, everybody’s families became targets—including yours.”\n\n“So what you did?”\n\n“I accepted the sentence. I gave them a boss and Saint stayed invisible.”\n\nAmaya stared at the phone.\n\n“You left us to protect him?”\n\n“I left to protect all of you from him.”\n\nReck’s anger became something colder.\n\n“And now he controlling the war.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Why?”\n\nNaz answered before Reem could.\n\nHe had opened the Renewal Foundation’s public development map on TJ’s tablet.\n\nThe proposed waterfront corridor covered Harbor View, Paradise Boulevard and Sunridge Gardens.\n\nThe Sunshine sat at its center.\n\n“It ain’t only about drugs,” Naz said. “He wants the land.”\n\nReem heard him.\n\n“Saint lets crews fight, lets police raid what survives, then buys everything through shell companies. King K feeds him names. Cell moves his product. Vance clears the streets. Saint stays clean.”\n\nReck looked through the windshield as Palm Haven’s towers appeared in the distance.\n\nCouncilman Gabriel Saint’s face smiled from a highway billboard.\n\n**RENEWING PALM HAVEN. PROTECTING OUR FUTURE.**\n\nReck ended the call without saying goodbye.\n\n---\n\nZay entered surgery at 2:17 a.m.\n\nThe doctors gave him a chance.\n\nNo promises.\n\nAmaya sat beside TJ in the waiting room. Mari paced with dried blood across his orange jacket. Naz stood near the window, tracking the fire at The Sunshine through neighborhood messages.\n\nReck remained alone in the hallway.\n\nLa Madrina approached without Stone.\n\nReck saw her and stood.\n\n“If you came to speak for my father, leave.”\n\n“I came to tell you I chose.”\n\nShe handed him a black folder.\n\nInside were property records, police payments, port manifests and photographs of Saint meeting King K, Cell and Captain Vance across twelve years.\n\n“Saint ordered the war to clear the waterfront,” she said. “Reem protected him once. I will not.”\n\nReck studied the evidence.\n\n“Why should I trust you?”\n\n“You shouldn’t. Trust what can be proven.”\n\nMari stopped pacing.\n\nNaz moved beside Reck and examined the first page.\n\nLa Madrina looked through the glass at Zay’s operating room.\n\n“Cell believes tonight weakened you.”\n\nReck closed the folder.\n\n“He wrong.”\n\nHis phone displayed the first images from Sunridge.\n\nThe Sunshine’s office was destroyed.\n\nMook had been caught on camera opening the gate.\n\nResidents wanted Reck to decide his fate.\n\nKing K remained free.\n\nMon was wounded.\n\nTide had taken the stash.\n\nReem had revealed the truth.\n\nAnd Gabriel Saint—the celebrated man promising to save Palm Haven—had been feeding the war from above them all.\n\nReck looked at the four founders still standing and the door behind which Zay fought for his life.\n\n“Nobody moves on Mook until Zay wakes up,” he said. “Founders decide together.”\n\nMari nodded reluctantly.\n\nReck handed the black folder to Naz.\n\n“Make copies. Hide them everywhere.”\n\nTJ rose from beside Amaya. “What we doing about Saint?”\n\nReck stared at the councilman’s smiling face on the folder.\n\n“King K wanted The Ridge.”\n\nHe turned toward the hospital window, where the first gray light of morning touched Palm Haven.\n\n“Cell wanted the water.”\n\nHe fastened the cracked crown around his neck.\n\n“Saint wants the whole city.”\n\nThe surgery light remained red.\n\nReck’s voice dropped.\n\n“So now we taking the war upstairs.”"},{"number":12,"title":"Chapter Twelve — D-Low’s Son","body":"Khaos walked out of juvenile detention carrying everything he owned inside a clear plastic bag.\n\nTwo black T-shirts.\n\nA paperback with half the pages folded.\n\nThree letters from his mother.\n\nOne photograph of D-Low standing beside a burnt-orange Chevrolet outside Paradise Haven.\n\nNobody had told him his father was dead until six days after the siege.\n\nBy then, the story had already changed depending on who told it.\n\nSome said D-Low died protecting Reck.\n\nSome said Reck used him as a shield.\n\nOthers said King K arranged everything and Cell allowed it.\n\nKhaos believed none of them completely.\n\nThe detention gate closed behind him.\n\nThree vehicles waited across the road.\n\nT-Rell leaned against the first. Kilo sat on its hood. Lil Smoke stood beside a gray sedan wearing a faded Haven Boy shirt beneath his jacket.\n\nOnly three.\n\nThere had been fourteen Haven Boyz when Khaos went away.\n\nHe crossed the road slowly.\n\n“Where everybody at?”\n\nT-Rell hugged him before answering.\n\n“Dreko and Six still locked. Quan got twenty years. Baby Ant and Jugg gone.”\n\nKhaos’s face remained still.\n\n“Rambo?”\n\n“Waiting at Paradise Haven.”\n\n“Deuce?”\n\nNobody answered.\n\nKhaos looked from one face to another.\n\n“I asked where Deuce at.”\n\nKilo lowered his eyes. “Canal Boyz.”\n\nThe plastic bag tightened inside Khaos’s fist.\n\n“He switched?”\n\n“Tide gave him our old spot,” T-Rell said. “Cell gave them the connection. They been using the HB name like we dead.”\n\nKhaos opened the photograph of his father.\n\n“Take me to D-Low.”\n\nThe silence returned.\n\nT-Rell nodded toward Sunridge.\n\n“We got to take you to Reck.”\n\nKhaos placed the photograph back inside the bag.\n\n“I ain’t ask for Reck.”\n\n“Your father’s grave is in Sunridge.”\n\nKhaos stared at him.\n\nThen he climbed into the car.\n\n---\n\nZay survived surgery.\n\nThe bullet had missed his heart by less than an inch. Doctors placed him under heavy sedation and warned that the next forty-eight hours would decide whether complications finished what King K started.\n\nReck sat beside the hospital bed while the morning news praised Councilman Gabriel Saint for calling an emergency summit against gang violence.\n\nSaint appeared behind a podium wearing a navy suit and a concerned expression.\n\n“Palm Haven’s children deserve neighborhoods free from fear,” he told the cameras. “We will rebuild every community damaged by these criminals.”\n\nBehind him was a rendering of the new waterfront corridor.\n\nThe proposed park sat where The Sunshine currently stood.\n\nReck muted the television.\n\nZay’s eyes remained closed.\n\n“You was right,” Reck said quietly. “Everybody who ain’t with us ain’t the enemy.”\n\nHe looked at the machines keeping rhythm beside the bed.\n\n“But Saint is.”\n\nThe door opened.\n\nNaz entered first.\n\nHis expression warned Reck before he spoke.\n\n“D-Low’s son home.”\n\nReck stood.\n\n“Khaos?”\n\n“He waiting at the grave.”\n\n“Who brought him?”\n\n“What’s left of the Haven Boyz.”\n\nMari appeared behind Naz with his orange jacket zipped over his injured shoulder.\n\n“He got questions.”\n\nReck looked back at Zay.\n\n“Then he deserves answers.”\n\n---\n\nD-Low’s grave was covered with fresh flowers and old rain.\n\nKhaos stood alone in front of it wearing the same black clothes issued when he left detention. He was seventeen, lean and broad-shouldered, with short freeform twists and eyes that looked older than the rest of his face.\n\nT-Rell, Rambo, Kilo and Lil Smoke waited beneath a nearby tree.\n\nThe Ridge Boyz approached from the opposite path.\n\nReck came first.\n\nMari and Naz followed. TJ remained at the hospital with Zay and Amaya.\n\nKhaos did not turn around.\n\n“My daddy saved your life?”\n\nReck stopped several feet behind him.\n\n“Yeah.”\n\n“After he told police the bag was yours?”\n\n“Yeah.”\n\nKhaos finally faced him.\n\n“So which D-Low am I supposed to remember?”\n\nReck took King K’s cooperation agreement from inside his jacket and held it out.\n\n“All of him.”\n\nKhaos did not accept the paper.\n\nReck continued.\n\n“He used me for a run without telling me everything. When it went bad, he put it on me. King K had both of us set up. Your father found out, came back and tried fixing it.”\n\n“Tried?”\n\n“He stepped in front of what was meant for me.”\n\nThe muscles in Khaos’s jaw moved.\n\n“You leave him there?”\n\nMari stepped forward. “Watch how you—”\n\nReck raised one hand.\n\n“No. He get to ask.”\n\nMari stopped.\n\nReck looked directly at his cousin.\n\n“I held him while he died.”\n\nKhaos’s eyes filled but no tears fell.\n\n“He say anything?”\n\n“He told me not to trust Cell.”\n\n“Anything about me?”\n\nReck’s silence answered first.\n\n“He didn’t have time.”\n\nKhaos looked back at the grave.\n\n“Everybody had time for their war. Nobody had time to tell me my daddy was gone.”\n\n“You right.”\n\n“You and him had problems before I got locked.”\n\n“We did.”\n\n“Then me and you got one.”\n\nKhaos removed his jacket and dropped it beside the headstone.\n\nRambo moved closer.\n\nMari did the same.\n\nKhaos pointed between the two groups.\n\n“Nobody jumps in. No weapons. Me and my cousin settle everything my daddy left unfinished. After that, we decide whether we family.”\n\nReck removed his cracked-crown chain and handed it to Naz.\n\n“Let’s settle it.”\n\n---\n\nKhaos hit harder than Reck expected.\n\nHis first punch caught Reck across the mouth. His second drove him backward through wet grass. Khaos fought with the anger of somebody who had rehearsed the moment inside a locked room for months.\n\nReck recovered and struck back.\n\nNeither crew interfered.\n\nThey fought beside D-Low’s grave until both cousins were breathing hard and bleeding. Reck was stronger. Khaos was quicker. Every time Reck knocked him down, Khaos rose again.\n\nAfter the fourth fall, Reck stepped back.\n\n“Enough.”\n\nKhaos pushed himself onto one knee.\n\n“I say when it’s enough.”\n\n“Then get up.”\n\nKhaos tried.\n\nHis legs failed.\n\nReck offered his hand.\n\nFor several seconds, Khaos stared at it.\n\nThen he accepted.\n\nReck pulled him to his feet.\n\n“Everything between me and your father died when he saved my life,” Reck said. “I’ll never lie about what he did wrong. I’ll never disrespect what he did right.”\n\nKhaos wiped blood from his lip.\n\n“Anybody responsible for him dying got a problem with me.”\n\n“They got a problem with us.”\n\nThe cousins embraced beside the grave.\n\nThe Ridge Boyz and Haven Boyz moved together for the first time.\n\n---\n\nReck took Khaos to D-Low’s old garage that afternoon.\n\nThe doors had remained locked since the funeral. Inside sat a black Durango, toolboxes covered in dust and a burnt-orange Chevrolet matching the one in Khaos’s photograph.\n\nOn the workbench rested D-Low’s repaired pendant.\n\nBeside it were two sealed packages from La Madrina’s supply and a case containing enough money to restart an organization.\n\nKhaos stared at everything.\n\n“What’s this?”\n\n“Your father’s chance to make it right,” Reck said. “And mine.”\n\n“You giving me all this?”\n\n“I’m giving you something to stand on. What it becomes is on you.”\n\nKhaos touched D-Low’s pendant.\n\n“You trying to buy my loyalty?”\n\n“If your loyalty got a price, I don’t want it.”\n\nReck opened the case.\n\n“This is for D-Low. Use it to take care of your people and rebuild what was yours. But money makes careless people feel powerful. Don’t let it own you.”\n\nKhaos lifted his father’s pendant and placed it beside the burning-HB chain T-Rell had saved during his incarceration.\n\n“Haven Boyz stay Haven Boyz.”\n\n“Ridge Boyz stay Ridge Boyz.”\n\n“Nobody over nobody.”\n\nReck extended his hand.\n\n“Family beside family.”\n\nKhaos shook it.\n\n“Then let’s get our territory back.”\n\n---\n\nThe old Haven spot stood behind a convenience store near the Royal Palms Motel.\n\nDeuce had replaced the burnt-orange HB halo with Tide’s silver canal emblem. Teal vehicles filled the lot. Music shook the windows as Canal Boyz counted money inside the building Khaos had controlled before detention.\n\nKhaos arrived in D-Low’s Chevrolet.\n\nThe surviving Haven Boyz followed.\n\nReck, Mari and Naz remained across the street. This was Khaos’s territory to reclaim.\n\nDeuce stepped outside wearing teal.\n\nHe looked at Khaos and laughed nervously.\n\n“They let anybody out now.”\n\nKhaos looked past him at the building.\n\n“Take that emblem down.”\n\n“HB died when you got sentenced.”\n\nKhaos’s eyes moved toward Deuce’s chain.\n\nThe pendant hanging from it belonged to Baby Ant, one of the Haven Boyz killed while Khaos was away.\n\n“Where you get that?”\n\nDeuce covered it with one hand.\n\nRambo moved forward.\n\nSeveral Canal Boyz emerged behind Deuce.\n\nKhaos raised his voice so everyone nearby could hear.\n\n“Anybody who left because they wanted another life can stay gone. I ain’t hunting people for choosing peace. But anybody who switched, helped our enemies and stole from our dead chose something else.”\n\nHe pointed toward the building.\n\n“That belongs to Haven.”\n\nDeuce reached beneath his shirt.\n\nT-Rell struck first.\n\nThe confrontation exploded across the parking lot. Windows shattered. Vehicles collided against concrete barriers. Residents fled into nearby stores as the Haven Boyz overwhelmed the smaller Canal crew and forced them from the property.\n\nDeuce escaped through the rear alley.\n\nKhaos let him run.\n\n“He going to Tide,” Rambo said.\n\n“Good,” Khaos replied. “Tell him who took it.”\n\nHe pulled the silver Canal Boy emblem from the wall.\n\nLil Smoke carried the old burning-HB halo from the Chevrolet’s trunk.\n\nTogether, they placed it above the entrance.\n\nAcross the street, Reck watched Paradise Haven recognize its returning leader.\n\nNaz looked toward the Royal Palms Motel.\n\n“Saint won’t let them keep this.”\n\nReck nodded.\n\n“They don’t need his permission.”\n\n---\n\nInside Palm Haven County Jail, Darius “Duke” Coleman received the news through a folded message hidden beneath his food tray.\n\n**KHAOS HOME. HB WITH RB.**\n\nDuke read it once and destroyed it.\n\nHe was nineteen, stocky and quiet, with short twists and a cracked-crown tattoo across his forearm. Reck had planned to make him the first Ridge captain before an arrest placed him inside the county jail.\n\nHe had refused every offer to cooperate.\n\nAcross the housing unit, DeAndre “Dreko” Price sat with three incarcerated Haven Boyz.\n\nDuke approached alone.\n\nDreko stood.\n\nThe entire unit became quiet.\n\n“Your leader standing beside mine now,” Duke said.\n\n“That mean we cool?” Dreko asked.\n\n“It means Ridge and Haven stand together on both sides of these walls.”\n\nDreko studied him.\n\nThen they shook hands.\n\nThe alliance moved through the jail before the next count.\n\nRidge members shared information with Haven.\n\nHaven members warned Duke which officers worked for Captain Vance.\n\nBoth groups agreed that uninvolved inmates remained outside the conflict. But anyone verified as part of an attack against Ridge or Haven would no longer find safety simply because police placed them behind a locked door.\n\nDuke made the boundary clear.\n\n“We check every name. We don’t move off rumors. But if somebody helped burn our headquarters, kidnap our family or set our people up, custody don’t erase it.”\n\nDreko nodded.\n\n“What about Reem?”\n\n“We respect Reck’s father.”\n\n“He run us?”\n\nDuke looked toward the camera above the unit door.\n\n“Reem controls his generation. Reck and Khaos control ours.”\n\n---\n\nThe alliance received its first test that evening.\n\nOfficers brought Corey “Splash” Davis into the housing unit after his arrest in a Canal Boy vehicle.\n\nSplash walked through the door expecting Cell’s name to protect him.\n\nInstead, conversations stopped on both sides of the room.\n\nDuke stepped from the Ridge table.\n\nDreko rose from the Haven table.\n\nSplash looked between them.\n\n“This ain’t got nothing to do with y’all in here.”\n\n“You helped burn The Sunshine,” Duke said.\n\nDreko moved beside him. “Your crew took Haven territory while Khaos was locked.”\n\nSplash backed toward the door.\n\n“That was Tide.”\n\n“You wear his emblem,” Duke replied.\n\nBefore either side moved closer, four officers rushed into the unit.\n\nThey removed Splash immediately.\n\nNo disciplinary order had been announced.\n\nNo protective-custody request had been filed.\n\nThe transfer came directly from the jail administrator’s office.\n\nDuke watched the door close.\n\n“Saint,” he said.\n\nDreko looked at him. “How you know?”\n\n“Because regular people don’t get rescued that fast.”\n\nDuke found the nearest trusted messenger.\n\n“Tell Reck Saint controls movement inside the jail too. Somebody protecting Canal leadership before we can learn what they know.”\n\nThe message left the unit before Splash reached protective custody.\n\n---\n\nKhaos spent his first night home rebuilding.\n\nD-Low’s surviving connections arrived one at a time.\n\nAn older club owner brought account books D-Low had hidden.\n\nA trucking-company operator returned keys to a vehicle King K never discovered.\n\nA lawyer delivered money held in trust for Khaos since before his arrest.\n\nThe final visitor carried a phone sealed inside a plastic evidence pouch.\n\n“Your father recorded something,” the man said. “Told me you were the only one allowed to hear it.”\n\nKhaos pressed play.\n\nD-Low’s voice filled the garage.\n\n“If something happen to me, King K ordered it. Cell knew enough to stop it. Don’t let my son inherit my mistakes.”\n\nStatic interrupted the recording.\n\nThen D-Low continued.\n\n“Khaos, the people calling themselves your friends might be the same ones opening doors for your enemies. Watch the ones who got richer while you was gone.”\n\nThe recording ended.\n\nKhaos looked toward T-Rell, Kilo, Rambo and Lil Smoke.\n\nNobody spoke.\n\nReck entered the garage with Duke’s message from the jail.\n\nKhaos handed him the phone.\n\nReck listened to his cousin’s father accuse both King K and Cell.\n\n“We got proof now,” Khaos said.\n\n“We got his word.”\n\n“That’s proof to me.”\n\n“Saint bigger than both of them.”\n\nKhaos looked around the garage his father left behind.\n\n“Then we get bigger too.”\n\nHe walked outside.\n\nNearly thirty people waited beneath the restored Haven Boy emblem. Some were old members. Others were neighborhood residents who believed HB had died. A few were young men seeking the protection of a name that suddenly mattered again.\n\nKhaos stood on the hood of D-Low’s Chevrolet.\n\n“Everybody thought I was finished because a judge sent me away,” he said. “My people died, got sentenced or switched. They took our territory. Then they killed my father and expected me to come home broken.”\n\nHe lifted D-Low’s pendant.\n\n“They got the wrong one.”\n\nReck and the Ridge Boyz stood beside the building.\n\n“If you stayed loyal, you eat. If you left peacefully, stay gone. But if you switched sides and helped our enemies take from Haven, we coming to collect what belongs to us.”\n\nThe crowd grew louder.\n\nKhaos pointed toward Sunridge.\n\n“Reck got The Ridge. I got Paradise Haven. Nobody over nobody. Family beside family.”\n\nReck raised the cracked crown.\n\nKhaos raised the burning halo.\n\n“Whoever got a problem with my cousin got a problem with me,” Khaos said. “Whoever helped take my father got a problem with both organizations.”\n\nAcross Palm Haven County Jail, Duke received confirmation that the alliance was official.\n\nHe stood beside Dreko beneath the unit lights.\n\nOutside, Reck and Khaos faced two connected neighborhoods preparing for war.\n\nInside, Ridge and Haven members controlled the same jail tier.\n\nCell had the Canal Boyz.\n\nSaint had the police and politicians.\n\nKing K had protection.\n\nBut D-Low’s son was home.\n\nThe Haven Boyz were alive again.\n\nAnd the Crown War now reached both sides of the walls."},{"number":13,"title":"Chapter Thirteen — Two Crowns","body":"Zay opened his eyes to an argument.\n\nReck stood near the hospital window with Mari. Naz occupied the chair beside the door, reviewing photographs from Gabriel Saint’s black folder. TJ slept with his head against the wall.\n\nAmaya was the first to notice.\n\n“Zay?”\n\nEvery voice stopped.\n\nReck reached the bed before the others.\n\nZay tried to speak. Pain tightened his face before sound came.\n\n“Y’all loud.”\n\nMari laughed and covered his mouth with both hands.\n\nTJ woke instantly.\n\n“You alive.”\n\nZay looked at him. “That usually how waking up works.”\n\nRelief moved through the room in different forms.\n\nTJ cried without hiding it.\n\nMari turned away and stared at the ceiling.\n\nNaz closed the folder.\n\nReck held Zay’s hand.\n\n“You saved my sister.”\n\nZay’s eyes shifted toward Amaya.\n\n“She safe?”\n\n“Because of you,” Amaya said.\n\nZay looked back at Reck. “King?”\n\n“Gone.”\n\n“Mon?”\n\n“Gone.”\n\n“The Sunshine?”\n\nNobody answered quickly enough.\n\nZay understood.\n\n“Again?”\n\nReck nodded.\n\nZay closed his eyes.\n\n“Saint wanted that.”\n\nNaz leaned forward. “Wanted what?”\n\n“Us angry. Running everywhere. Leaving home open.”\n\nReck’s grip tightened.\n\n“They shot you.”\n\n“And if you answer without thinking, they get to shoot everybody else too.”\n\nReck released his hand and stepped back.\n\n“I ain’t letting it go.”\n\n“I didn’t say let it go.” Zay forced his eyes open again. “I said win.”\n\nThe room became silent.\n\nZay looked at all four founders.\n\n“Saint needs us acting like the monsters he puts on television. Don’t give him the picture he wants.”\n\nReck stared through the window at Palm Haven.\n\n“Khaos ready to move.”\n\n“Then make sure both crowns got a plan.”\n\n---\n\nMook waited inside D-Low’s garage with his hands resting on the table.\n\nHe had been brought there before sunrise by Mari and Rambo. Nobody struck him. Nobody explained what would happen.\n\nThe security video played on a laptop in front of him.\n\nMook opened The Sunshine’s side gate.\n\nTide entered.\n\nThe Canal Boyz burned the office.\n\nThe video restarted.\n\nKhaos stood against the tool cabinets with D-Low’s pendant beside his burning halo. Reck sat across from Mook. Mari, Naz and TJ represented the founding Ridge Boyz in person.\n\nZay appeared through a secure hospital call.\n\nAll five founders would decide.\n\n“Tell it again,” Reck said.\n\nMook looked exhausted.\n\n“Deuce called me. Said a Mar Azul vehicle was bringing replacement supplies. He knew the gate code, the security phrase, everything.”\n\nNaz placed a photograph of Deuce on the table.\n\n“You never verified with Stone.”\n\n“I thought we had to move fast.”\n\nMari leaned forward. “You thought wrong and almost got people killed.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“You took money?” Khaos asked.\n\n“Tide gave me an envelope after. I dropped it.”\n\n“After you saw the building burning.”\n\nMook lowered his head.\n\nReck looked toward the phone screen.\n\n“Zay?”\n\nZay’s voice was weak but clear.\n\n“He didn’t plan the attack. He still opened the gate without checking.”\n\n“Mari?”\n\n“Put him out The Ridge.”\n\n“TJ?”\n\nTJ studied Mook. “He can’t know anything important again.”\n\n“Naz?”\n\n“Remove him from the organization. Make him repay the construction worker’s medical costs and every family affected by the fire.”\n\nReck looked at Mook.\n\n“You wanted the crown before you earned it. Now you never will.”\n\nMook’s eyes rose.\n\n“I can fix this.”\n\n“You can repay what you damaged. You can tell us everything Deuce said. But you ain’t Ridge.”\n\nMari’s expression showed he wanted more.\n\nReck continued.\n\n“If we punish every mistake like betrayal, Saint won’t need to destroy us. We’ll do it ourselves.”\n\nZay heard him through the phone.\n\nFor the first time since waking, he smiled.\n\nMook was escorted from the garage without his colors.\n\nKhaos watched the door close.\n\n“You let people live with consequences.”\n\nReck looked at him. “When the truth says it was a mistake.”\n\n“And when it says betrayal?”\n\n“That’s different.”\n\nNeither cousin knew the answer would matter before the night ended.\n\n---\n\nKilo Warren arrived at D-Low’s garage in a new black BMW.\n\nThe car was too expensive for somebody who claimed he had barely survived while Khaos was incarcerated.\n\nKhaos noticed immediately.\n\n“That yours?”\n\nKilo smiled. “Business been good.”\n\n“What business?”\n\n“Moving around. Keeping money coming in after HB fell apart.”\n\nKhaos walked around the car.\n\nFresh paper tags.\n\nCustom wheels.\n\nA watch on Kilo’s wrist worth more than everything Lil Smoke owned.\n\n“You said Tide took all our spots.”\n\n“He did.”\n\n“You said Cell cut off every old connection.”\n\nKilo’s smile weakened.\n\n“People find ways to eat.”\n\nKhaos looked through the windshield and saw a parking receipt from Saint’s Renewal Center.\n\nHe removed it before Kilo noticed.\n\n“Where you was last night?”\n\n“Home.”\n\n“Anybody confirm that?”\n\nKilo laughed. “You interrogating your own people your first day back?”\n\n“I’m learning who my people are.”\n\nT-Rell called Khaos from inside the garage.\n\nBy the time Khaos turned back, Kilo had covered his watch with his sleeve.\n\n---\n\nCouncilman Gabriel Saint convened the Palm Haven Emergency Safety Summit at noon.\n\nTelevision cameras filled the Renewal Center’s auditorium. Police commanders, property developers, clergy and neighborhood representatives occupied the stage.\n\nSaint stood beneath a giant rendering of the waterfront corridor.\n\n“Violence has stolen opportunity from Sunridge and Harbor View for too long,” he announced. “Our renewal plan will replace abandoned properties with housing, parks and businesses.”\n\nThe rendering showed luxury towers where Garden Courts currently stood.\n\nNobody mentioned where existing families would go.\n\nOutside, Naz sat inside a delivery van with La Madrina and a financial investigator named Elena Ruiz.\n\nElena opened Saint’s property records across three screens.\n\n“The Renewal Foundation doesn’t own the land directly,” she explained. “Forty-three shell companies do.”\n\nNaz followed the payments.\n\n“They started buying after Reem went to prison.”\n\n“Then accelerated every time violence increased,” Elena said.\n\nLa Madrina looked at the auditorium entrance. “Saint creates the crisis, purchases the damage and receives public money to repair it.”\n\nNaz found a cluster of recent transfers.\n\nOne company had purchased the Royal Palms Motel forty-eight hours before Amaya’s abduction.\n\nAnother owned the warehouse Cell gave Tide.\n\nA third paid a consulting company registered to Captain Vance’s wife.\n\n“Got him,” Naz said.\n\nElena shook her head. “You have a pattern. Not a case.”\n\n“Then we find the person moving the money.”\n\nNaz opened the Renewal Center’s visitor records obtained from the parking system.\n\nOne name appeared repeatedly.\n\nKendrick Warren.\n\nKilo.\n\n---\n\nReem entered the county jail unit surrounded by six older inmates.\n\nHe had arranged a temporary medical transfer from state prison through a doctor Saint once controlled. Reem used the same hidden system that freed King K, but he used it to reach Duke.\n\nThe unit fell silent when he arrived.\n\nDuke and Dreko stood side by side.\n\n“Ridge on the left,” Reem ordered. “Haven on the right.”\n\nNobody moved.\n\nReem looked at Duke.\n\n“You hear me?”\n\n“We heard.”\n\n“Then organize your people.”\n\nDuke folded his arms.\n\n“They already organized.”\n\nReem stepped closer. “My son’s crew exists because of my name.”\n\n“Reck says it exists because of five founders.”\n\nThe older inmates shifted.\n\nDreko moved beside Duke.\n\n“Khaos says Haven stays Haven.”\n\nReem looked between them.\n\n“You children think independence means disrespect.”\n\n“No,” Duke said. “It means you don’t command us without Reck.”\n\nFor several seconds, father and son’s representative stared at each other.\n\nThen Reem smiled.\n\n“Good.”\n\nDuke’s expression did not change.\n\n“Good?”\n\n“If you folded because I walked in with six men, you weren’t fit to represent my son.”\n\nReem handed him a folded transfer roster.\n\nSplash Davis would be moved through the medical corridor at 6:40 that evening. The jail administrator, Milton Graves, had personally authorized the protected route.\n\n“Don’t touch Splash,” Reem said.\n\nDreko frowned. “Why not?”\n\n“Because scared people talk when they think they’re escaping.”\n\nReem pointed to a name on the roster.\n\nAdministrator Graves would escort him.\n\n“Get proof Graves works for Saint. Information hurts this man more than blood.”\n\nDuke accepted the paper.\n\n“That an order?”\n\nReem turned away.\n\n“Advice from somebody who survived long enough to learn the difference.”\n\n---\n\nAt 6:40, Splash entered the medical corridor wearing restraints.\n\nAdministrator Graves walked beside him carrying transfer papers. A hidden phone inside Graves’s jacket connected him to Captain Vance.\n\nDuke’s people did not attack.\n\nDreko’s people did not block the corridor.\n\nInstead, a kitchen worker loyal to Haven activated the corridor camera Saint’s officers believed had been disabled. Another inmate captured the conversation through a service vent.\n\nSplash whispered, “Tide said I’d be out tonight.”\n\nGraves answered, “Saint can move you to state custody. Cell handles the rest.”\n\n“What about the Ridge people?”\n\n“They’ll be transferred after the next incident.”\n\n“What incident?”\n\nGraves lowered his voice.\n\n“The one King K is arranging.”\n\nThe recording reached Duke.\n\nDuke sent it outside through three separate channels.\n\nOne reached Reck.\n\nOne reached Khaos.\n\nThe third reached Detective Holloway.\n\nBy the time Graves realized the camera was active, Internal Affairs was already entering the jail.\n\n---\n\nThe Two Crowns Agreement was signed without paper.\n\nReck and Khaos met at the boundary between Sunridge and Paradise Haven. The street separated two neighborhoods Saint intended to erase from his waterfront map.\n\nRidge Boyz stood on one side in their individual colors beneath the cracked crown.\n\nHaven Boyz stood on the other in burnt orange, black and steel gray beneath the burning halo.\n\nResidents watched from balconies and storefronts.\n\nReck spoke first.\n\n“Ridge stays Ridge.”\n\nKhaos answered, “Haven stays Haven.”\n\n“Neither crown controls the other.”\n\n“But an attack on one brings both.”\n\n“Families stay outside the war.”\n\n“Residents don’t become enemies because they refuse to join.”\n\n“Every name gets verified.”\n\n“Every betrayal gets answered.”\n\nReck extended his hand.\n\nKhaos clasped it.\n\nThe two organizations raised their emblems together.\n\nThere was no cheering from politicians.\n\nNo television cameras.\n\nOnly two neighborhoods recognizing that survival required unity without surrender.\n\nTide watched the gathering through a live video sent by Deuce.\n\nCell stood behind him inside the canal warehouse.\n\n“They got numbers now,” Tide said.\n\nCell looked unconcerned.\n\n“Numbers don’t matter when the money man belongs to us.”\n\nDeuce turned the camera toward Kilo’s black BMW parked near the Haven side.\n\n---\n\nThe first Ridge–Haven move began before sunset.\n\nThree Canal Boy locations had been built on property taken from D-Low and Sunridge families. Reck and Khaos applied pressure at the same time, forcing Tide’s people to divide their attention.\n\nThe Ridge Boyz reclaimed a car lot used to move Cell’s money.\n\nThe Haven Boyz retook two motel buildings Deuce had placed beneath the silver canal emblem.\n\nThere were fights, shattered windows and fleeing vehicles, but both young leaders enforced the agreement: residents were cleared away, families remained untouched and nobody was targeted without confirmation.\n\nBy nightfall, two silver CB emblems lay broken in the street.\n\nOne cracked crown hung above the car lot.\n\nOne burning halo shone beside the Royal Palms Motel.\n\nThe message traveled across Palm Haven.\n\nTwo crowns.\n\nOne war.\n\nAt the hospital, Zay watched the footage on TJ’s phone.\n\n“They followed the plan,” TJ said.\n\nZay looked relieved.\n\nThen Naz called.\n\nThe relief disappeared.\n\n---\n\nKhaos returned to D-Low’s garage expecting a celebration.\n\nInstead, Reck, Naz and La Madrina waited beside the workbench.\n\nKilo entered behind him.\n\nT-Rell closed the garage doors.\n\nRambo and Lil Smoke stood near the Chevrolet.\n\nKhaos looked around.\n\n“What happened?”\n\nNaz placed the Renewal Center parking records on the table.\n\nKilo’s name appeared eleven times.\n\nKhaos looked at him.\n\n“You told me you was home last night.”\n\nKilo laughed once. “This what we doing? Trusting paperwork from Saint?”\n\nLa Madrina placed photographs beside the records.\n\nKilo entering Cell’s warehouse.\n\nKilo meeting Deuce near the canal.\n\nKilo handing an envelope to Captain Vance’s missing jump-out officer.\n\nThe smile left his face.\n\nKhaos removed the parking receipt from his pocket and added it to the table.\n\n“Tell me why.”\n\nKilo looked toward the garage door.\n\nRambo blocked it.\n\n“I kept HB alive,” Kilo said. “While you was locked, everybody needed money. D-Low was losing control. Cell offered business.”\n\nKhaos stepped closer.\n\n“Did you help set up my father?”\n\n“No.”\n\nNaz connected D-Low’s recovered phone to the laptop.\n\nThe full recording had been hidden beneath the damaged audio file. Elena restored it that afternoon.\n\nD-Low’s voice filled the garage again.\n\n“Khaos, if you hearing this, check Kilo. He gave King the Paradise route. I caught him meeting Cell. He said it was only business, but he knew they wanted Reck in that car with me.”\n\nKilo’s face collapsed.\n\nThe recording continued.\n\n“He your friend, but he helped open the door. Don’t let anger make you miss who handed them the key.”\n\nSilence followed.\n\nKhaos stared at the person who had stood beside him before the Haven Boyz had a name.\n\n“You knew?”\n\nKilo backed away from the table.\n\n“King said police was only taking the shipment. He said D-Low would come home. I didn’t know Mon was going to—”\n\nKhaos struck him.\n\nKilo fell against the BMW.\n\nRambo moved forward, but Reck stopped him.\n\nKhaos grabbed Kilo by the jacket.\n\n“My father died.”\n\n“I ain’t pull the trigger.”\n\n“You gave them the road.”\n\nKilo’s fear turned desperate.\n\n“You need me. I know Cell’s locations. I know how Saint pays Vance. I know where King going next.”\n\nKhaos froze.\n\nReck saw the choice tearing through him.\n\nRevenge wanted an immediate answer.\n\nLeadership demanded information.\n\nKhaos released Kilo.\n\n“Tie him to the chair.”\n\nKilo looked surprised to still be alive.\n\nKhaos removed D-Low’s pendant and placed it on the workbench where Kilo could see it.\n\n“You going to tell us everything.”\n\nReck stood beside his cousin.\n\n“And after that?” Kilo asked.\n\nKhaos looked at the burning halo above the garage door.\n\nThen at the cracked crown around Reck’s neck.\n\n“After that, both crowns decide what betrayal costs.”\n\nOutside, engines approached from both ends of the street.\n\nTeal headlights reflected across the garage windows.\n\nCanal Boyz.\n\nKilo began laughing through his fear.\n\n“Too late.”\n\nKhaos turned toward him.\n\n“What did you do?”\n\nThe first vehicle struck the garage door.\n\nMetal folded inward.\n\nKilo looked at D-Low’s son.\n\n“I brought them the Haven Boyz.”"},{"number":14,"title":"Chapter Fourteen — The Garage","body":"The steel door folded inward.\n\nTide’s SUV forced halfway into D-Low’s garage before its front wheels lost traction against twisted metal. Teal headlights swept across the walls. The Canal Boy emblem flashed between sparks and smoke.\n\nKhaos overturned the workbench.\n\nD-Low’s pendant slid across it.\n\nReck caught the chain before it fell.\n\nThe first shots tore through the garage windows.\n\nEverybody moved at once.\n\nT-Rell pulled Lil Smoke behind the Chevrolet. Rambo dragged Kilo’s chair toward the rear office while Naz secured D-Low’s phone and the documents implicating Saint. La Madrina disappeared behind a concrete support as her security detail returned pressure toward the shattered entrance.\n\nKhaos reached for the weapon beneath D-Low’s tool cabinet.\n\nReck grabbed his arm.\n\n“Think.”\n\n“They in my father’s building.”\n\n“That’s why you think.”\n\nAnother vehicle struck the side gate.\n\nDeuce entered behind the Canal Boyz wearing Baby Ant’s stolen pendant.\n\nRambo saw it.\n\nWhatever discipline he had disappeared.\n\nHe rushed through the smoke.\n\n“Rambo!” Khaos shouted.\n\nToo late.\n\nRambo and Deuce collided beside the damaged SUV. Their fight carried them across broken glass as Canal members flooded the garage.\n\nTide stepped through the opening last.\n\nHe wore deep teal beneath a black vest, long twists tied behind his head and no expression on his face.\n\n“Khaos,” he called. “Welcome home.”\n\nKhaos rose from behind the workbench.\n\n“You brought an army to my daddy’s garage.”\n\nTide looked around. “Your daddy ain’t using it.”\n\nKhaos moved before Reck could stop him.\n\nTide met him in the center of the garage.\n\nTheir crews crashed together around them.\n\nThe Haven Boyz were outnumbered, but they knew every corner of the building. T-Rell opened the old maintenance partition and moved Lil Smoke and Naz through it. Mari entered from the rear alley with Ridge reinforcements, trapping several Canal members between both organizations.\n\nReck fought beside Khaos for the first time.\n\nNo argument.\n\nNo competition.\n\nTwo cousins moving beneath two different emblems against the same enemy.\n\nTide struck Khaos across the face and forced him against D-Low’s Chevrolet.\n\n“Prison made you famous,” Tide said. “It didn’t make you ready.”\n\nKhaos drove him backward.\n\n“You’ll remember me when you leave.”\n\nNear the office, Kilo tipped his chair onto its side and worked one wrist free from the plastic restraint. Nobody saw him crawl toward the rear exit.\n\nNobody except T-Rell.\n\nT-Rell abandoned cover and crossed the open floor.\n\nHe caught Kilo at the door.\n\nKilo raised a broken piece of metal.\n\nT-Rell knocked it away.\n\n“You sold D-Low.”\n\n“I kept us paid.”\n\n“You kept yourself paid.”\n\nKilo reached toward the handle.\n\nT-Rell pulled him back into the garage.\n\n“You facing Khaos.”\n\nAn impact shook the rear wall before Kilo could answer.\n\nFire climbed from the storage room.\n\nDeuce had ignited the fuel D-Low once kept for the tow trucks.\n\nSmoke spread across the ceiling.\n\n“Everybody out!” Naz shouted.\n\nKhaos looked toward D-Low’s Chevrolet.\n\nFlames had reached its rear quarter panel.\n\nFor one second, he stopped fighting.\n\nThe car in his only photograph with his father began burning in front of him.\n\nTide used the hesitation to break free.\n\nReck caught Khaos before he followed.\n\n“Your people first.”\n\n“That’s all I got left of him.”\n\nReck pressed D-Low’s pendant into Khaos’s palm.\n\n“No, it ain’t.”\n\nKhaos looked toward T-Rell, Rambo, Kilo and the Haven Boyz disappearing through smoke.\n\nLeadership defeated grief by inches.\n\n“Clear the building!” he ordered.\n\nT-Rell carried Kilo through the rear door. Mari pulled an injured Rambo away from Deuce. Naz escaped with D-Low’s recording and Saint’s documents.\n\nKhaos was the last Haven Boy out.\n\nThe garage exploded behind him.\n\nThe force lifted both cousins from their feet.\n\nThey landed in the alley as glass and burning metal fell across the roofline.\n\nTide’s surviving vehicles escaped toward Harbor View.\n\nDeuce left without Baby Ant’s pendant.\n\nRambo held it inside a blood-covered fist.\n\nKhaos rose slowly and watched D-Low’s garage disappear inside fire.\n\nT-Rell stood beside him.\n\n“We saved the people.”\n\nKhaos opened his hand.\n\nD-Low’s pendant remained there.\n\n“Then we ain’t lose everything.”\n\n---\n\nDetective Holloway arrested Jail Administrator Milton Graves while the garage burned.\n\nInternal Affairs entered his office with the corridor recording, bank transfers from Saint’s consulting company and video of Graves moving Splash into unauthorized protective custody.\n\nGraves did not ask for a lawyer immediately.\n\nHe asked to call Gabriel Saint.\n\nHolloway placed his phone inside an evidence bag.\n\n“That’s not the call I’d make if I were you.”\n\nGraves’s confidence changed.\n\n“You don’t understand what you’re touching.”\n\n“Explain it.”\n\n“Saint chooses police chiefs. Judges attend his fundraisers. Half this jail was renovated through his foundation.”\n\nHolloway sat across from him.\n\n“And the other half?”\n\nGraves looked toward the one-way glass.\n\n“Paid for by seizures.”\n\n“Whose seizures?”\n\n“Everybody’s.”\n\nHe described a system hidden beneath Palm Haven’s war.\n\nThe jump-out unit stole cash and drugs during unofficial raids.\n\nKing K identified vulnerable rivals through his informant agreement.\n\nCell repurchased seized supply through port intermediaries.\n\nSaint’s shell companies cleaned the money through construction contracts and property sales.\n\nThe same neighborhoods were robbed twice—first by street organizations, then by the officials claiming to rescue them.\n\n“The drugs are not the biggest business,” Graves whispered. “The land is.”\n\nHolloway placed Saint’s waterfront map in front of him.\n\n“Why free King K?”\n\n“Saint needed him to keep Reck angry.”\n\n“Why?”\n\n“Because angry young men make the mayor sign emergency redevelopment orders.”\n\nHolloway leaned back.\n\n“The mayor hasn’t signed anything.”\n\nGraves looked at her.\n\n“Not yet.”\n\n---\n\nSaint responded before Holloway finished the interview.\n\nDuke and Dreko’s names appeared on emergency transfer orders. Both were labeled instigators of organized gang activity inside the jail.\n\nThe orders sent them to separate state facilities.\n\nDuke would enter Blackwater, where several Canal Boyz awaited him.\n\nDreko would be sent south to Gulf Rock, a prison controlled by King K’s older allies.\n\nReem received the transfer list five minutes before movement.\n\nHe called Duke through the unit phone.\n\n“They separating you.”\n\n“We knew Saint would answer.”\n\n“Blackwater got people waiting.”\n\nDuke looked through the glass as officers gathered outside.\n\n“Then tell Reck Ridge going to be standing when they open the door.”\n\n“This ain’t a loyalty test.”\n\n“Everything in here is.”\n\nReem lowered his voice.\n\n“Listen to me. Saint wants you hurt so Reck reacts. Don’t give him that.”\n\nDuke smiled without humor.\n\n“Sound like Zay been talking to you.”\n\nThe officers entered.\n\nDuke passed Dreko on the way out of the unit.\n\nThey clasped hands once.\n\n“Two crowns,” Dreko said.\n\n“Both sides of the walls,” Duke answered.\n\nThen Saint’s system carried them in opposite directions.\n\n---\n\nLa Madrina watched D-Low’s garage burn from the rear seat of her SUV.\n\nStone sat across from her.\n\nHe had arrived too late with six armed men and an explanation nobody requested.\n\n“How did Tide know you were here?” she asked.\n\nStone looked through the window. “Kilo gave them the address.”\n\n“Kilo gave them Khaos. Tide knew my vehicle was inside.”\n\nStone did not answer.\n\nLa Madrina placed a second phone between them.\n\nIt contained eleven calls from Stone to a number registered through the Renewal Foundation.\n\n“Gabriel Saint,” she said.\n\nStone’s eyes shifted toward the driver.\n\nThe doors locked.\n\n“He threatened my family.”\n\n“Your family has lived in Cartagena under my protection for eight years.”\n\n“He knows where they are.”\n\n“Because you told him.”\n\nStone’s composure cracked.\n\n“I gave him small things. Meetings. Vehicle movements. Nothing that could destroy Mar Azul.”\n\n“There are no small betrayals at this level.”\n\nStone leaned forward. “You used Reck too.”\n\n“I offered him business.”\n\n“You sent him toward King K and Cell because you needed them weakened.”\n\nLa Madrina did not deny it.\n\nStone continued.\n\n“Saint knew. He let you supply Reck because every shipment increased the war. You thought you were challenging Saint. You were financing his redevelopment plan.”\n\nThe truth struck deeper than the accusation.\n\nLa Madrina had manipulated Reck.\n\nSaint had manipulated her manipulation.\n\nShe looked back at the fire.\n\n“Where is the next transfer?”\n\nStone hesitated.\n\n“What transfer?”\n\n“The one Saint expects every organization to chase.”\n\nStone realized she already knew enough.\n\n“Tomorrow night. Renewal Center payroll convoy. Except it isn’t payroll.”\n\n“What is it?”\n\n“Money from the evidence vault, King K’s informant fund and Cell’s port sales. Saint is moving everything before Holloway reaches it.”\n\n“Why leak the route?”\n\nStone swallowed.\n\n“He wants it robbed.”\n\n---\n\nZay left the hospital against medical advice at 9:15 that night.\n\nHe could barely stand without assistance.\n\nTJ found him attempting to put on his royal-blue jacket over the bandage.\n\n“You lost your mind?”\n\n“Reck ain’t answering.”\n\n“Because the garage just exploded.”\n\n“Exactly.”\n\nTJ tried to block the door.\n\nZay stared at him until he moved.\n\nAmaya arranged a wheelchair. A private nurse followed them into the elevator, protesting every floor.\n\nThey found Reck and Khaos inside the burned garage shell after firefighters left.\n\nKilo remained tied to a metal chair pulled into the alley. His face was swollen. T-Rell guarded him. Rambo sat nearby while a medic treated his arm.\n\nKhaos stood where D-Low’s Chevrolet had been.\n\nOnly the frame remained.\n\nReck turned when he saw Zay.\n\n“What you doing here?”\n\n“Stopping you from making Saint’s next move for him.”\n\n“Go back to the hospital.”\n\n“Tell me the plan first.”\n\nKhaos looked toward Kilo.\n\n“He knows King’s next move.”\n\nReck dragged Kilo’s chair closer.\n\n“Talk.”\n\nKilo looked at the burned building.\n\n“King and Mon hitting Saint’s payroll convoy tomorrow. Cell and Tide too.”\n\nNaz arrived with La Madrina.\n\n“It isn’t payroll,” she said.\n\nShe told them what Stone revealed.\n\nMillions in hidden cash.\n\nSeized money.\n\nInformant payments.\n\nPort profits.\n\nRecords capable of connecting Saint to every level of the operation.\n\nKhaos looked at Reck.\n\n“We take it first.”\n\nZay shook his head. “That’s what Saint wants.”\n\nKhaos turned toward him. “You got shot and left the hospital to tell us not to touch millions?”\n\n“I left to tell you the millions are bait.”\n\nNaz agreed. “The convoy gets robbed, Saint claims insurance, destroys the evidence and gets a federal task force to occupy both neighborhoods.”\n\nReck looked toward La Madrina. “So we let Saint move it?”\n\n“No,” she said. “We change what gets stolen.”\n\nNaz understood first.\n\n“Take the records. Leave enough money for every enemy to fight over.”\n\nMari smiled. “And while they watching the cash?”\n\n“We take the proof,” Reck said.\n\nZay sank deeper into the wheelchair, exhausted.\n\n“That’s how you win.”\n\nKhaos walked toward the remains of his father’s Chevrolet.\n\n“They keep burning buildings because they think buildings make us.”\n\nHe held D-Low’s pendant against his chest.\n\n“Let them burn every spot. As long as we standing, Haven and Ridge still alive.”\n\nHe looked at Reck.\n\n“We taking the records. But I want Haven’s share of the money too.”\n\nReck nodded. “Everybody eats.”\n\n---\n\nThe convoy left the Renewal Center the following night beneath police escort.\n\nKing K watched from a closed gas station with Mon.\n\nCell and Tide waited near the canal bridge.\n\nCaptain Vance controlled two of the escort vehicles.\n\nEach believed Saint had secretly given the route only to them.\n\nNone understood they were all expected to collide.\n\nThe first interruption came at the bridge.\n\nA false emergency diverted the marked police units. Streetlights died across three blocks. Vehicles blocked the east approach while alarms drew private security toward an empty warehouse.\n\nThen Palm Haven’s war arrived from every direction.\n\nKing K’s people struck the rear.\n\nCanal Boyz attacked from the water side.\n\nVance’s officers abandoned their escort positions and attempted to seize the cash themselves.\n\nRidge and Haven moved through the confusion with a different objective.\n\nReck and Khaos reached the armored records vehicle while the older organizations fought over the money truck. Naz identified the black archive cases. T-Rell and Mari moved them into separate vehicles. TJ captured every badge, face and license plate through remote cameras positioned around the corridor.\n\nThe street became noise, headlights and betrayal.\n\nKing K saw Reck across the bridge.\n\n“That’s mine!” he shouted.\n\nReck lifted one archive case.\n\n“Everything you got belonged to somebody else first.”\n\nMon moved toward him.\n\nKhaos stepped between them wearing D-Low’s pendant.\n\nMon stopped.\n\nFor the first time, D-Low’s son stood face-to-face with the man who helped cause his father’s death.\n\n“You look like him,” Mon said.\n\n“Remember that when I come back for you.”\n\nTide’s SUV crashed between them as Cell’s men attempted to escape with the cash vehicle.\n\nKhaos could have chased Mon.\n\nInstead, he protected T-Rell and the archive cases.\n\nThe choice cost him revenge that night.\n\nIt gained him the operation.\n\nReck saw the difference.\n\nKhaos was no longer merely D-Low’s angry son.\n\nHe was becoming a leader.\n\nThe Ridge and Haven vehicles left the bridge with Saint’s records and part of the cash. Behind them, King K, Cell, Tide and Vance’s corrupt officers continued fighting over what remained.\n\nSirens approached from downtown.\n\nHolloway’s homicide task force arrived alongside federal agents she had never called.\n\nSaint’s final move had begun.\n\n---\n\nBy sunrise, Palm Haven declared a state of emergency.\n\nThe mayor appeared beside Gabriel Saint and announced a joint federal task force targeting organized crime in Sunridge, Paradise Haven and Harbor View.\n\nSaint offered Renewal Center properties as temporary police command posts.\n\nHe appeared calm.\n\nConcerned.\n\nVictorious.\n\nHe had lost the archive cases but gained the occupation he wanted.\n\nAt a hidden location beneath D-Low’s old trucking company, Naz opened the first case.\n\nCash filled the top layer.\n\nBeneath it were contracts, ledgers and encrypted drives.\n\nThe second case contained photographs of judges, officers, developers and organization leaders accepting payments.\n\nThe third held property deeds for nearly half of Sunridge and Paradise Haven.\n\nEvery deed transferred ownership to a company none of them recognized.\n\n**CROWN HARBOR DEVELOPMENT GROUP.**\n\nLa Madrina examined the signatures.\n\n“These weren’t signed by Saint.”\n\nNaz turned to the final page.\n\nThe controlling owner had been hidden behind a trust.\n\nElena Ruiz broke the encryption and displayed the beneficiary.\n\nReck read the name aloud.\n\n“Kareem Dawson Sr.”\n\nNobody moved.\n\nKhaos looked from the document to Reck.\n\n“Your father owns Saint’s companies?”\n\n“That don’t make sense.”\n\nLa Madrina stared at the screen.\n\n“Unless Reem was never protecting Saint.”\n\nNaz understood the implication.\n\n“Saint has been operating the streets.”\n\nHe turned the document toward Reck.\n\n“But every time the violence lowered the property value, ownership moved closer to your father’s trust.”\n\nReck remembered Reem’s words.\n\n**I built you into my revenge.**\n\nThe war had made Reck powerful.\n\nIt had made Khaos rich enough to rebuild.\n\nIt had weakened King K, Cell and Saint.\n\nAnd it had quietly transferred Palm Haven’s future into Reem’s name.\n\nReck looked at La Madrina.\n\n“Did you know?”\n\nFor once, she had no answer.\n\nKhaos placed D-Low’s recording beside the property deeds.\n\n“Everybody been manipulating everybody.”\n\nReck stared at his father’s name.\n\nOutside, federal vehicles entered Sunridge.\n\nInside, the Ridge Boyz and Haven Boyz had more money than either crew had ever controlled—and evidence powerful enough to destroy half the city.\n\nBut the price of that power had arrived with it.\n\nZay was wounded.\n\nDuke and Dreko were being transferred.\n\nD-Low’s garage was gone.\n\nThe neighborhoods were under occupation.\n\nAnd Reck’s father might have been the hidden hand behind the entire Crown War.\n\nReck closed the archive case.\n\n“Saint thinks the task force got us surrounded.”\n\nKhaos stood beside him.\n\n“Reem thinks we building his empire.”\n\nReck fastened the cracked crown.\n\nKhaos lifted the burning halo.\n\n“They both wrong,” Reck said.\n\nThe two cousins looked toward the Renewal Center rising above downtown Palm Haven.\n\n“Now we find out who really owns the war.”"},{"number":15,"title":"Chapter Fifteen — Candy Paint & Gun Smoke","body":"Palm Haven looked different when the Two Crowns rode together.\n\nCandy paint turned morning sunlight into moving color across Paradise Boulevard. A cherry-red Caprice rolled on oversized gold rims beside Khaos’s burnt-orange Impala. TJ followed in a purple-and-gray box Chevy. Naz drove a forest-green Cadillac with charcoal trim. Mari’s orange Hellcat growled behind them while two lifted trucks carried the cracked crown and burning halo above traffic.\n\nReck led the convoy in a black-and-gold Ford Super Duty.\n\nThe passenger doors still carried scars from the war.\n\nHe refused to repaint them.\n\n“Let everybody see we still riding,” he told Mari.\n\nPeople came outside as the vehicles passed.\n\nChildren pointed at glowing wheels and outrageous wraps. Older men recognized restored Cutlasses, Monte Carlos and Grand Nationals from another generation of Florida streets. Phone cameras followed the fleet from Sunridge to Paradise Haven.\n\nIt looked like power.\n\nNaz knew it also looked like evidence.\n\n“Saint got every federal camera in the city watching us,” he said through the radio.\n\nReck looked toward a helicopter circling beyond downtown.\n\n“Let them watch us go home.”\n\nThey never reached home.\n\nThree unmarked vehicles entered the boulevard behind them.\n\nTwo more appeared ahead.\n\nThe first shot shattered Reck’s rear window.\n\nGunfire ripped across the candy-black paint and sent pedestrians diving behind parked cars. Reck forced the truck away from the sidewalk as a teal Trackhawk struck his rear bumper.\n\n“Canal Boyz!” Mari shouted through the radio.\n\nReck saw Deuce leaning from the passenger side.\n\nKhaos turned his Impala toward them.\n\n“Stay with the people,” Reck ordered. “Get everybody off the boulevard.”\n\n“They shooting at you.”\n\n“And they want you chasing.”\n\nThe lifted truck pushed through the intersection while the others blocked traffic long enough for residents to clear. Teal vehicles followed Reck toward the warehouse district.\n\nHe disappeared between two abandoned packing buildings and emerged alone five minutes later.\n\nThe Canal Boyz were gone.\n\nThe Ford’s passenger side was destroyed.\n\nReck climbed out beneath a cracked-crown mural and stared at the damage.\n\nMari arrived first.\n\n“We need to move you.”\n\nReck opened the rear door and removed a black bag.\n\n“We need to find who sent them.”\n\n“You already know.”\n\n“Knowing the organization ain’t knowing the names.”\n\nNaz arrived with Khaos.\n\nTJ’s cameras had captured Deuce and three verified Canal members. A marina access sticker appeared on one windshield.\n\nKhaos looked at the shattered truck.\n\n“We going back tonight.”\n\nReck nodded.\n\n“After the streets clear.”\n\nZay’s rule remained alive even while he recovered.\n\nVerify the target.\n\nProtect the neighborhood.\n\nThen answer.\n\n---\n\nThe federal operation began before Reck could return.\n\nAgents called it **Operation Crown Sweep**.\n\nUndercover purchases had been arranged across Sunridge, Paradise Haven and Harbor View. Search teams moved simultaneously against The Sunshine’s damaged property, D-Low’s trucking company, three custom garages and several Ridge–Haven money locations.\n\nSome warrants were legitimate.\n\nOthers contained addresses supplied by Captain Vance.\n\nOne listed a building demolished two years earlier.\n\nAnother described evidence that had not yet entered Palm Haven.\n\nDetective Holloway read the paperwork from a command vehicle and understood Saint’s strategy.\n\nThe raids were not designed only to arrest people.\n\nThey were designed to seize land, money and vehicles before federal auditors examined the waterfront records.\n\n“Stop the Sunridge teams,” she ordered.\n\nThe federal commander looked at her. “You don’t control this operation.”\n\n“These affidavits are contaminated.”\n\n“Then challenge them in court.”\n\nThe first battering ram struck The Sunshine.\n\n---\n\nKilo had given the task force one final offering.\n\nA large Ridge–Haven transfer would pass through the Royal Palms corridor that afternoon. He promised drugs, cash and enough organization members to justify conspiracy charges.\n\nWhat he did not know was that Naz had already changed the shipment.\n\nThe vehicles carried only a fraction of La Madrina’s supply.\n\nThe most important cargo was one archive case containing copies of Saint’s ledgers.\n\nT-Rell drove it inside the candy-blue box Chevy.\n\nReck saw federal SUVs enter the corridor from both ends.\n\n“Move,” he said into the radio.\n\nThe street erupted.\n\nMarked cruisers poured from motel parking lots. Unmarked jump-out cars climbed curbs to block side streets. A helicopter dropped over the rooftops as agents closed on the convoy.\n\nMari’s Hellcat broke toward downtown.\n\nKhaos stayed beside Reck’s damaged truck.\n\nT-Rell disappeared through the back streets behind Paradise Haven with the archive case.\n\nPolice followed all three.\n\nThe chase tore across Palm Haven in separate streams of color and sirens.\n\nReck pushed the wounded Ford through the narrow cuts behind Sunridge, where murals and low apartment buildings turned the helicopter’s view into flashes. Khaos’s orange Impala remained behind him even when the lifted truck began smoking.\n\n“Leave me,” Reck ordered.\n\n“Two crowns,” Khaos answered.\n\nMari drew half the pursuit toward the downtown drawbridge. His orange Hellcat flew beneath neon signs and past stopped traffic while federal SUVs struggled to follow without striking civilians.\n\nT-Rell took the blue Chevy toward the abandoned citrus roads west of the city.\n\nTJ monitored traffic cameras from a moving purple box Chevy.\n\n“They steering all of y’all south,” he warned.\n\nNaz studied the road closures.\n\nEvery available escape path led toward the marina and Harbor View.\n\n“This ain’t a chase,” Naz said. “Saint moving everybody into Canal territory.”\n\nReck saw teal headlights appear beyond the next intersection.\n\nTide was waiting.\n\nFederal vehicles closed from behind.\n\nCanal Boyz blocked the road ahead.\n\nReck turned the Ford through the rusted gates of an abandoned citrus warehouse. Khaos followed. They exited through the loading yard seconds before police entered the front.\n\nThe helicopter remained above them.\n\nThere was nowhere left to disappear.\n\nThen hundreds of custom vehicles entered the boulevard.\n\nOwners from Palm Haven’s car clubs had watched police seize vehicles all morning. Donks, lifted trucks and old-school Chevrolets filled the roads for an unscheduled protest cruise. Candy paint covered every lane.\n\nReck and Khaos vanished inside the moving colors.\n\nFederal agents could no longer identify which black truck or orange Chevrolet they were following without endangering uninvolved drivers.\n\nThe helicopter climbed.\n\nThe Two Crowns escaped the trap.\n\nT-Rell did not.\n\n---\n\nTide captured the candy-blue Chevy near the unfinished wetland bridge.\n\nT-Rell escaped through the orange groves with Saint’s archive case.\n\nLil Smoke, who had followed as protection, did not.\n\nCanal Boyz dragged him into a fishing truck and drove beyond Harbor View, where paved roads dissolved into water and cypress trees.\n\nThe Gator Hole sat at the end of a forgotten canal.\n\nStories surrounded it.\n\nEnemies disappeared there.\n\nBoats returned empty.\n\nTide used the fear more often than the place itself.\n\nLil Smoke sat tied beneath an open shelter while dark water moved beyond the dock.\n\nDeuce placed a phone in front of him.\n\n“Tell Khaos where Naz hid Saint’s records.”\n\n“I don’t know.”\n\n“Then tell him something useful.”\n\nLil Smoke looked toward the canal.\n\nTwo eyes broke the surface and disappeared.\n\nTide stepped from the shadows.\n\n“You get one chance. After that, we let the swamp decide whether you lying.”\n\nLil Smoke stared into the phone.\n\n“Khaos, don’t bring them nothing.”\n\nDeuce struck him and ended the recording.\n\nTide smiled.\n\n“Loyal.”\n\n“Always,” Lil Smoke said.\n\n“That’s going to cost you.”\n\n---\n\nReck returned to Sunridge after dark in the same damaged truck.\n\nThe federal task force had taken six Ridge vehicles, three Haven cars and nearly every customer vehicle from a custom garage. Yellow seizure stickers covered doors across the neighborhood.\n\nThe Sunshine was closed behind federal tape.\n\nSaint appeared on television praising the operation.\n\nThen Khaos received Lil Smoke’s video.\n\nEvery Haven Boy wanted to leave immediately.\n\nNaz stopped them.\n\n“The Gator Hole got one road, open water and Canal lookouts. Running angry gets him killed.”\n\nKhaos played the video again.\n\n“He stayed loyal while I was locked. I’m not leaving him.”\n\n“Nobody said leave him.” Reck entered wearing black, King K’s watch and D-Low’s bloodline standing beside him. “We verify who there and bring him home.”\n\nT-Rell placed the archive case on the table.\n\n“I know another way near it.”\n\nReck looked at him.\n\n“How?”\n\n“D-Low used to tow boats from those fishing camps. There’s an old service path from the marina.”\n\nKhaos nodded.\n\n“Then we move after the last fishing families leave.”\n\nReck saw the growth in him again.\n\nAnger remained.\n\nBut leadership now stood in front of it.\n\n---\n\nThe return strike began with engines.\n\nReck’s damaged Ford led the convoy down Paradise Boulevard so everybody saw Tide had failed to stop him.\n\nBehind it rolled candy-red and lime-green donks, Khaos’s burnt-orange Impala, Mari’s recovered backup Charger and black lifted trucks carrying both emblems.\n\nThey did not approach the wetlands as an invisible force.\n\nThey approached as a message.\n\nAt the marina, Ridge and Haven members confronted the verified Canal attackers from Reck’s ambush. Money taken from seized neighborhood operations sat inside the warehouse. Several corrupt jump-out officers were already there demanding their share.\n\nNobody trusted anyone.\n\nThe first shot came from Deuce’s side.\n\nThe marina erupted.\n\nGlass fell from the warehouse. Boats struck docks as men scattered. Corrupt officers attempted to leave with cash while Canal Boyz turned on them. Reck and Khaos pushed toward the wetland path instead of chasing every enemy.\n\nTheir objective was Lil Smoke.\n\nMari and Rambo held the marina entrance.\n\nNaz recovered the stolen neighborhood money.\n\nT-Rell led the cousins beyond the final dock.\n\nThey found the shelter burning.\n\nLil Smoke had freed one hand and knocked over a fuel lamp. Smoke forced his guards outside before they could carry him toward the water.\n\nKhaos reached him first.\n\n“Took y’all long enough,” Lil Smoke said.\n\nKhaos cut him free.\n\n“You talked?”\n\nLil Smoke looked offended. “About what?”\n\nKhaos pulled him into an embrace.\n\nAcross the canal, Tide climbed into an airboat with Deuce.\n\nReck appeared on the dock.\n\n“This ain’t finished.”\n\nTide started the engine.\n\n“Come deeper next time.”\n\nThe boat disappeared between cypress trees.\n\nThe Gator Hole remained his territory.\n\nBut the person he intended to make an example of left alive beneath the burning halo.\n\n---\n\nSaint controlled the story by sunrise.\n\nNews stations played footage of the custom Ridge–Haven convoy, the federal chase and the marina battle. They did not show Reck’s ambush. They did not mention Lil Smoke’s kidnapping. They did not show corrupt officers removing money from the warehouse.\n\nCouncilman Saint called the Two Crowns a domestic criminal army.\n\nThe mayor expanded emergency powers.\n\nFederal command posts opened inside Renewal Center properties.\n\nInsurance companies canceled coverage across Sunridge custom shops and Paradise Haven businesses.\n\nThe city moved to condemn The Sunshine permanently.\n\nCell increased the price on Reck and Khaos.\n\nLa Madrina demanded payment for every package seized during Crown Sweep.\n\nThe streets became too hot for anyone to conduct business normally.\n\nKing K blamed Reck.\n\nCell blamed Saint.\n\nTide blamed Khaos.\n\nThe cartel blamed everyone.\n\nDetective Holloway stood inside the federal evidence warehouse examining the packages seized from the Ridge–Haven transfer.\n\nEach carried a faded identification mark beneath newer wrapping.\n\nShe entered the numbers into the police system.\n\nAll of them had been seized six months earlier by Captain Vance’s jump-out unit.\n\nThe same drugs had already appeared in a closed federal case.\n\nThey were supposed to have been destroyed.\n\nInstead, they had returned to the streets through Cell, passed into La Madrina’s war supply and ended inside a federal drug bust designed by Saint.\n\nHolloway called the task-force commander.\n\n“Your biggest seizure belongs to the police.”\n\nHe stared at the evidence markings.\n\n“That’s impossible.”\n\n“No,” Holloway said. “That’s Palm Haven.”\n\nAcross the city, Captain Vance received an alert that the markings had been discovered.\n\nHe called Saint.\n\nSaint watched federal vehicles surround Sunridge from his top-floor office.\n\n“Burn the chain of custody,” he said.\n\n“The evidence is inside a federal warehouse.”\n\nSaint looked toward the police station across Government Square.\n\n“Then give the city another emergency.”\n\nThat night, somebody entered the station’s evidence annex carrying gasoline."},{"number":16,"title":"Chapter Sixteen — State of War","body":"Mook believed the fire would make him Ridge again.\n\nThe message had reached him from an unknown number shortly after Operation Crown Sweep.\n\n**THEY PLANTED THE CASE ON RECK. DESTROY THE ANNEX AND SAVE THE CREW.**\n\nBeneath it was a photograph of federal boxes labeled with Reck’s name.\n\nMook never asked who took the picture.\n\nHe never called Naz.\n\nHe never verified anything.\n\nHe entered the Palm Haven Police Department through a service door left unlocked by Captain Vance’s missing officer. The gasoline waited inside a janitorial cart.\n\nEvery decision had been prepared for him.\n\nAll Mook supplied was desperation.\n\nHe poured the fuel across the evidence-annex floor and struck the flame.\n\nIt spread faster than promised.\n\nFire climbed the shelves, rolled across the ceiling and reached the detention corridor before Mook made it back to the service door. Alarms failed. Emergency locks sealed. Smoke swallowed officers, civilian employees and six people waiting in temporary holding cells.\n\nMook stood outside watching windows turn orange.\n\nThen he heard screaming.\n\nHe ran back inside.\n\n---\n\nThe explosion reached Reck as a flash beyond downtown.\n\nHe stood inside D-Low’s trucking warehouse with Khaos, Naz, Mari and TJ, counting what remained after the federal raids. La Madrina waited near the archive cases while cartel representatives demanded the missing shipment be repaid.\n\nEvery television changed to breaking news.\n\n**PALM HAVEN POLICE STATION UNDER ATTACK.**\n\nSaint appeared on screen before firefighters reached the building.\n\n“This is an act of war against law enforcement,” he declared.\n\nReck stared at the words scrolling beneath him.\n\n**RIDGE BOYZ SUSPECTED.**\n\n“How they got suspects already?” TJ asked.\n\nNaz’s phone rang.\n\nMook’s name appeared.\n\nReck answered.\n\nSmoke and coughing filled the line.\n\n“I was trying to help,” Mook said.\n\nReck’s expression emptied.\n\n“What did you do?”\n\n“They told me your case was in here. Said if I destroyed it—”\n\n“Who told you?”\n\n“I don’t know.”\n\nReck closed his eyes.\n\nThe same mistake.\n\nThe same open gate.\n\nThe same need to earn something he had already lost.\n\nMook began shouting through the phone.\n\n“People trapped! The holding doors won’t open!”\n\nReck grabbed his jacket.\n\nMari blocked him. “Police station burning and you going toward it?”\n\n“Our name already on it.”\n\n“That ain’t a reason.”\n\n“Mook got civilians trapped.”\n\nKhaos picked up the keys to his lifted Silverado.\n\n“Then both crowns go.”\n\nLa Madrina looked at them as if they had lost their minds.\n\nNaz understood first.\n\n“Saint wants footage of us attacking police.”\n\nReck opened the warehouse door.\n\n“Then we give the city different footage.”\n\n---\n\nCustom trucks arrived before the fire department established a perimeter.\n\nReck’s damaged Ford stopped beside Khaos’s burnt-orange Silverado. Ridge and Haven members climbed out without weapons. TJ immediately began streaming from three phones.\n\nThe entire city watched them run toward the burning station.\n\nOfficers raised guns.\n\nDetective Holloway pushed through them.\n\n“Lower those!”\n\n“They did this,” an officer shouted.\n\n“Then arrest them after they help your people.”\n\nReck entered the smoke with Khaos and Mari.\n\nThey found Mook beside the holding corridor, burned across one arm and trying to lift a steel bench through the locked door.\n\n“I’m sorry,” he coughed.\n\nReck grabbed the other side.\n\n“Be sorry outside.”\n\nTogether they shattered the glass partition. Khaos and Mari carried detainees through the opening while firefighters reached the second floor. Holloway pulled an unconscious records clerk from the stairwell.\n\nBehind the annex, Vance’s corrupt officers used the chaos differently.\n\nThey loaded cash, jewelry, seized property and confidential files into two unmarked trucks.\n\nNaz captured the theft from across Government Square.\n\n“There,” he told TJ. “That’s the real operation.”\n\nThe trucks left through the courthouse tunnel while television cameras remained focused on the flames.\n\nSaint watched from the Renewal Center.\n\nReck was supposed to look like a terrorist.\n\nInstead, live video showed him carrying a wounded police employee from the station.\n\nSaint muted the television.\n\n“Find another angle,” he told his communications director.\n\n---\n\nDuke’s transfer convoy crossed the wetland highway at the same time the station burned.\n\nHe occupied the rear cage of the first transport van. Dreko rode in the second. Four Canal Boy inmates sat between them.\n\nThe route had been changed without explanation.\n\nReem’s people planned to delay the convoy at the county line so attorneys could challenge the emergency orders.\n\nSaint’s people planned something else.\n\nA dump truck entered the highway from a dark service road.\n\nThe first transport swerved.\n\nThe second struck its rear corner and rolled into the marsh.\n\nMetal folded.\n\nGlass burst.\n\nThe world turned over twice before stopping against a cypress tree.\n\nDuke woke hanging from his restraints.\n\nWater entered through a broken window.\n\nAcross the aisle, a Canal Boy kicked at the rear door while an injured officer struggled beneath a seat.\n\nDuke freed one hand from the damaged restraint and pulled the officer above the rising water.\n\nThe rear doors opened from outside.\n\nMasked men stood on the road.\n\nOne pointed toward Duke.\n\n“That’s him.”\n\nThey had not come to free prisoners.\n\nThey had come to remove a witness.\n\nDuke pushed the officer through the side window and followed into the marsh. Gunfire cut through reeds behind him. He disappeared between cypress trees still wearing one handcuff and a torn Ridge shirt.\n\nDreko escaped the second van long enough to drag a wounded Haven member away from the wreck.\n\nHe could have run.\n\nInstead, he returned for another.\n\nState officers surrounded him before he could leave again.\n\nDreko was recaptured.\n\nDuke vanished into the wetlands carrying Administrator Graves’s original recording inside the lining of his jail shoe.\n\nBy midnight, Palm Haven announced its most wanted fugitive.\n\nSaint blamed Duke for the transport attack.\n\nReem received a single message from the swamp.\n\n**RIDGE STILL STANDING.**\n\n---\n\nMook survived the station fire.\n\nHolloway handcuffed him to a hospital bed.\n\nReck stood in the doorway while doctors treated the burns.\n\nMook could not look at him.\n\n“I thought I was helping.”\n\n“You thought getting your name back mattered more than checking the truth.”\n\n“Saint set me up.”\n\n“Saint opened a door. You walked through it twice.”\n\nMook began crying.\n\n“What happens now?”\n\nReck looked toward Holloway.\n\n“Now you tell her everything. The number, the picture, the service door and whoever left that gasoline.”\n\n“That make me a snitch?”\n\n“You almost killed innocent people for somebody else’s lie. This ain’t crew business. This is you fixing what you did.”\n\nMook looked at him for the first time.\n\nReck continued.\n\n“You wanted to prove you could stand ten toes. Start by standing in the truth.”\n\nHolloway activated her recorder.\n\nMook began talking.\n\n---\n\nNaz traced the stolen evidence trucks to an abandoned municipal auction yard near the port.\n\nThe vehicles carried everything taken from Ridge, Haven and several unrelated crews during Crown Sweep.\n\nLa Madrina’s missing supply occupied one truck.\n\nKing K’s confidential files occupied another.\n\nD-Low’s property, jewelry from Sunridge residents and cash skimmed from Harbor View filled the rest.\n\nCaptain Vance intended to divide it between Cell, Saint and the corrupt officers before federal auditors arrived.\n\nEvery organization wanted it.\n\nKing K and Mon approached from downtown.\n\nCell sent Tide from the canal.\n\nLa Madrina demanded the Two Crowns recover her shipment.\n\nReck saw the pattern.\n\n“Saint keeps putting everybody in the same place.”\n\nKhaos checked D-Low’s pendant. “This time we know.”\n\n“Knowing don’t mean we leave our money.”\n\n“Then we take what’s ours before they turn it into another trap.”\n\nZay joined through a hospital video call.\n\n“Get proof first,” he warned. “Money disappears. Proof keeps Saint from owning the story.”\n\nReck looked at Naz.\n\n“Cameras everywhere.”\n\nTJ smiled. “Already on it.”\n\n---\n\nThe auction yard became the center of Palm Haven’s war shortly before dawn.\n\nRows of seized donks, lifted trucks, motorcycles and luxury cars sat behind razor wire. Many belonged to people never charged with anything.\n\nVance’s officers opened the evidence trucks beneath portable lights.\n\nCell arrived first.\n\n“My percentage increased,” he told Vance. “The city too hot because you lost control.”\n\nVance pointed toward the burning skyline. “You think that station fire was losing control?”\n\nTide’s Canal Boyz entered through the port gate.\n\nKing K and Mon watched from an auction building.\n\nLa Madrina’s people occupied the rooftop across the street.\n\nRidge and Haven arrived last.\n\nReck’s damaged Ford broke through the outer gate beside Khaos’s Silverado. Candy-painted vehicles followed, their custom lights reflecting across confiscated cars.\n\nVance raised his weapon.\n\n“Federal agents are coming.”\n\nReck stepped from the truck.\n\n“Then they can watch you stealing from your own evidence room.”\n\nTJ transmitted the live feed.\n\nVance looked toward the cameras.\n\nCell realized Saint had abandoned the secrecy of their arrangement.\n\nKing K saw his confidential files being unloaded.\n\nEvery alliance broke at once.\n\nThe yard erupted into gunfire, engines and shattered lights.\n\nCanal Boyz rushed the supply truck. King K’s people moved for the informant files. Vance’s officers attempted to escape with cash. Mar Azul security demanded everything bearing cartel marks.\n\nReck and Khaos divided their people.\n\nNaz secured the records.\n\nMari reclaimed Ridge vehicles.\n\nT-Rell recovered D-Low’s property.\n\nHaven members moved neighborhood cash away from the corrupt officers.\n\nReck saw Mon across the rows of cars.\n\nMon fired first.\n\nBullets struck the candy-red Caprice between them.\n\nReck answered and moved closer, but a vehicle exploded near the auction building. Smoke separated them again.\n\nKhaos chased King K toward the informant truck.\n\nKing held D-Low’s confidential file in one hand.\n\n“Your father was trying to become me,” King said.\n\n“He died becoming better than you.”\n\nKing threw the file into a burning barrel.\n\nKhaos reached through the flames and pulled it out before the pages disappeared. King escaped through the auction tunnel while Khaos saved the proof.\n\nAgain, leadership cost him immediate revenge.\n\nAgain, it made him more dangerous.\n\nFederal sirens approached from the port.\n\nReck ordered both crowns out.\n\nThey left with their seized vehicles, neighborhood money, D-Low’s file and the records connecting Vance to the station theft.\n\nLa Madrina recovered only half her shipment.\n\nShe was waiting when Reck reached the trucking warehouse.\n\n“You owe Mar Azul the rest,” she said.\n\n“Police took from everybody.”\n\n“My agreement was with you.”\n\n“And my agreement was everybody connected to The Ridge eats.”\n\nHer expression hardened.\n\n“Do not confuse partnership with equality.”\n\nKhaos stepped beside Reck.\n\n“Two crowns. Nobody over nobody.”\n\nLa Madrina looked at D-Low’s son.\n\n“That slogan will become expensive.”\n\nReck closed the warehouse door between them.\n\n“Power already is.”\n\n---\n\nDuke emerged from the wetlands after sunrise.\n\nHe reached Paradise Haven through drainage canals and abandoned service roads. A custom-car owner recognized the Ridge tattoo on his arm and hid him beneath a vehicle cover before police passed.\n\nKhaos brought him to the trucking warehouse.\n\nDuke entered barefoot, wet and still wearing one broken handcuff.\n\nReck embraced him.\n\n“They saying you attacked the transport.”\n\nDuke removed Graves’s recording from his shoe.\n\n“They saying whatever Saint pays them to say.”\n\nHe handed it to Naz.\n\n“Dreko got recaptured saving one of ours.”\n\nKhaos lowered his head.\n\n“We getting him home.”\n\n“Not by running into Saint’s next setup,” Duke said. “He got people inside every transfer, every jail and every court.”\n\nReck looked at Zay’s image on the screen.\n\nHis two most disciplined voices were finally in the same room.\n\nThe Ridge Boyz had their first captain back.\n\nHe was also the most wanted fugitive in Palm Haven.\n\n---\n\nNaz opened a sealed compartment recovered from the evidence annex.\n\nInside were contracts older than Reem’s federal case.\n\nOne established the original port-development partnership between Gabriel Saint and Mar Azul investors.\n\nAnother transferred startup money through a company controlled by Camila Vélez.\n\nLa Madrina’s signature appeared beneath Saint’s.\n\nThe room went silent.\n\nReck called her back.\n\nShe entered alone.\n\nHe placed the contract in front of her.\n\n“You told me Saint manipulated your war.”\n\nLa Madrina read her own signature.\n\n“He did.”\n\n“These papers say you helped build his system.”\n\n“I financed a port. Saint promised legitimate access for Mar Azul businesses. He turned the partnership into political control.”\n\n“And Reem?”\n\n“He discovered the land transfers and tried taking the system from Saint.”\n\nNaz opened the deeds tied to Reem’s trust.\n\n“So Reem wasn’t only accepting prison to protect Saint.”\n\nLa Madrina looked toward the window.\n\n“He was protecting his ownership until his son became strong enough to reclaim it.”\n\nReck felt the manipulation closing from every direction.\n\nSaint used King K, Cell, Vance and the federal occupation.\n\nReem used La Madrina, prison power and his own son’s rise.\n\nLa Madrina used Reck to weaken her old partners.\n\nEvery older leader had treated the younger crews like pieces on a board.\n\nKhaos placed D-Low’s burned file beside the contracts.\n\n“Everybody explains betrayal after we find the proof.”\n\nLa Madrina faced him.\n\n“And everybody demanding power eventually learns it was purchased before they arrived.”\n\nOutside, federal armored vehicles entered Sunridge, Paradise Haven and Harbor View simultaneously.\n\nThe mayor declared a citywide emergency.\n\nStreet businesses closed.\n\nDrug operations stopped.\n\nMoney froze.\n\nArrests climbed.\n\nBodies appeared in neighborhoods where alliances collapsed overnight.\n\nNobody could earn safely.\n\nNobody trusted their supplier.\n\nNobody trusted police.\n\nEverybody wanted revenge.\n\nReck looked around the warehouse at the Ridge Boyz, Haven Boyz, a wanted Duke, the wounded faces of his people and millions in recovered money that could not buy peace.\n\n“Saint wants the whole city desperate,” Naz said. “Desperate people sell cheap.”\n\nReck looked toward the Renewal Center.\n\n“Then we keep our neighborhoods from selling.”\n\nMari frowned. “And the war?”\n\n“We finish it.”\n\nKhaos raised the burning halo.\n\n“Who first?”\n\nReck placed Saint’s contract, King K’s files and Reem’s deeds inside one archive case.\n\n“Everybody hiding behind the corruption.”\n\nThe station still burned across Government Square.\n\nDuke was free.\n\nDreko was trapped.\n\nLa Madrina wanted her money.\n\nKing K and Mon wanted revenge.\n\nCell and Tide wanted business restored.\n\nFederal agents wanted the Two Crowns dismantled.\n\nAnd Saint wanted Palm Haven frightened enough to surrender.\n\nThe streets had never been hotter.\n\nThe price of power had never been higher.\n\nThe citywide war had officially begun."},{"number":17,"title":"Chapter Seventeen — No Business, No Peace","body":"The first thing Palm Haven ran out of was trust.\n\nThe second was money.\n\nFederal vehicles occupied the entrances to Sunridge, Paradise Haven and Harbor View. Officers stopped custom cars before they reached the boulevard. Banks froze accounts connected to Saint’s conspiracy investigation. Stores closed early. Construction crews refused to enter the neighborhoods without police escorts.\n\nStreet business stopped.\n\nLegitimate business stopped with it.\n\nFamilies who had never touched the war began missing rent because the restaurants, garages, salons and trucking companies employing them could no longer operate.\n\nSaint called it pressure on criminal organizations.\n\nReck called it starving the neighborhood.\n\nHe opened the recovered auction-yard money inside D-Low’s trucking warehouse.\n\nNaz separated it into three groups.\n\nLegal defense.\n\nNeighborhood survival.\n\nOrganization operations.\n\nMari looked at the stacks shrinking across the tables.\n\n“At this rate, we broke in two weeks.”\n\n“People eat first,” Reck said.\n\n“Can’t protect people if we got nothing left.”\n\n“Can’t claim The Ridge if we let it starve.”\n\nZay entered on crutches wearing royal blue beneath a loose jacket.\n\nEvery conversation stopped.\n\n“You supposed to be in the hospital,” TJ said.\n\n“Y’all keep starting wars during visiting hours.”\n\nReck moved to help him.\n\nZay waved him away and reached the table alone.\n\nHe studied the money.\n\n“This is what Saint waiting on.”\n\nMari frowned. “Us paying bills?”\n\n“Us getting desperate when it runs out.”\n\nNaz opened Saint’s relocation offers.\n\nResidents were being offered cash to surrender leases and sell damaged property to Crown Harbor Development Group. The offers expired in ten days.\n\n“He freezes the neighborhood’s income,” Naz said. “Then waves money in front of everybody.”\n\nZay looked at Reck.\n\n“You want to beat Saint? Keep people from having to sell.”\n\nReck pushed the neighborhood pile toward Naz.\n\n“Then everybody eats until business comes back.”\n\n---\n\nKhaos restarted Paradise Haven through D-Low’s legitimate connections.\n\nThe trucking company delivered groceries, construction materials and medical supplies instead of anything that could invite another federal seizure. The old club owner reopened his kitchen as a community meal location. T-Rell organized drivers. Rambo protected the lots without displaying weapons. Lil Smoke delivered food to families who had remained loyal while Khaos was incarcerated.\n\nFor the first time, the Haven Boyz made money without hiding every dollar.\n\nKhaos stood inside the dispatch office beneath a photograph of D-Low.\n\n“My father had all this and still kept choosing the street first.”\n\nT-Rell checked the delivery board. “Street money faster.”\n\n“So is losing it.”\n\nKhaos looked toward the federal checkpoint beyond the trucking gate.\n\n“We keep both sides alive. But nobody uses the business to dirty D-Low’s name again.”\n\nHis phone rang.\n\nCell.\n\n“Everybody hurting,” Cell said. “Mar Azul wants payment. My canal routes closed. Your people can’t move. Reck’s neighborhood bleeding cash.”\n\n“Sounds like you having a bad week.”\n\n“It becomes your bad year if business doesn’t return.”\n\nCell proposed a forty-eight-hour ceasefire between the Two Crowns, Canal Boyz, King K’s people and Mar Azul.\n\nNo attacks.\n\nNo territory moves.\n\nNo retaliation.\n\nEach side would pressure Saint’s emergency task force to reopen commercial routes.\n\nKhaos laughed.\n\n“You want peace now because you can’t make money.”\n\n“Peace has always been business wearing cleaner clothes.”\n\nKhaos looked at D-Low’s photograph.\n\n“I’ll bring it to Reck. That ain’t agreement.”\n\n“Bring him something else.”\n\nCell’s voice lowered.\n\n“Kilo is talking.”\n\n---\n\nKilo sat inside a federal interview room wearing clean clothes and no restraints.\n\nKing K’s attorney had arranged the meeting through Captain Vance’s remaining contacts. In exchange for protection, Kilo agreed to describe the Ridge–Haven alliance as one organization controlled by Reck.\n\nThe statement was false.\n\nIt was also exactly what federal prosecutors needed for a conspiracy case.\n\nSpecial Agent Marcus Reed entered without introducing himself.\n\nHe was forty-two, tall and controlled, with dark brown skin, a close beard and gray beginning at his temples. He commanded the task force occupying Palm Haven.\n\nKilo expected threats.\n\nReed placed D-Low’s recovered recording on the table.\n\n“You helped send his car down the Paradise route.”\n\nKilo’s confidence disappeared.\n\n“I got immunity.”\n\n“From people who needed you to lie.”\n\n“I’m telling the truth.”\n\n“Then explain why your first statement says Khaos created the Haven Boyz after his release.”\n\nKilo said nothing.\n\nReed continued.\n\n“He created them before detention. You know that because you were there.”\n\nKilo looked toward the camera.\n\n“I want my lawyer.”\n\nReed gathered the file.\n\n“Your lawyer works for King K.”\n\n“So?”\n\n“King protects witnesses until their story stops helping him.”\n\nReed reached the door.\n\n“Decide whether you want protection from Reck or protection from the man arranging your testimony.”\n\n---\n\nThe ceasefire meeting took place inside an unfinished restaurant at the marina.\n\nReck and Khaos sat together without surrendering separate leadership.\n\nCell represented his network.\n\nKing K arrived with Mon.\n\nLa Madrina entered last and refused to sit.\n\nTide was absent.\n\nThat concerned Naz more than anything said inside the room.\n\nCell placed the problem plainly.\n\n“Nobody earning. Federal pressure stays because Saint wants the waterfront empty. We stop shooting for forty-eight hours, reopen routes and put business before revenge.”\n\nMon looked at Reck. “He still wearing King’s watch.”\n\nReck raised his wrist. “Come get it.”\n\nKing K smiled. “That attitude why everybody broke.”\n\nKhaos touched D-Low’s pendant. “Your setup why my father dead.”\n\nLa Madrina struck the table with one palm.\n\n“This is not reconciliation. It is arithmetic. Every hour the city remains closed, all of us lose money while Saint purchases land.”\n\nReck looked toward her.\n\n“And your shipment?”\n\n“Still owed.”\n\n“Then say this meeting about your money.”\n\n“Everything is about money eventually.”\n\nNaz slid a document across the table.\n\nThe Two Crowns would honor forty-eight hours under three conditions:\n\nNo attacks on families.\n\nNo movement against Sunridge or Paradise Haven property.\n\nDreko’s transfer information provided immediately.\n\nKing K leaned back. “You negotiating for inmates now?”\n\nKhaos looked at him. “I negotiate for everybody beneath my halo.”\n\nCell signed first.\n\nLa Madrina signed second.\n\nKing K refused until Mon whispered something in his ear.\n\nThen he added his name.\n\nReck and Khaos signed on separate lines.\n\nTwo crowns.\n\nOne temporary peace.\n\nGunfire struck the restaurant before the ink dried.\n\nThe front windows collapsed.\n\nTide’s people attacked from the marina parking lot while everybody inside scattered behind unfinished walls.\n\nCell shouted into his phone.\n\nTide did not answer.\n\nMon fired through a broken window. La Madrina’s security surrounded her. Reck pulled Zay’s empty chair away from the open floor out of instinct before remembering Zay had refused to attend.\n\nKhaos saw Deuce beside a teal truck.\n\nThe ceasefire had lasted forty-three seconds.\n\nReck moved toward the rear exit with Khaos.\n\nKing K blocked them.\n\n“This your setup?” Reck asked.\n\n“If it was, you wouldn’t still be talking.”\n\nFor one impossible moment, old enemies fought toward the same exit.\n\nThe attack ended as quickly as it began.\n\nTide disappeared before police arrived.\n\nOne Canal Boy remained wounded in the parking lot.\n\nCell looked at his signature on the table.\n\n“He moved without me.”\n\nKhaos stared at him. “Or you need us believing that.”\n\nThe ceasefire was dead.\n\nBusiness remained closed.\n\nAnd Tide had declared himself independent from the man who built him.\n\n---\n\nDreko arrived at Gulf Rock State Prison beneath a black sky.\n\nThe intake officer removed his burning-halo pendant, photographed it and placed it inside a property envelope.\n\n“Haven Boy?” the officer asked.\n\nDreko said nothing.\n\nThe officer smiled. “King’s people waiting.”\n\nInside the unit, older men connected to King K controlled phones, food access and protection. A guard wearing Saint’s Renewal Foundation pin opened the wrong gate at the right time.\n\nSix men entered Dreko’s corridor.\n\nHe stood alone.\n\nBefore they reached him, prison lights died.\n\nReem’s voice came through the darkness from the neighboring tier.\n\n“Tell Saint the young crown arrived under old protection.”\n\nDoors slammed across the unit.\n\nWhen emergency power returned, Dreko was gone from the corridor.\n\nReem’s network had moved him into a maintenance wing Saint’s guards did not control.\n\nDreko picked up a hidden phone.\n\nKhaos answered.\n\n“You good?”\n\n“For tonight.”\n\n“We getting you out.”\n\n“Get Saint first,” Dreko said. “His people run the prison like another neighborhood.”\n\n---\n\nReem called Reck immediately afterward.\n\n“Dreko is alive because of me.”\n\n“Then I appreciate that.”\n\n“Appreciation doesn’t settle ownership.”\n\nReck looked at the deeds spread across Naz’s desk.\n\n“There it is.”\n\n“Crown Harbor belongs to my trust. Saint stole control while I was incarcerated. You recovered my property.”\n\n“We recovered our neighborhoods.”\n\n“With my money, my connection and my prison network.”\n\nReck lowered his voice.\n\n“With D-Low dead, Zay shot, Duke hunted and families sleeping behind federal checkpoints.”\n\n“Power costs.”\n\n“You keep charging everybody else.”\n\nReem became silent.\n\nReck continued.\n\n“You want the deeds? Come home and explain everything face-to-face.”\n\n“You know I can’t.”\n\n“Then stop trying to rule Palm Haven through a prison phone.”\n\nReck ended the call.\n\nZay stood in the doorway on his crutches.\n\n“You like telling powerful people no.”\n\n“Somebody got to.”\n\n“Just make sure you ain’t saying it because you want to become the only person nobody can tell no.”\n\nReck looked at him.\n\nZay continued.\n\n“You started feeding The Ridge because you loved it. Now every decision sound like control.”\n\n“We at war.”\n\n“War don’t decide who you are. It exposes it.”\n\nReck turned toward the neighborhood survival ledger.\n\nMoney was disappearing faster than Naz could replace it.\n\n“What you want me to do?”\n\n“Build something people can own without owing you.”\n\n---\n\nThe answer came from the property deeds.\n\nNaz created community-owned companies with residents, shopkeepers and family representatives holding shares. D-Low’s trucking business became the first investor. The recovered neighborhood money funded emergency purchases of buildings Saint’s shell companies were attempting to acquire.\n\nReck did not own the properties personally.\n\nKhaos did not either.\n\nThe neighborhoods did.\n\nWhen Saint’s relocation representatives arrived, residents placed copies of the new ownership documents in their hands.\n\n“We’re not selling,” Ms. Lena told them.\n\nSaint watched the rejection from his office.\n\nFor the first time, Reck had attacked his empire without gunfire.\n\nIt frightened him more than the convoy robbery.\n\n---\n\nSpecial Agent Marcus Reed entered D-Low’s trucking warehouse alone.\n\nDuke raised a weapon from the shadows.\n\nReed slowly placed his badge on the floor.\n\n“I came for the archive cases.”\n\nReck stepped forward. “Federal agents usually bring more agents.”\n\n“Federal commanders usually don’t meet fugitives in warehouses.”\n\nDuke remained behind him.\n\n“You here to arrest me?”\n\n“Eventually.”\n\nReed looked toward Reck.\n\n“Tonight I need Gabriel Saint’s original contracts.”\n\nNaz studied him. “Why?”\n\nReed removed an old photograph from his jacket.\n\nSaint stood beside a young woman and a ten-year-old boy.\n\nThe boy was Reed.\n\n“Gabriel Saint is my father.”\n\nNobody spoke.\n\nReck looked from the photograph to the federal commander.\n\n“Your last name Reed.”\n\n“My mother’s. Danielle Reed.”\n\nLa Madrina recognized it.\n\n“The port accountant.”\n\nReed faced her. “You knew her?”\n\n“She discovered Saint’s first missing development funds.”\n\n“She died in a car accident three days later.”\n\nReed placed a sealed report beside the photograph.\n\nThe crash investigator had received a payment from Saint’s foundation.\n\n“I joined federal law enforcement to build a case against him,” Reed said. “Every time I got close, evidence disappeared. Witnesses changed stories. Then your war produced more records in three weeks than I found in twenty years.”\n\nReck folded his arms. “So Crown Sweep was about your father?”\n\n“It was supposed to be. Saint fed my team false warrants and turned the operation against you.”\n\n“You still occupied our neighborhoods.”\n\n“And you still run criminal organizations.”\n\nThe room tightened.\n\nReed did not soften.\n\n“I am not your friend. I will prosecute what I can prove. But Saint killed my mother, corrupted my task force and built his career on stolen neighborhoods.”\n\nHe looked at the archive cases.\n\n“Give me copies, and I can freeze Crown Harbor before the relocation deadline.”\n\nNaz asked the question everyone needed answered.\n\n“What stops you from taking everything and arresting us?”\n\nReed glanced toward Duke.\n\n“Nothing except the fact that Saint controls the official evidence system. If I take originals, they disappear. If copies exist everywhere, he can’t bury all of them.”\n\nZay entered behind Reck.\n\n“That sound familiar.”\n\nReck remembered his own order to Naz.\n\nMake copies. Hide them everywhere.\n\nReed extended his hand toward the case.\n\n“We want the same man.”\n\nReck did not shake it.\n\n“Wanting the same enemy don’t make us partners.”\n\n“No,” Reed said. “It makes us useful to each other.”\n\nOutside, federal vehicles remained at every entrance to Sunridge.\n\nInside, Gabriel Saint’s son stood beside the most wanted people in Palm Haven asking for proof that could destroy his father.\n\nReck looked at Naz.\n\n“Give him copies.”\n\nReed accepted the drive.\n\nHis phone vibrated before he reached the door.\n\nHe checked the message.\n\nSaint had scheduled an emergency vote for the following morning.\n\nIf approved, Crown Harbor would receive permanent control of the waterfront properties—including The Sunshine, D-Low’s trucking company and most of Paradise Haven.\n\nReed showed them the screen.\n\n“You have twelve hours.”\n\nKhaos looked toward the Renewal Center.\n\n“To do what?”\n\nReck closed the final archive case.\n\n“Stop Saint from buying the city before breakfast.”"},{"number":18,"title":"Chapter Eighteen — The Palm Haven Heist","body":"The Gulf Empress entered Palm Haven before dawn.\n\nFrom the shoreline, it looked like a moving city.\n\nStacks of containers rose above the black water. Tugboats guided the ship toward Saint’s redevelopment docks while federal patrols watched from both sides of the channel.\n\nOfficially, the cargo carried steel, glass and construction equipment for the Crown Harbor waterfront.\n\nUnofficially, half Palm Haven’s criminal economy was hidden inside it.\n\nCell waited inside a harbor office.\n\nLa Madrina watched from a private yacht beyond the inspection line.\n\nCaptain Vance controlled the police boats.\n\nGabriel Saint stood behind the windows of the Renewal Center, seven miles away, following the ship on a live port feed.\n\nAt 4:17 a.m., container 294 exploded.\n\nThe blast tore through the middle deck.\n\nSteel doors ripped away. Cranes collapsed. Containers slid across the ship and crashed against one another before falling into the channel. One split open above the dock.\n\nWhite packages rained across concrete.\n\nPort workers ran.\n\nPolice boats turned toward the fire.\n\nThen the second explosion opened the cargo hold.\n\nPalm Haven saw everything through news helicopters.\n\nBy sunrise, kilos were floating against seawalls, scattered across the port and disappearing into trucks, fishing boats and the hands of anyone fast enough to reach them.\n\nThe Gulf Empress had broken open.\n\nThe White Tide had arrived.\n\n---\n\nReck watched the disaster from D-Low’s trucking warehouse.\n\nEvery phone in the building rang at once.\n\nStreet crews wanted to purchase loose cargo.\n\nFormer Ridge associates offered locations.\n\nResidents reported strangers hiding packages inside apartment courtyards.\n\nMari stared at the television.\n\n“That’s more money in the water than we made our whole lives.”\n\nKhaos looked toward the port. “Enough to rebuild Haven ten times.”\n\nZay sat beside the archive cases, still pale from the shooting.\n\n“That’s what whoever opened it wants you thinking.”\n\nTJ enlarged a video of the damaged ship.\n\nOne container remained attached to a hanging crane above the channel.\n\n**317.**\n\nNaz compared the number to Saint’s ledgers.\n\n“That’s not product.”\n\n“What is it?” Reck asked.\n\n“Original port records. Payments. Shipping manifests. Maybe the key to Saint’s bank vault.”\n\nThe crane cable snapped.\n\nContainer 317 fell into the water and vanished.\n\n---\n\nThe first White Tide shooting happened twenty-three minutes later.\n\nTwo small crews fought over packages washed beneath the Harbor View bridge. A passing bus was caught between them. By the time police arrived, three people were dead and six civilians wounded.\n\nThe second shooting happened outside a motel.\n\nThe third happened inside a grocery-store parking lot where somebody had hidden cargo behind a dumpster.\n\nBy noon, Palm Haven’s emergency rooms overflowed.\n\nPrices collapsed.\n\nRobberies multiplied.\n\nInexperienced sellers moved unfamiliar product into every neighborhood while King K, Canal Boyz and corrupt officers hunted the same packages.\n\nReck ordered Ridge members to clear loose cargo away from schools, parks and apartment buildings.\n\nKhaos gave Haven the same order.\n\n“Secure it,” Reck said. “Nobody sells anything until we know who opened that ship.”\n\nMari looked at him. “We sitting on money while everybody else getting rich.”\n\n“Everybody else dying over bait.”\n\nZay nodded.\n\n“That’s the difference between running streets and streets running you.”\n\n---\n\nThe Blue Table arrived in three armored vehicles.\n\nDon Esteban “El Almirante” Vélez entered the city beneath the protection of men La Madrina did not command. Mateo “El Contador” Salas carried a ledger documenting every missing dollar. Lucía “La Sombra” Ortega carried nothing visible and noticed everything.\n\nThey met La Madrina inside a closed oceanfront hotel.\n\nEl Almirante remained standing.\n\n“You were sent to control Florida.”\n\nLa Madrina faced him in a white suit stained with smoke from the port.\n\n“Saint sabotaged the ship.”\n\n“A politician opened Mar Azul cargo beneath your authority.”\n\n“With help inside our organization.”\n\nMateo placed a loss report on the table.\n\n“Hundreds of millions missing. Records gone. Street crews selling our property for fractions.”\n\nEl Almirante’s voice remained quiet.\n\n“You are removed from Florida command until this is resolved.”\n\nLa Madrina looked at the soldiers behind him.\n\nHalf had once answered directly to her.\n\nNone moved.\n\nLucía spoke.\n\n“Forty-eight hours. Return the shipment, Container 317 and whoever opened the Gulf Empress.”\n\n“Or?”\n\nEl Almirante looked through the window toward Palm Haven.\n\n“Nobody conducts business safely until Mar Azul is repaid.”\n\n---\n\nThe ultimatum reached every faction.\n\nCell blamed La Madrina.\n\nTide claimed part of the loose shipment as Harbor View property.\n\nKing K offered to trade recovered cargo for a new federal agreement.\n\nVance ordered officers to seize packages without documenting them.\n\nSmaller crews abandoned alliances and fought over fast money.\n\nThe Two Crowns received a separate demand.\n\nEl Almirante wanted every package secured in Sunridge and Paradise Haven, along with Saint’s archive cases.\n\nReck met him at the trucking warehouse with Khaos beside him.\n\n“The files ain’t yours,” Reck said.\n\n“The shipment is.”\n\n“Then go ask your people how it got opened.”\n\nThe room changed.\n\nEl Almirante’s guards stepped closer.\n\nRidge and Haven members did the same.\n\nKhaos touched the burning halo.\n\n“You want who stole from you? We want them too. But you threaten our neighborhoods, now you got another war instead of an answer.”\n\nEl Almirante studied both young leaders.\n\n“Courage is expensive when borrowed from somebody else’s supply.”\n\nReck looked at the cartel chief.\n\n“Then send us the bill after we find Container 317.”\n\nLucía hid a small smile.\n\nEl Almirante did not.\n\n---\n\nNaz found Container 317 through a toll-camera photograph.\n\nA salvage truck removed it from the channel before federal divers arrived. The truck belonged to a company controlled by Sebastián Vélez—El Almirante’s son.\n\nThe container traveled directly to First Palm National Bank.\n\nLa Madrina stared at the image.\n\n“Sebastián authorized security access on the ship.”\n\nLucía added another record.\n\nThe explosive device came from a Mar Azul security shipment signed out under Sebastián’s code.\n\nThe betrayal had not entered the cartel from Palm Haven.\n\nIt came from the Blue Table’s family.\n\nEl Almirante refused to believe it until Sebastián called.\n\nHis son appeared on a video feed from Saint’s bank vault.\n\n“You built Mar Azul for another century,” Sebastián said. “La Madrina was going to lose Florida to children. I saved it.”\n\n“You destroyed our ship.”\n\n“I destroyed her authority.”\n\nGabriel Saint stepped into frame behind him.\n\n“The emergency vote begins in two hours,” Saint said. “By noon, the waterfront belongs to Crown Harbor. By tonight, every organization responsible for the port crisis will be federal property.”\n\nEl Almirante’s face turned to stone.\n\n“My son is not yours.”\n\nSaint smiled. “Everybody belongs to the person holding their future.”\n\nThe call ended.\n\nThe Blue Table declared war on Saint.\n\nLa Madrina declared war on Sebastián.\n\nAnd every road led to the same bank.\n\n---\n\nFirst Palm National occupied an entire block beside Government Square.\n\nBeneath it sat a private vault connected to the Renewal Center through an underground service tunnel. Saint had moved the community escrow money, police-seizure cash, cartel profits, waterfront deeds and original smuggling records into that vault before ordering the ship sabotaged.\n\nHe wanted every rival to attack the bank.\n\nThe city would see criminals destroying downtown.\n\nThe council would approve his emergency takeover.\n\nHis hidden tactical team would eliminate whoever survived.\n\nThen insurance would replace the money Saint had already moved somewhere else.\n\nMarcus Reed discovered the final part too late.\n\n“The bank is a kill box,” he told Reck through a secure call. “Don’t enter.”\n\nReck watched Saint’s emergency vote beginning on television.\n\nCouncil members entered the Renewal Center across the square. Residents from Sunridge and Paradise Haven remained trapped behind federal checkpoints, unable to speak before the vote.\n\n“If we don’t enter, Saint gets the deeds,” Reck said.\n\n“If you do, he gets bodies and a federal case.”\n\nKhaos looked at D-Low’s trucking company on the seizure map.\n\n“He gets everything either way.”\n\nZay’s voice came from behind them.\n\n“Then don’t play for what he expects.”\n\nNaz opened the bank schematics contained in Saint’s archive.\n\n“Everybody else going for the cash.”\n\nReck understood.\n\n“We go for the records.”\n\n---\n\nThe Palm Haven heist began at 9:42 a.m.\n\nKing K and Mon entered through the parking garage disguised among a private security convoy.\n\nCell’s people approached from the port tunnel.\n\nTide and the Canal Boyz attacked the rear service entrance.\n\nVance’s corrupt officers entered through the front carrying legitimate badges.\n\nThe Blue Table surrounded the block.\n\nLa Madrina’s loyal faction separated from them and moved toward Sebastián.\n\nRidge and Haven arrived inside custom trucks carrying community-ownership documents and copies of Saint’s records.\n\nNews helicopters circled above Government Square.\n\nThe first shots came from Saint’s hidden tactical team.\n\nThey fired into Vance’s officers from an upper bank floor.\n\nVance believed Cell betrayed him and answered toward the tunnel.\n\nCell’s people blamed the Canal Boyz.\n\nTide used the confusion to push for the vault.\n\nWithin seconds, every alliance collapsed.\n\nThe bank became thunder.\n\nGlass covered the marble lobby. Civilians hid behind counters. Council members across the square dropped beneath tables as bullets struck Renewal Center windows.\n\nReck and Khaos entered through the side hall with Naz, Mari, T-Rell and Rambo.\n\nTheir first move was not toward the vault.\n\nIt was toward the civilians trapped inside the lobby.\n\nThey cleared a path through the records wing while Marcus Reed’s loyal federal agents opened an evacuation route from outside.\n\nRambo remained behind Khaos.\n\n“You get the container,” he said. “I keep the hall.”\n\n“We move together.”\n\n“You the crown. Act like it.”\n\nDeuce appeared at the far end wearing Baby Ant’s missing chain replacement and carrying D-Low’s original port file.\n\nKhaos started toward him.\n\nRambo saw the tactical shooter above them first.\n\nHe pushed Khaos aside.\n\nThe shot struck Rambo beneath the shoulder.\n\nHe fell against the vault corridor.\n\nKhaos caught him.\n\n“Stay with me.”\n\nRambo pressed D-Low’s pendant back against Khaos’s chest.\n\n“Get Deuce.”\n\nT-Rell dragged Rambo toward the evacuation route while Khaos followed the stolen file deeper into the bank.\n\n---\n\nMarcus Reed reached the council chamber with evidence against his father.\n\nAn agent from his own task force blocked the door.\n\n“Orders changed,” the agent said.\n\n“Whose orders?”\n\nThe answer came as a gunshot.\n\nReed fell against the marble wall.\n\nDetective Holloway fired from the stairwell, forcing the agent away. She reached Reed and pressed both hands against the wound.\n\nAcross the chamber, cameras continued broadcasting the emergency vote.\n\nSaint stepped over broken glass and demanded the council proceed.\n\n“The violence outside proves why Crown Harbor must act now.”\n\nHolloway dragged Reed toward the live cameras.\n\nHe raised the drive Reck had given him.\n\n“Your councilman built this crisis,” he said into the nearest microphone. “He sabotaged the Gulf Empress, corrupted the task force and arranged the attack happening outside.”\n\nSaint looked at his son without emotion.\n\n“You always blamed me for your mother.”\n\nReed’s blood spread across the floor.\n\n“Because you killed her.”\n\nThe entire city heard him.\n\n---\n\nReck reached the private vault as King K emerged from another corridor.\n\nMon stood beside him.\n\nFor the first time, there was no vehicle, crowd or Harbor View crash between Reck and the man who had hunted his family.\n\nMon raised his weapon.\n\n“D-Low died because he stepped in front of you,” he said. “Zay got shot doing the same. Everybody around you bleeds while you wear the crown.”\n\nReck looked at him.\n\n“And everybody around King dies so he can keep one.”\n\nThe confrontation exploded across the vault lobby.\n\nKing K moved toward his informant files while Reck and Mon fought between shattered security gates. Mon was larger, older and certain rage made him stronger.\n\nReck had learned rage needed direction.\n\nHe forced Mon away from the civilians sheltering behind the deposit counters and drove him toward the empty tunnel.\n\nBefore either could finish it, Tide crashed through the vault door with Canal Boyz behind him.\n\n“Everybody move!” Tide shouted. “The vault mine.”\n\nCell appeared behind him.\n\n“You don’t own anything I built.”\n\nTide turned his weapon toward his former supplier.\n\n“That’s why I’m taking it.”\n\nThe Canal Boyz split in two.\n\nSome remained with Cell.\n\nOthers stood behind Tide.\n\nTheir civil war began inside Saint’s vault.\n\nKing K escaped with part of his file.\n\nMon disappeared into smoke.\n\nReck reached the vault controls as Khaos arrived carrying D-Low’s records.\n\nBehind them, Container 317 sat open.\n\nIt contained drives, original manifests and a metal key bearing First Palm National’s crest.\n\nNaz used the key to open the inner vault.\n\nMoney rose from floor to ceiling.\n\nMore than either young leader had imagined.\n\nCommunity funds.\n\nCartel profits.\n\nPolice theft.\n\nPolitical bribes.\n\nReem’s hidden fortune.\n\nKhaos stared at it.\n\n“We can rebuild everything.”\n\nZay’s voice came through TJ’s phone.\n\n“If you take all the money and bury the proof, you become another Saint.”\n\nReck looked toward the archive drives.\n\nHe made the decision.\n\n“Community escrow comes with us. Enough dirty money to keep both neighborhoods alive.”\n\nKhaos nodded slowly.\n\n“And the rest?”\n\n“Expose it.”\n\nNaz transmitted the contents of Container 317 to news stations, independent investigators, federal oversight offices and thousands of Palm Haven residents.\n\nSmuggling manifests filled television screens while the bank was still under siege.\n\nNames appeared.\n\nJudges.\n\nOfficers.\n\nDevelopers.\n\nBank executives.\n\nCartel intermediaries.\n\nPoliticians in cities beyond Florida.\n\nPalm Haven was not the first community the network had destroyed and purchased.\n\nIt was only the most profitable.\n\n---\n\nLucía found Sebastián inside the bank’s underground tunnel.\n\nLa Madrina stood beside her.\n\nEl Almirante approached from the opposite end.\n\nSebastián had nowhere left to move.\n\n“I did this for Mar Azul,” he told his father.\n\n“You did it for my chair,” El Almirante replied.\n\nSebastián looked toward La Madrina. “She was losing Florida.”\n\n“And you handed it to Saint.”\n\nLucía displayed the sabotage authorization.\n\nThe Blue Table saw the truth together.\n\nEl Almirante removed Sebastián from succession in the tunnel beneath the city he tried to purchase with betrayal.\n\nBut before cartel guards could take him, the lights died.\n\nSaint’s emergency escape system opened a hidden wall.\n\nSebastián disappeared through it.\n\nLa Madrina followed.\n\nShe reached an underground platform as a private vehicle accelerated away.\n\nGabriel Saint sat in the rear.\n\nSebastián was beside him.\n\nA third passenger remained hidden in shadow.\n\n---\n\nThe bank burned by afternoon.\n\nRambo entered surgery.\n\nMarcus Reed remained alive but critical.\n\nCaptain Vance’s corruption played across every news station.\n\nThe emergency waterfront vote collapsed before it could finish.\n\nFederal authorities froze Crown Harbor’s property transfers.\n\nResidents carried community deeds out of the bank while cameras watched.\n\nThe Two Crowns escaped with their neighborhood funds, D-Low’s records and enough money to survive the occupation.\n\nThey did not leave untouched.\n\nFour Ridge–Haven members were arrested.\n\nSeveral Canal Boyz and corrupt officers died during the crisis.\n\nDowntown Palm Haven became a federal crime scene.\n\nSaint disappeared.\n\nKing K and Mon remained free.\n\nTide survived and broke fully from Cell.\n\nThe Blue Table occupied the port.\n\nAnd the entire national smuggling network now knew Reck and Khaos had released its secrets.\n\nTJ recovered surveillance footage from the tunnel.\n\nThe private vehicle carrying Saint and Sebastián passed beneath one working camera.\n\nFor two seconds, light crossed the third passenger’s face.\n\nReck stopped the video.\n\nKhaos leaned closer.\n\nLa Madrina said nothing.\n\nThe man leaving with Gabriel Saint was Kareem Dawson Sr.\n\nReck’s father was supposed to be inside Blackwater State Prison.\n\nInstead, Reem sat beside Palm Haven’s most wanted politician while the city burned behind them.\n\nThe prison calls.\n\nThe waterfront deeds.\n\nLa Madrina’s arrival.\n\nThe hidden trust.\n\nThe war that made Reck powerful enough to open Saint’s vault.\n\nEvery answer created a worse question.\n\nKhaos looked at his cousin.\n\n“Your father planned this with Saint?”\n\nReck stared at the frozen image.\n\nReem had said he built his son into revenge.\n\nMaybe revenge had never been the destination.\n\nMaybe Reck had been the key.\n\nOutside, smoke from the Gulf Empress mixed with smoke from First Palm National above Palm Haven’s skyline.\n\nThe biggest shipment in the city’s history had flooded the streets.\n\nThe biggest bank heist had exposed a national empire.\n\nThe deadliest shooting crisis had stopped Saint’s vote.\n\nBut the two men who understood the entire plan were gone.\n\nReck fastened the cracked crown.\n\n“Find Reem.”"},{"number":19,"title":"Chapter Nineteen — Find Reem","body":"By nightfall, Kareem Dawson Sr. had replaced his son as Palm Haven’s most wanted man.\n\nFederal bulletins described Reem as the architect of the Crown Harbor conspiracy, the organizer of the First Palm National heist and the fugitive leader of a multigenerational criminal network.\n\nHis prison photograph appeared beside Gabriel Saint and Sebastián Vélez on every news station.\n\nReck watched from Rambo’s hospital waiting room.\n\nThe report called Reem armed, dangerous and responsible for the violence consuming Palm Haven.\n\nKhaos stood beside the intensive-care doors wearing Rambo’s blood across his burnt-orange shirt.\n\n“They got footage of him leaving with Saint,” Khaos said.\n\n“Footage don’t tell me why.”\n\n“It tells you he ain’t in prison.”\n\nReck turned from the television.\n\n“Your father died saving me. Mine might be risking everything to save my mama and little brother. I can’t call him a traitor until I hear it from him.”\n\nKhaos looked through the glass at Rambo.\n\nMachines surrounded the Haven enforcer. Doctors had removed the bullet, but internal damage kept him unconscious.\n\n“Then we find Reem,” Khaos said. “But if he chose Saint over us, you got to be Reck before you be his son.”\n\nReck nodded once.\n\nThe intensive-care light remained red.\n\nTwo fathers had left two sons different kinds of pain.\n\nNeither cousin had time to carry it slowly.\n\n---\n\nDuke became the first official Ridge captain inside D-Low’s trucking warehouse.\n\nNo celebration followed.\n\nHe remained a wanted fugitive, one wrist scarred from the broken handcuff and his face displayed on highway signs throughout the county.\n\nThe founding Ridge Boyz stood around him.\n\nZay attended on crutches.\n\nNaz held the cracked-crown pendant.\n\nTJ monitored federal radio traffic.\n\nMari kept watch near the door.\n\nReck placed the chain around Duke’s neck.\n\n“You stood for us inside. You saved an officer during that transport even when Saint sent people to kill you. You carried proof out when you could’ve carried nothing but yourself.”\n\nDuke touched the crown.\n\n“I ain’t looking for a title.”\n\n“That’s why you get one.”\n\nReck faced the founders.\n\n“Darius ‘Duke’ Coleman—first captain of the Ridge Boyz.”\n\nZay offered his hand first.\n\nThe others followed.\n\nDuke accepted the position with one condition.\n\n“We bring Dreko home too.”\n\nKhaos extended his hand from the Haven side.\n\n“Both sides of the walls.”\n\nThey shook.\n\n---\n\nThe White Tide rebuilt the street economy faster than anyone could control it.\n\nTide seized three missing containers and turned the Gator Hole into a protected distribution point. With Cell’s old routes and Sebastián’s surviving port contacts, the Canal Boyz earned more in forty-eight hours than they had during their entire existence.\n\nKing K and Mon recovered loose cargo through smaller crews desperate for protection.\n\nMoney returned to Palm Haven.\n\nSo did bodies.\n\nCorners that had remained quiet during federal occupation became battlegrounds. Robberies followed every sale. Hospitals treated overdoses from packages damaged by seawater and mixed by inexperienced sellers.\n\nCell watched his empire break into younger pieces.\n\nHe had supplied Tide.\n\nNow Tide no longer answered.\n\nHe had built Reck.\n\nReck had rejected him.\n\nHe called Naz with an offer.\n\n“I know where Saint took Reem.”\n\nNaz placed the call on speaker.\n\nReck leaned toward the phone.\n\n“Say it.”\n\n“Private island south of the channel. Saint used it for meetings nobody could photograph.”\n\n“What you want?”\n\n“Protection from the Blue Table.”\n\nKhaos laughed. “The man who created the Canal Boyz scared of his own business now.”\n\nCell ignored him.\n\n“La Madrina wants me alive long enough to explain the Gulf Empress. El Almirante wants somebody to pay for his son.”\n\n“Did you help Sebastián?” Reck asked.\n\nSilence.\n\n“Wrong answer,” Reck said.\n\n“I gave him port access. I didn’t know he planned to open the ship.”\n\nNaz wrote the admission down.\n\nCell continued.\n\n“Saint’s island is called Pelican Key. Cameras, private dock, underground bunker. He’ll destroy whatever remains before federal teams reach it.”\n\n“Send the location.”\n\n“Protection first.”\n\nReck looked at Duke.\n\n“You get one safe conversation with La Madrina. After that, whatever you did decides what happens.”\n\nCell knew it was the only offer coming.\n\nThe location appeared on Naz’s screen.\n\n---\n\nMarcus Reed awakened with a tube in his chest and Holloway beside the bed.\n\nHis first question was not about Saint.\n\n“Did the vote pass?”\n\n“No.”\n\nReed closed his eyes with relief.\n\nHolloway showed him the Pelican Key coordinates.\n\n“Recognize it?”\n\n“My father took me there once when I was twelve.”\n\n“What’s on it?”\n\n“A house above. Bunker below. Private tunnel from the dock.”\n\nHe struggled to sit higher.\n\n“Danielle found records there before she died.”\n\n“Your mother?”\n\n“She copied something Saint never recovered. If he still has Shanice Dawson, it’s because he thinks Reem knows where Danielle hid it.”\n\nHolloway stared at him.\n\n“Shanice is alive?”\n\nReed looked toward the door.\n\n“My father sent me one message before the bank attack.”\n\nHe handed her a photograph.\n\nShanice and Kobe sat inside a white room beneath a coastal painting.\n\n“He told me to destroy the archive or they would disappear.”\n\nHolloway called Reck.\n\nFor the first time, he knew why Reem entered Saint’s vehicle.\n\n---\n\nAt Gulf Rock, Dreko started a rebellion without throwing the first punch.\n\nHe copied names.\n\nGuards paid by Saint.\n\nInmates moved for King K.\n\nMedical transfers used to hide witnesses.\n\nConfiscated phone calls sold back to criminal organizations.\n\nReem’s network carried the list between units.\n\nWhen a corrupt guard attempted to move Dreko into an isolated corridor, incarcerated Ridge and Haven members refused to return to their cells. Other inmates joined after learning the same guards had stolen family money and sold protection.\n\nThe prison locked down.\n\nNo doors opened.\n\nNo meals moved.\n\nNo transport left Gulf Rock without every camera recording.\n\nDreko called it standing together.\n\nNews stations called it an uprising.\n\nSaint’s remaining allies ordered tactical teams inside.\n\nHolloway released Administrator Graves’s recording before they could enter.\n\nThe public heard prison officials discussing Saint, Cell and King K.\n\nState authorities suspended the assault.\n\nDreko remained incarcerated.\n\nBut he had survived long enough to turn one prison against Saint’s system.\n\n---\n\nLa Madrina met the Blue Table on the deck of the damaged Gulf Empress.\n\nEl Almirante looked older than he had the previous morning.\n\nHis son had betrayed him publicly.\n\nHis shipment filled enemy streets.\n\nHis organization had divided into factions beneath him.\n\nLucía placed Sebastián’s communications on the table.\n\nOne message identified Pelican Key.\n\nAnother ordered Mar Azul soldiers loyal to him to kill La Madrina before she reached it.\n\nEl Almirante read both.\n\n“Bring him back alive.”\n\nLa Madrina looked toward the island beyond the horizon.\n\n“If he resists?”\n\nEl Almirante’s face revealed nothing.\n\n“He already did.”\n\nMateo objected. “We still have missing cargo throughout the city.”\n\n“Cargo can be replaced,” Lucía said. “Authority cannot.”\n\nLa Madrina regained temporary command of the Florida operation.\n\nHer first order was not against Reck.\n\nIt was against Sebastián.\n\n---\n\nReck returned to Sunridge before leaving for the island.\n\nResidents surrounded Crown Harbor representatives attempting to post new seizure notices. Ms. Lena stood on the steps of The Sunshine holding her community-ownership certificate.\n\n“We’re still here,” she told them.\n\nThe representatives left under federal escort.\n\nZay watched Reck study the neighborhood.\n\n“You thinking about your father or your crown?”\n\n“My family.”\n\n“Good answer.”\n\n“You don’t believe me?”\n\nZay adjusted his crutch.\n\n“I believe you don’t know the difference anymore.”\n\nReck looked toward children playing beside the damaged car wash.\n\n“If I get Shanice and Kobe back, Saint still got to answer.”\n\n“He does. Just don’t sacrifice everybody trying to make the answer look like revenge.”\n\nReck handed Zay King K’s watch.\n\n“Hold this.”\n\nZay frowned. “Why?”\n\n“Because I’m going as Reem’s son. Not King K’s replacement.”\n\nZay accepted the watch.\n\nReck left wearing only the cracked crown.\n\n---\n\nPelican Key appeared beyond the morning fog.\n\nReck and Khaos approached with Duke, Mari, Naz, TJ, T-Rell and a small group representing both crowns. La Madrina’s faction traveled separately. Lucía accompanied the Blue Table team. Holloway and Reed’s loyal agents waited beyond the island’s private waters for evidence sufficient to enter.\n\nNobody trusted anybody completely.\n\nEverybody wanted somebody different.\n\nReck wanted his family.\n\nKhaos wanted the truth about Reem.\n\nLa Madrina wanted Sebastián.\n\nLucía wanted Container 317’s missing master drive.\n\nHolloway wanted Saint alive.\n\nDuke wanted the transfer orders connecting Saint to Gulf Rock.\n\nThe first explosion destroyed the island dock behind them.\n\nSebastián’s soldiers opened fire from the tree line.\n\nThe group scattered through tropical gardens surrounding Saint’s white mansion. Windows shattered above them. Security vehicles crossed the courtyard while alarms sealed the underground bunker.\n\nMari and Duke moved toward the generator house.\n\nNaz and TJ followed the surveillance lines.\n\nKhaos remained beside Reck.\n\n“Two crowns.”\n\n“Family beside family.”\n\nThey entered the mansion together.\n\n---\n\nShanice heard the shooting from below the house.\n\nKobe sat beside her inside the bunker room, frightened but unharmed. Saint had kept them alive because Reem refused to disclose the location of Danielle Reed’s missing account book.\n\nReem stood on the opposite side of the locked glass.\n\nHis prison uniform was gone. He wore black clothing supplied by Saint and a tracking cuff around his ankle.\n\nSaint entered holding the master drive from Container 317.\n\nSebastián followed.\n\n“Your son is on the island,” Saint said.\n\nReem looked toward the ceiling as another explosion shook dust loose.\n\n“Then you already lost it.”\n\n“He opened my bank because you made him believe the city was his inheritance.”\n\n“You opened your bank because you thought everybody had a price.”\n\nSaint placed the drive on a steel table.\n\n“Where did Danielle hide the original ledger?”\n\n“Ask your son.”\n\n“Marcus was a child.”\n\n“Children see what powerful men ignore.”\n\nSebastián raised his weapon toward Shanice.\n\nReem moved instantly.\n\nThe tracking cuff shocked him before he reached the glass.\n\nHe fell to one knee.\n\nSaint watched without sympathy.\n\n“Tell me, or Reck watches his family disappear because his father chose secrets again.”\n\nReem lifted his head.\n\n“I got in your car to save them.”\n\nSaint leaned closer.\n\n“You got in because part of you still believes we can finish what we started.”\n\nReem said nothing.\n\nThe silence frightened Shanice more than Saint’s threat.\n\n---\n\nReck reached the bunker corridor through the mansion’s collapsed kitchen.\n\nKhaos covered the rear while Duke forced the first security door. La Madrina entered from the opposite side with Lucía.\n\nSebastián appeared above the lower stairwell.\n\n“A street crew and a cartel fighting over a politician’s basement,” he said. “This is what my father was trying to prevent.”\n\nLa Madrina looked at him.\n\n“Your father was trying to prevent you from becoming exactly this.”\n\nSebastián fired and disappeared behind the blast door.\n\nLucía followed him.\n\nReck continued downward.\n\nHe found the white room first.\n\nShanice struck the glass when she saw him.\n\n“Kareem!”\n\nKobe ran toward the door.\n\nReck crossed the room and opened it with Saint’s security card recovered upstairs. His mother held him with both arms while Kobe pressed against his side.\n\nFor several seconds, the war disappeared.\n\nThen Reck looked through the second glass wall.\n\nReem stood inside the control room.\n\nFather and son saw each other face-to-face for the first time in years.\n\nReck entered slowly.\n\nReem looked older than the prison calls had shown.\n\n“You came.”\n\n“You left with Saint.”\n\n“He had your mother and brother.”\n\n“You could’ve told me.”\n\n“He monitored every call.”\n\n“You manipulated every call before he took them.”\n\nReem lowered his eyes.\n\n“That part is true.”\n\nKhaos entered behind Reck.\n\n“Did you build the war with Saint?”\n\nReem looked at D-Low’s pendant around his nephew’s neck.\n\n“We built the system together. Then Saint turned it against everybody.”\n\nReck stepped closer.\n\n“Did you use me to take it back?”\n\nReem did not answer quickly enough.\n\nReck felt the truth before hearing it.\n\n“I gave you the chance to become strong enough that nobody could erase our family again,” Reem said.\n\n“That ain’t what I asked.”\n\n“Yes. I used your war to weaken Saint, King K and Cell.”\n\nKhaos’s expression hardened.\n\nReem continued.\n\n“But I never ordered D-Low’s death. I never ordered Amaya taken. I never wanted Zay shot.”\n\n“You just built the road everybody used to reach us.”\n\nThe words struck Reem silent.\n\nAn alarm announced Saint’s escape tunnel opening beneath the bunker.\n\nReck looked toward it.\n\nThen back at his father.\n\nReem removed the tracking cuff with the released control key and placed it on the table.\n\n“We can argue when your family is off this island.”\n\nReck stared at him.\n\n“You coming with us?”\n\nReem looked toward the tunnel where Saint had disappeared.\n\n“I came here to finish him.”\n\nReck stepped between his father and the exit.\n\n“You came here to save them.”\n\nShanice stood in the doorway holding Kobe.\n\n“For once,” Reck said, “choose the family before the empire.”\n\nReem looked at his wife.\n\nThen his youngest son.\n\nThen the child he had turned into revenge.\n\nThe island shook as fire reached the generator room.\n\nReem made his choice.\n\nHe walked toward his family.\n\nBehind them, Gabriel Saint escaped into the final tunnel carrying Container 317’s master drive."},{"number":20,"title":"Chapter Twenty — Blood Before Empire","body":"Federal boats surrounded Pelican Key before Reck reached the beach.\n\nRed and blue lights covered the water. Helicopters circled above Saint’s burning mansion while loudspeakers ordered everyone on the island to surrender.\n\nReck stood between his family and the shoreline.\n\nReem looked toward the jungle path leading to the eastern cove.\n\n“There’s another boat.”\n\nShanice stared at him. “No more running.”\n\n“Federal agents think I helped Saint rob a bank and escape prison.”\n\n“Did you?”\n\nThe question stopped him.\n\nReck watched his parents face each other beneath smoke from a criminal empire they had both hidden from their children.\n\nReem answered carefully.\n\n“I escaped because Saint’s people controlled the transfer. I entered his vehicle because he had you and Kobe.”\n\n“That wasn’t all I asked.”\n\nReem looked away.\n\nMarcus Reed’s loyal agents landed at the ruined dock with Detective Holloway. Reed himself remained hospitalized, but his order placed Shanice and Kobe under federal-witness protection.\n\nHolloway approached with her weapon lowered.\n\n“Reem, I need your hands where I can see them.”\n\nReck stepped forward.\n\n“He tells you where Saint went first.”\n\n“That isn’t how surrender works.”\n\n“He ain’t surrendering until my family leaves this island.”\n\nHolloway looked toward Shanice.\n\nShanice took Kobe’s hand.\n\n“We’re leaving. Then Kareem answers for Kareem.”\n\nReem heard the finality in her voice.\n\nHe raised his hands.\n\n“Saint’s tunnel exits beneath a boathouse on the north reef. He’ll transfer to a private aircraft beyond federal water.”\n\nHolloway relayed the location.\n\nAgents rushed toward the north side.\n\nToo late.\n\nAn aircraft lifted beyond the horizon before they reached the boathouse.\n\nSaint still had the master drive.\n\nReem allowed Holloway to place the handcuffs around his wrists.\n\nReck looked at him.\n\n“You choosing prison again?”\n\n“I’m choosing not to make you fight federal agents for my mistakes.”\n\nIt was the first decision Reem made without turning his son into the weapon.\n\n---\n\nSunridge came under attack while Reck was trapped on the island.\n\nKing K and Mon knew the founding leader was gone. They also knew federal pressure had thinned Ridge protection around The Sunshine.\n\nThree vehicles entered Garden Courts before sunrise.\n\nMon’s people struck the community office holding Crown Harbor deeds. King K’s remaining crew hit the trucking-payment location. A third group attempted to burn the temporary housing ledger so residents could not prove community ownership.\n\nDuke received the warning first.\n\nHe stood inside The Sunshine wearing the captain’s cracked crown and the same clothes he escaped the swamp in.\n\n“Founders out,” Mari said. “Reck unreachable.”\n\nDuke looked toward the residents gathering in the courtyard.\n\n“Then I lead.”\n\nHe divided the response without pretending to be Reck.\n\nMari protected the deed office.\n\nTJ secured the digital records.\n\nNaz moved families away from the attack.\n\nDuke held the center with Ridge soldiers and trusted residents who refused to surrender their property.\n\nZay arrived on crutches.\n\n“You ain’t fighting,” Duke told him.\n\n“I’m organizing people.”\n\n“Same thing today.”\n\nZay stood in front of the community office with Ms. Lena and dozens of property owners. Phones streamed every moment.\n\nWhen King K’s group attempted to claim the building as abandoned, residents held up their ownership certificates.\n\n“This ain’t gang property,” Ms. Lena shouted. “This belongs to us.”\n\nMon’s shooters opened fire from the far end of the block.\n\nDuke moved residents behind concrete walls before Ridge members answered. The street became chaos, but the documents remained protected.\n\nMari chased Mon’s group away from the office.\n\nNaz caught one attacker attempting to burn the paper ledger.\n\nTJ saved every digital copy before the power failed.\n\nDuke reached the center courtyard as King K’s final vehicle withdrew.\n\nHe could have pursued it.\n\nInstead, he stayed with the neighborhood.\n\nWhen Reck called from Pelican Key, Duke answered with smoke behind him.\n\n“The Ridge still ours.”\n\nReck looked at his new captain through the screen.\n\n“Anybody hurt?”\n\n“Some wounded. Everybody alive.”\n\n“King?”\n\n“Ran.”\n\n“Why you ain’t chase?”\n\nDuke looked toward Zay.\n\n“Crown protects what’s behind it before it chases what’s in front.”\n\nReck understood he had chosen the right captain.\n\n---\n\nRambo awakened that afternoon.\n\nKhaos sat beside the bed wearing D-Low’s pendant and Baby Ant’s recovered chain wrapped around one hand.\n\nRambo tried moving his injured arm.\n\nNothing happened.\n\nHis face changed.\n\n“Doctor says nerves damaged,” Khaos told him. “They don’t know how much coming back.”\n\nRambo stared at the ceiling.\n\n“I can’t be your enforcer with one arm.”\n\n“You pushed me out that shot.”\n\n“That ain’t a position.”\n\nKhaos leaned closer.\n\n“You think Haven only needs people who can shoot and fight? That thinking got half our members dead or locked.”\n\nRambo looked at him.\n\n“Then what I am?”\n\n“Alive. Loyal. And still one of mine.”\n\nKhaos placed Baby Ant’s pendant on the bedside table.\n\n“When you heal, you train the young ones to survive without becoming Deuce.”\n\nFor the first time since waking, Rambo’s expression softened.\n\n“You really changed in jail.”\n\n“Nah.” Khaos sat back. “I changed after I got out.”\n\n---\n\nKilo and Deuce were captured on different sides of Palm Haven.\n\nKilo remained in federal protection after his false statement collapsed.\n\nDeuce was found hiding inside a White Tide stash location abandoned by Tide. T-Rell brought him to the burned foundation of D-Low’s garage.\n\nKhaos stood with the surviving Haven Boyz.\n\nReck attended after returning from Pelican Key.\n\nDeuce’s confidence disappeared when he saw Baby Ant’s chain beside D-Low’s pendant.\n\n“Tide made me do it,” he said.\n\nKhaos looked at him. “Tide made you wear our dead friend’s jewelry?”\n\n“I was surviving.”\n\n“T-Rell survived without switching. Rambo survived getting shot for me. Lil Smoke survived the Gator Hole without talking.”\n\nKhaos stepped closer.\n\n“You kept using survival to explain every choice that paid you.”\n\nThe Haven Boyz wanted revenge.\n\nKhaos wanted it too.\n\nBut D-Low’s final recording had warned him not to inherit his father’s mistakes.\n\nHe looked toward Reck.\n\n“Both crowns decide betrayal.”\n\nReck answered. “Truth first.”\n\nDeuce was forced to disclose every Canal location, White Tide storage site and payment he received from Sebastián’s network. The information was recorded and copied.\n\nAfterward, Khaos removed every Haven symbol from him.\n\n“You never wear our dead again. You never enter Paradise Haven. You spend the rest of your life knowing you traded family for people who abandoned you.”\n\nDeuce looked surprised.\n\n“That’s it?”\n\nKhaos’s eyes hardened.\n\n“Living without the name you sold is the first consequence. What law does with everything you confessed comes next.”\n\nReck saw D-Low’s son choose something harder than rage.\n\nHe chose to become different.\n\n---\n\nAt Gulf Rock, Dreko’s evidence forced the state to remove six guards and suspend the warden.\n\nThe prison remained locked down, but outside investigators entered with cameras Saint’s people could not disable.\n\nDreko sat across from a state inspector.\n\n“You expect protection for talking?” she asked.\n\n“I expect the same rules for guards as inmates.”\n\n“That isn’t how prison works.”\n\n“That’s why your prison almost exploded.”\n\nHe provided dates, transfers and payment names without describing Ridge or Haven operations.\n\nHe exposed corruption without surrendering his people.\n\nThe distinction mattered to him.\n\nIt also gave Holloway the first legal path to challenge Duke’s sabotaged transfer.\n\n---\n\nLa Madrina and Lucía found Sebastián at a private airfield north of Palm Haven.\n\nSaint’s aircraft had dropped him there before continuing west.\n\nSebastián stood beside two Mar Azul pilots and a case containing duplicate access codes from Container 317.\n\nEl Almirante joined through a secure video call.\n\nHis son faced him across the screen.\n\n“Saint escaped because you trusted an outsider over blood,” El Almirante said.\n\nSebastián looked toward La Madrina. “You trusted her over me first.”\n\n“She earned authority.”\n\n“I was born to it.”\n\nLucía closed the hangar doors.\n\n“That is the difference.”\n\nSebastián tried bargaining with the network names contained in the master drive. La Madrina refused.\n\n“You opened our ship, flooded a city and delivered our records to Saint. Information does not erase betrayal.”\n\nEl Almirante removed his son from Mar Azul permanently and ordered Lucía to take him into cartel custody.\n\nBefore the feed ended, Sebastián gave La Madrina one final warning.\n\n“Saint isn’t running to hide. He is meeting the man who financed everything.”\n\n“Who?”\n\nSebastián smiled.\n\n“Ask Shanice Dawson.”\n\n---\n\nShanice refused federal protection until she spoke to her children.\n\nShe gathered Reck, Amaya and Kobe inside the same Sunridge apartment where the family had once worried about grocery money while Reem controlled fortunes from prison.\n\nReem attended through a secured interview-room screen, wrists still cuffed.\n\nShanice placed an old accounting ledger on the table.\n\nMarcus Reed joined from his hospital room.\n\nHe recognized the handwriting.\n\n“My mother.”\n\nShanice nodded.\n\n“Danielle gave it to me three days before she died.”\n\nReck stared at his mother.\n\n“You knew all this time?”\n\n“I knew Saint’s money touched the port, police and your father’s businesses. I didn’t know how far it reached until years later.”\n\n“Why didn’t you tell me?”\n\n“Because I was trying to give you a life where none of it mattered.”\n\nReem looked through the screen.\n\n“You told me the ledger was destroyed.”\n\n“Because you would’ve used it for revenge.”\n\nShanice opened another folder.\n\nInside were business registrations, insurance policies and original property certificates.\n\n“While your father built prison alliances, I kept the legitimate companies alive. The trucking operation. The Sunshine land. Three Garden Courts buildings. I moved them into trusts for you, Amaya and Kobe before Saint could find them.”\n\nReck felt another version of his childhood rearrange itself.\n\n“You controlled the businesses?”\n\n“I protected them.”\n\n“You shaped this too.”\n\nShanice met his anger without looking away.\n\n“Your father wanted you powerful enough to inherit his empire. I wanted you protected enough to refuse it.”\n\nReem lowered his head.\n\nShanice turned Danielle’s ledger toward Marcus Reed.\n\nThe earliest payments did not begin with Saint.\n\nThey came from **Meridian Coastal Holdings**, a national development company controlling ports, banks and luxury waterfront projects throughout Florida.\n\nIts founder appeared on magazine covers, charity boards and political donor lists.\n\n**Augustus “Gus” Bellamy.**\n\nBellamy was seventy-three, worth billions and publicly celebrated for rebuilding struggling coastal cities.\n\nDanielle’s ledger showed something else.\n\nBellamy financed Saint’s first election.\n\nHe funded Reem’s original port organization through intermediaries.\n\nHe introduced Mar Azul money into First Palm National.\n\nHe profited every time street violence lowered property prices.\n\nPalm Haven was one project inside a national machine.\n\nMarcus Reed read the final page.\n\n“Grand jury convenes in six days.”\n\nNaz looked at him. “Bellamy knows?”\n\n“If Saint has the master drive, yes.”\n\nShanice closed the ledger.\n\n“Then he won’t spend six days running.”\n\n“What will he do?” Reck asked.\n\nReem answered through the screen.\n\n“Erase everybody who can connect him to Palm Haven.”\n\n---\n\nTide declared himself Palm Haven’s new supplier that night.\n\nHe stood at the Gator Hole surrounded by White Tide cargo and the Canal Boyz who chose him over Cell.\n\n“Cell built routes,” Tide said. “I own what moves through them now.”\n\nKing K and Mon joined him through a private call.\n\nThe old enemies reached a new agreement.\n\nTide supplied.\n\nKing distributed.\n\nMon enforced.\n\nTheir first objective was not Reck.\n\nIt was Duke.\n\nThe escaped captain carried proof capable of destroying Saint’s jail network and King K’s protection.\n\nIf Duke died, they could blame Bellamy’s cleanup operation.\n\nNobody would know which war killed him.\n\n---\n\nReck returned to The Sunshine after midnight.\n\nThe founding five, Duke, Khaos and the Haven leadership waited beneath the damaged roof.\n\nHe placed Danielle’s ledger beside the cracked crown and burning halo.\n\n“Everybody keeps telling us what we inherited,” Reck said. “My father left me a war. My mother left me a way out. D-Low left Khaos the truth. Saint left the whole city bleeding.”\n\nHe looked at every face.\n\n“Now we choose what survives. We can inherit their mistakes, or we can build something they never controlled.”\n\nKhaos touched his father’s pendant.\n\n“Bellamy first?”\n\nNaz shook his head.\n\n“Bellamy got billions, national connections and six days to erase evidence. He won’t come at us like King K.”\n\nZay leaned on his crutches.\n\n“Then we don’t fight him like King K.”\n\nReck looked toward downtown Palm Haven.\n\nThe street war remained active.\n\nTide controlled the White Tide.\n\nKing K and Mon were rebuilding.\n\nSaint held the master drive.\n\nBellamy controlled the money above them all.\n\nAnd a federal grand jury waited six days away.\n\nReck placed King K’s watch permanently inside the archive case.\n\nHe no longer needed to wear another man’s proof of power.\n\n“We protect Duke. We get Dreko home. We keep the neighborhoods owned by the people.”\n\nHe closed the case.\n\n“Then we take down the man who been buying every war.”"}];
